window.SUBJECTS=[{"id":"maths-h","name":"Maths (Higher)","group":"maths","level":"Higher","weeks":[{"t":"Surds, bounds & recurring decimals","intro":"These are Higher-only number topics that often appear as quick marks early in a paper. Surds keep answers exact, bounds tell you how accurate a measurement is, and recurring decimals can always be written as fractions.","how":["Simplify a surd by finding the biggest square number factor: √48 = √16 × √3 = 4√3.","Rationalise a denominator by multiplying top and bottom by the surd: 6/√3 = 6√3/3 = 2√3.","For bounds, halve the unit of accuracy: 8 cm to the nearest cm has bounds 7.5 cm and 8.5 cm.","Maximum of a division = upper bound ÷ lower bound. Minimum = lower bound ÷ upper bound.","For a recurring decimal, let x equal it, multiply by 10, 100 or 1000 to line up the repeats, then subtract."],"example":{"q":"Write 0.272727… as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["Let x = 0.272727…","Two digits repeat, so multiply by 100: 100x = 27.272727…","Subtract: 100x − x = 27, so 99x = 27.","x = 27/99 = 3/11."],"a":"0.27 recurring = 3/11"},"walk":[{"h":"Surd rules","b":"√a × √b = √(ab) and √a ÷ √b = √(a/b). You can only add surds when the number under the root matches: 2√5 + 3√5 = 5√5, but √2 + √3 cannot be simplified. Expand brackets like (2 + √3)(2 − √3) = 4 − 3 = 1."},{"h":"Upper and lower bounds","b":"A length of 6.4 m to 1 dp lies between 6.35 m and 6.45 m. Write it as an error interval: 6.35 ≤ L < 6.45. To make an answer as big as possible, add the biggest values or divide the biggest by the smallest."},{"h":"Recurring decimals","b":"Dots go over the first and last repeating digit. For 0.1666…, only the 6 repeats: 10x = 1.666…, 100x = 16.666…, so 90x = 15 and x = 15/90 = 1/6."}],"qs":[["Simplify √50.",["5√2","25√2","2√5","10√5"],"50 = 25 × 2, and √25 = 5, so √50 = 5√2."],["Simplify √12 + √27.",["5√3","√39","6√3","13√3"],"√12 = 2√3 and √27 = 3√3, so the total is 5√3."],["Rationalise 10/√5.",["2√5","5√2","10√5","√2"],"10/√5 × √5/√5 = 10√5/5 = 2√5."],["Expand (3 + √2)(3 − √2).",["7","9","11","9 − √2"],"This is a difference of two squares: 9 − 2 = 7."],["A length is 12 cm to the nearest cm. What is its lower bound?",["11.5 cm","11 cm","11.9 cm","11.95 cm"],"Half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so the lower bound is 12 − 0.5 = 11.5 cm."],["x = 3.7 to 1 dp. Which is the error interval?",["3.65 ≤ x < 3.75","3.6 ≤ x < 3.8","3.65 < x ≤ 3.75","3.7 ≤ x < 3.8"],"Half of 0.1 is 0.05, giving 3.65 ≤ x < 3.75."],["Distance 100 m (nearest 10 m), time 20 s (nearest s). Upper bound of speed?",["105 ÷ 19.5","105 ÷ 20.5","95 ÷ 19.5","100 ÷ 20"],"Largest speed = largest distance ÷ smallest time = 105 ÷ 19.5."],["Write 0.444… as a fraction.",["4/9","4/10","2/5","44/100"],"10x − x = 4, so 9x = 4 and x = 4/9."],["Write 0.151515… as a fraction in simplest form.",["5/33","15/100","3/20","15/90"],"100x − x = 15, so x = 15/99 = 5/33."],["Simplify (√3)² × 2.",["6","2√3","12","3√2"],"(√3)² = 3, and 3 × 2 = 6."]],"bonus":["Rationalise 1/(1 + √2).",["√2 − 1","1 − √2","1 + √2","(1 + √2)/3"],"Multiply by (1 − √2)/(1 − √2): the bottom becomes 1 − 2 = −1, giving (1 − √2)/(−1) = √2 − 1."]},{"t":"Quadratics & simultaneous equations","intro":"Quadratic equations come up in every Higher paper. You need three ways to solve them: factorising, the quadratic formula and completing the square. You also need to solve a pair of equations where one is quadratic.","how":["Rearrange so one side is 0, e.g. x² + 5x + 6 = 0.","Try factorising first: find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b.","If it won't factorise, use x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a and round as asked.","Complete the square: x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c. The turning point is (−b/2, −(b/2)² + c).","For linear + quadratic simultaneous equations, rearrange the linear one and substitute it into the quadratic."],"example":{"q":"Solve x² − 4x − 7 = 0, giving answers to 2 dp.","steps":["a = 1, b = −4, c = −7.","b² − 4ac = 16 + 28 = 44.","x = (4 ± √44) / 2.","√44 ≈ 6.633, so x = 5.32 or x = −1.32."],"a":"x = 5.32 or x = −1.32"},"walk":[{"h":"Factorising when a ≠ 1","b":"For 2x² + 7x + 3, multiply a × c = 6. Find numbers that multiply to 6 and add to 7: 6 and 1. Split the middle: 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3) = (2x + 1)(x + 3)."},{"h":"Completing the square","b":"x² + 6x + 2 = (x + 3)² − 9 + 2 = (x + 3)² − 7. The minimum point of y = x² + 6x + 2 is (−3, −7). To solve = 0, write (x + 3)² = 7, so x = −3 ± √7."},{"h":"Linear and quadratic together","b":"Solve y = x + 1 and x² + y² = 13. Substitute: x² + (x + 1)² = 13 → 2x² + 2x − 12 = 0 → x² + x − 6 = 0 → (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0. So x = 2, y = 3 or x = −3, y = −2. Always find both pairs."}],"qs":[["Solve x² + 5x + 6 = 0.",["x = −2 or x = −3","x = 2 or x = 3","x = −1 or x = −6","x = 1 or x = 6"],"(x + 2)(x + 3) = 0, so x = −2 or −3."],["Factorise x² − 49.",["(x + 7)(x − 7)","(x − 7)²","(x + 7)²","x(x − 49)"],"Difference of two squares: a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b)."],["Factorise 2x² + 7x + 3.",["(2x + 1)(x + 3)","(2x + 3)(x + 1)","(2x − 1)(x − 3)","(x + 7)(2x + 3)"],"Check: 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = 2x² + 7x + 3."],["Write x² + 8x + 5 as (x + p)² + q.",["(x + 4)² − 11","(x + 4)² + 5","(x + 8)² − 59","(x + 4)² − 21"],"(x + 4)² = x² + 8x + 16, so subtract 11 to get +5."],["What is the turning point of y = (x − 3)² + 2?",["(3, 2)","(−3, 2)","(3, −2)","(2, 3)"],"The bracket is 0 when x = 3, giving y = 2."],["In the quadratic formula, what is b² − 4ac for x² + 3x − 4 = 0?",["25","−7","7","13"],"9 − 4(1)(−4) = 9 + 16 = 25."],["Solve 3x + y = 11 and x − y = 1.",["x = 3, y = 2","x = 2, y = 5","x = 4, y = −1","x = 3, y = 1"],"Add the equations: 4x = 12, so x = 3 and y = 2."],["How many solutions does x² + 4 = 0 have (real numbers)?",["0","1","2","4"],"x² = −4 has no real solution because a square cannot be negative."],["Solve x² = 5x.",["x = 0 or x = 5","x = 5 only","x = √5","x = 0 only"],"x² − 5x = 0 → x(x − 5) = 0. Never divide by x, or you lose x = 0."],["Solve (x − 1)² = 9.",["x = 4 or x = −2","x = 10","x = 4 only","x = 3 or x = −3"],"x − 1 = ±3, so x = 4 or x = −2."]],"bonus":["Solve 2x² − 3x − 2 = 0.",["x = 2 or x = −½","x = −2 or x = ½","x = 1 or x = −2","x = 3 or x = −1"],"(2x + 1)(x − 2) = 0, so x = 2 or x = −0.5."]},{"t":"Trigonometry & circle theorems","intro":"Higher papers expect you to go beyond SOH CAH TOA. You need the sine rule, cosine rule and area formula for any triangle, exact trig values without a calculator, and the circle theorems with reasons.","how":["Right-angled triangle: use SOH CAH TOA or Pythagoras.","Two sides and the angle between them: use the cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, or area = ½ab sin C.","A side and its opposite angle known: use the sine rule a/sin A = b/sin B.","Learn exact values: sin 30 = ½, cos 60 = ½, tan 45 = 1, sin 60 = √3/2, cos 30 = √3/2, tan 30 = 1/√3.","For circle theorems, write the angle AND the theorem as your reason."],"example":{"q":"Triangle ABC has b = 7 cm, c = 5 cm and angle A = 60°. Find side a.","steps":["Use the cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A.","a² = 49 + 25 − 2 × 7 × 5 × cos 60.","cos 60 = 0.5, so a² = 74 − 35 = 39.","a = √39 ≈ 6.24 cm."],"a":"a ≈ 6.24 cm"},"walk":[{"h":"Which rule?","b":"Label sides with small letters opposite the matching capital-letter angles. If you have a matching pair (side a and angle A), use the sine rule. If not, you'll need the cosine rule. To find an angle with the cosine rule, use cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/2bc."},{"h":"Key circle theorems","b":"The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference. The angle in a semicircle is 90°. Angles in the same segment are equal. Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°. A tangent meets a radius at 90°."},{"h":"More theorems","b":"Two tangents from one point to a circle are equal in length. The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord. The alternate segment theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment."}],"qs":[["What is sin 30° exactly?",["½","√3/2","1","1/√2"],"sin 30° = 0.5."],["What is tan 45°?",["1","0","√3","½"],"In a right-angled isosceles triangle opposite = adjacent, so tan 45° = 1."],["Which formula gives the area of any triangle?",["½ab sin C","½bh cos C","ab sin C","a² + b² − 2ab cos C"],"Area = ½ × a × b × sin C, where C is the angle between sides a and b."],["You know two angles and one side. Which rule finds another side?",["Sine rule","Cosine rule","Pythagoras","Area formula"],"With a side and its opposite angle (find the third angle first), the sine rule works."],["The angle at the centre is 140°. What is the angle at the circumference on the same arc?",["70°","140°","280°","40°"],"The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference."],["One angle of a cyclic quadrilateral is 115°. What is the opposite angle?",["65°","115°","75°","245°"],"Opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°."],["What angle does a tangent make with the radius at the point of contact?",["90°","45°","180°","60°"],"A tangent is perpendicular to the radius."],["What is the angle in a semicircle?",["90°","180°","60°","45°"],"Any triangle drawn from the diameter to the circumference has a right angle there."],["Area of a triangle with sides 6 cm and 8 cm and an included angle of 30°?",["12 cm²","24 cm²","48 cm²","20.8 cm²"],"½ × 6 × 8 × sin 30 = 24 × 0.5 = 12 cm²."],["In a right-angled triangle, hypotenuse 10 cm and angle 30°. How long is the opposite side?",["5 cm","8.66 cm","10 cm","20 cm"],"opp = hyp × sin 30 = 10 × 0.5 = 5 cm."]],"bonus":["What is cos 30° exactly?",["√3/2","½","1/√3","√2/2"],"cos 30° = √3/2, which is about 0.866."]},{"t":"Statistics & probability","intro":"The statistics questions at the end of a Higher paper are often the most predictable marks. Practise reading histograms, cumulative frequency graphs and box plots, and use tree diagrams for probability.","how":["Histograms: frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. Area of each bar = frequency.","Cumulative frequency: plot the running total at the upper end of each class.","Read the median at half the total, and the quartiles at ¼ and ¾. IQR = upper quartile − lower quartile.","To compare distributions, compare an average (median) AND a spread (IQR or range) in context.","On tree diagrams, multiply along branches and add between branches. Without replacement, the second-pick fractions change."],"example":{"q":"A bag has 5 red and 3 blue counters. Two are taken without replacement. Find P(both red).","steps":["First pick: P(red) = 5/8.","One red has gone, so 4 red are left out of 7.","Second pick: P(red) = 4/7.","Multiply: 5/8 × 4/7 = 20/56 = 5/14."],"a":"P(both red) = 5/14"},"walk":[{"h":"Histograms","b":"A class 10 < x ≤ 30 with frequency 40 has width 20, so frequency density = 2. To find a frequency from the graph, multiply the bar's height by its width. Bars can have different widths, so never just read off the height."},{"h":"Box plots","b":"A box plot shows the minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile and maximum. When comparing, say for example: 'Class A had a higher median (62 vs 55), so on average did better. Class B had a smaller IQR, so their scores were more consistent.'"},{"h":"Conditional probability","b":"'Given that' means you only look at part of the outcomes. From a two-way table, P(girl given plays football) = girls who play ÷ everyone who plays. Venn diagrams work the same way."}],"qs":[["A class 0 < t ≤ 5 has frequency 20. What is the frequency density?",["4","100","20","0.25"],"20 ÷ 5 = 4."],["A histogram bar is 10 wide with frequency density 3. What is the frequency?",["30","13","3.3","7"],"Frequency = density × width = 3 × 10."],["Total frequency 80. Where do you read the median on a cumulative frequency graph?",["At 40","At 20","At 60","At 80"],"The median is half way: 80 ÷ 2 = 40."],["LQ = 24, UQ = 41. What is the IQR?",["17","65","32.5","24"],"IQR = 41 − 24 = 17."],["Which is the best comparison of two box plots?",["Compare medians and IQRs, in context","Compare only the maximums","Compare only the ranges","Say which box is bigger"],"Full marks need an average and a spread, linked to the context."],["P(rain) = 0.3. What is P(no rain)?",["0.7","0.3","1.3","0.03"],"Probabilities of all outcomes add to 1."],["Two fair coins are flipped. P(two heads)?",["¼","½","⅓","1"],"½ × ½ = ¼."],["A bag has 3 red and 2 green. Two picks with replacement. P(red then red)?",["9/25","6/20","3/10","6/25"],"3/5 × 3/5 = 9/25."],["Same bag, without replacement. P(red then red)?",["3/10","9/25","6/25","1/2"],"3/5 × 2/4 = 6/20 = 3/10."],["On a tree diagram, when do you add probabilities?",["To combine different routes that give the outcome you want","Along a branch","Never","For the first pick only"],"Multiply along a route, then add the routes."]],"bonus":["Of 30 students, 12 play football and 8 of those are girls. P(girl given they play football)?",["2/3","8/30","4/15","8/12 of 30"],"Only look at the 12 footballers: 8/12 = 2/3."]}]},{"id":"maths-f","name":"Maths (Foundation)","group":"maths","level":"Foundation","weeks":[{"t":"Fractions, decimals & percentages","intro":"Fractions, decimals and percentages turn up all over Foundation papers, in money, recipes and best-buy questions. Getting quick at converting and calculating with them earns easy marks.","how":["To change a fraction to a decimal, divide the top by the bottom: 3/8 = 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375.","To change a decimal to a percentage, multiply by 100: 0.375 = 37.5%.","To add or subtract fractions, make the denominators the same first.","To multiply fractions, multiply tops and multiply bottoms. To divide, flip the second fraction and multiply.","For a percentage increase, use a multiplier: increase by 15% → × 1.15. Decrease by 15% → × 0.85."],"example":{"q":"A coat costs £80. It is reduced by 25% in a sale. What is the sale price?","steps":["A 25% decrease leaves 75% of the price.","75% as a decimal is 0.75.","£80 × 0.75 = £60."],"a":"The sale price is £60."},"walk":[{"h":"Percentage change","b":"Percentage change = change ÷ original × 100. If a price goes from £40 to £50, the change is £10, so 10 ÷ 40 × 100 = 25% increase."},{"h":"Reverse percentages","b":"After a 20% rise a TV costs £360. That is 120% of the original, so 1% = 360 ÷ 120 = 3, and 100% = £300. Don't take 20% off £360."},{"h":"Simple and compound interest","b":"Simple interest is the same every year: 5% of £200 is £10 a year. Compound interest grows on itself: £200 × 1.05³ = £231.53 after 3 years."}],"qs":[["What is 3/4 as a percentage?",["75%","34%","0.75%","43%"],"3 ÷ 4 = 0.75, and 0.75 × 100 = 75%."],["Work out 2/5 + 1/10.",["1/2","3/15","3/10","2/50"],"2/5 = 4/10, and 4/10 + 1/10 = 5/10 = 1/2."],["Work out 2/3 × 3/4.",["1/2","5/7","6/7","8/9"],"(2 × 3)/(3 × 4) = 6/12 = 1/2."],["Work out 1/2 ÷ 1/4.",["2","1/8","1/2","4"],"Flip and multiply: 1/2 × 4/1 = 2."],["What is 15% of £60?",["£9","£15","£6","£4"],"10% is £6, 5% is £3, so 15% is £9."],["Which multiplier increases an amount by 8%?",["1.08","0.08","1.8","0.92"],"100% + 8% = 108% = 1.08."],["A price rises from £50 to £60. What is the percentage increase?",["20%","10%","16.7%","60%"],"10 ÷ 50 × 100 = 20%."],["After a 10% discount a game costs £27. What was the original price?",["£30","£29.70","£24.30","£37"],"£27 is 90%, so 10% = £3 and 100% = £30."],["Write 0.6 as a fraction in simplest form.",["3/5","6/100","1/6","60/10"],"0.6 = 6/10 = 3/5."],["£500 earns 2% compound interest for 2 years. How much is there?",["£520.20","£520","£510","£504"],"500 × 1.02² = 520.20."]],"bonus":["Which is biggest: 0.7, 2/3 or 68%?",["0.7","2/3","68%","They are equal"],"As decimals: 0.7, 0.667 and 0.68, so 0.7 is biggest."]},{"t":"Ratio, proportion & speed","intro":"Ratio and proportion questions are often worded as real-life problems: sharing money, recipes, maps and exchange rates. Compound measures like speed and density use the same thinking.","how":["To share in a ratio, add the parts, find one part, then multiply.","Simplify a ratio by dividing all parts by the same number: 12:18 = 2:3.","For recipes, find the amount for 1 person first, then scale up.","Speed = distance ÷ time. Density = mass ÷ volume. Pressure = force ÷ area.","Check units: if time is in minutes, change it to hours for km/h."],"example":{"q":"Share £60 between Sam and Priya in the ratio 2:3.","steps":["Add the parts: 2 + 3 = 5 parts.","One part: £60 ÷ 5 = £12.","Sam gets 2 × £12 = £24. Priya gets 3 × £12 = £36.","Check: £24 + £36 = £60."],"a":"Sam £24, Priya £36"},"walk":[{"h":"Best buys","b":"To compare a 400 g pack for £1.20 with a 650 g pack for £1.80, find the price per 100 g: 30p and about 27.7p. The bigger pack is better value. Always show both working values."},{"h":"Ratio in the form 1:n","b":"To write 4:10 as 1:n, divide both parts by 4: 1:2.5. This is handy for maps: a scale of 1:25 000 means 1 cm on the map is 25 000 cm (250 m) in real life."},{"h":"Speed, distance, time","b":"A car travels 150 km in 2 hours 30 minutes. 30 minutes is 0.5 hours, so speed = 150 ÷ 2.5 = 60 km/h. Don't write 2.30 hours."}],"qs":[["Simplify 15:25.",["3:5","5:3","1:10","15:5"],"Divide both by 5."],["Share £40 in the ratio 3:1. What is the larger share?",["£30","£10","£13.33","£20"],"4 parts, so 1 part is £10 and 3 parts is £30."],["A recipe for 4 uses 200 g flour. How much for 6?",["300 g","250 g","150 g","1200 g"],"200 ÷ 4 = 50 g each, × 6 = 300 g."],["Write 5:20 in the form 1:n.",["1:4","1:5","1:15","4:1"],"Divide both by 5."],["A car travels 90 miles in 1.5 hours. What is its speed?",["60 mph","135 mph","45 mph","90 mph"],"90 ÷ 1.5 = 60."],["How long does 120 km take at 40 km/h?",["3 hours","4 hours","80 hours","2 hours"],"Time = distance ÷ speed = 120 ÷ 40."],["Mass 600 g, volume 200 cm³. What is the density?",["3 g/cm³","120 000 g/cm³","0.33 g/cm³","400 g/cm³"],"600 ÷ 200 = 3."],["£1 = €1.15. How many euros is £200?",["€230","€173.91","€215","€201.15"],"200 × 1.15 = 230."],["Which is better value: 3 for £2.40 or 5 for £3.75?",["5 for £3.75","3 for £2.40","They are the same","Can't tell"],"80p each vs 75p each."],["Boys to girls is 2:3 and there are 12 boys. How many girls?",["18","8","15","30"],"One part is 6, so girls = 3 × 6 = 18."]],"bonus":["Force 50 N on an area of 2 m². What is the pressure?",["25 N/m²","100 N/m²","52 N/m²","0.04 N/m²"],"Pressure = force ÷ area = 50 ÷ 2."]},{"t":"Algebra: equations, sequences & graphs","intro":"Algebra is about a third of the Foundation paper. This week covers solving equations, the nth term of a sequence, straight-line graphs and simple quadratics.","how":["To solve an equation, do the same to both sides to get x on its own.","Expand brackets by multiplying everything inside: 3(x + 4) = 3x + 12.","Factorise by taking out the highest common factor: 6x + 9 = 3(2x + 3).","The nth term of a linear sequence is (difference)n + (zero term).","A straight line y = mx + c has gradient m and crosses the y-axis at c."],"example":{"q":"Find the nth term of 5, 8, 11, 14, …","steps":["The difference is +3, so it starts 3n.","3n gives 3, 6, 9, 12.","Each term is 2 more than 3n.","So the nth term is 3n + 2."],"a":"nth term = 3n + 2"},"walk":[{"h":"Equations with x on both sides","b":"Solve 5x − 3 = 2x + 9. Take 2x from both sides: 3x − 3 = 9. Add 3: 3x = 12. Divide by 3: x = 4. Check: 5(4) − 3 = 17 and 2(4) + 9 = 17."},{"h":"Gradient","b":"Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x. From (1, 3) to (3, 11), y goes up 8 and x goes up 2, so the gradient is 4. The line is y = 4x + c, and 3 = 4 + c gives c = −1."},{"h":"Quadratics","b":"To factorise x² + 7x + 10, find two numbers that multiply to 10 and add to 7: 5 and 2. So (x + 5)(x + 2). Then x² + 7x + 10 = 0 gives x = −5 or x = −2."}],"qs":[["Solve 4x + 3 = 23.",["x = 5","x = 6.5","x = 20","x = 4"],"4x = 20, so x = 5."],["Expand 3(2x − 5).",["6x − 15","6x − 5","5x − 15","6x + 15"],"3 × 2x = 6x and 3 × −5 = −15."],["Factorise 8x + 12.",["4(2x + 3)","2(4x + 12)","8(x + 4)","4(2x + 12)"],"The HCF of 8 and 12 is 4."],["What is the nth term of 7, 11, 15, 19?",["4n + 3","4n + 7","n + 4","3n + 4"],"Difference 4, and 4n gives 4, 8, 12, so add 3."],["What is the gradient of y = 3x − 2?",["3","−2","2","x"],"In y = mx + c the gradient is m."],["Where does y = 2x + 5 cross the y-axis?",["(0, 5)","(5, 0)","(0, 2)","(2, 5)"],"The y-intercept is c = 5."],["Solve 2(x + 4) = 18.",["x = 5","x = 7","x = 11","x = 9"],"x + 4 = 9, so x = 5."],["Factorise x² + 5x + 6.",["(x + 2)(x + 3)","(x + 1)(x + 6)","(x + 5)(x + 1)","(x − 2)(x − 3)"],"2 × 3 = 6 and 2 + 3 = 5."],["Solve x/3 = 7.",["x = 21","x = 10","x = 4","x = 7/3"],"Multiply both sides by 3."],["If a = 4 and b = −2, what is 3a − b?",["14","10","−14","12"],"3(4) − (−2) = 12 + 2 = 14."]],"bonus":["Solve 3x − 4 = x + 10.",["x = 7","x = 3","x = 14","x = 6"],"2x − 4 = 10, so 2x = 14 and x = 7."]},{"t":"Geometry: area, volume & Pythagoras","intro":"Geometry questions reward clear working and correct units. This week covers area and volume, angle rules and right-angled triangles with Pythagoras and trigonometry.","how":["Area: rectangle = l × w, triangle = ½ × b × h, trapezium = ½(a + b)h, circle = πr².","Circumference = πd. Volume of a prism = area of cross-section × length.","Angles on a straight line add to 180°, around a point to 360°, in a triangle to 180°.","Pythagoras: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse (longest side).","SOH CAH TOA: sin = opp/hyp, cos = adj/hyp, tan = opp/adj."],"example":{"q":"A right-angled triangle has sides 6 cm and 8 cm. Find the hypotenuse.","steps":["Square the two short sides: 36 and 64.","Add them: 36 + 64 = 100.","Square root: √100 = 10."],"a":"The hypotenuse is 10 cm."},"walk":[{"h":"Finding a shorter side","b":"If you know the hypotenuse is 13 and one side is 5, subtract instead: 13² − 5² = 169 − 25 = 144, so the side is √144 = 12."},{"h":"Polygon angles","b":"Exterior angles of any polygon add to 360°. A regular hexagon has exterior angles of 360 ÷ 6 = 60°, so each interior angle is 180 − 60 = 120°."},{"h":"Using trigonometry","b":"Label the sides from the angle: opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse. If you know the hypotenuse is 10 and the angle is 40°, the opposite side = 10 × sin 40 ≈ 6.43. To find an angle use the inverse: tan⁻¹(opp ÷ adj)."}],"qs":[["Area of a triangle with base 10 cm and height 6 cm?",["30 cm²","60 cm²","16 cm²","15 cm²"],"½ × 10 × 6 = 30."],["Area of a circle with radius 5 cm (to 1 dp)?",["78.5 cm²","31.4 cm²","15.7 cm²","25 cm²"],"π × 5² ≈ 78.5."],["Volume of a cuboid 2 cm × 3 cm × 4 cm?",["24 cm³","9 cm³","26 cm³","12 cm³"],"2 × 3 × 4 = 24."],["Two angles in a triangle are 50° and 70°. What is the third?",["60°","120°","180°","40°"],"180 − 50 − 70 = 60."],["Each exterior angle of a regular pentagon is…",["72°","108°","60°","36°"],"360 ÷ 5 = 72."],["Hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle with sides 5 and 12?",["13","17","7","11"],"25 + 144 = 169, √169 = 13."],["Which ratio is tan?",["opposite ÷ adjacent","opposite ÷ hypotenuse","adjacent ÷ hypotenuse","hypotenuse ÷ opposite"],"TOA: tan = opp/adj."],["Area of a trapezium with parallel sides 4 and 6 and height 5?",["25","50","120","30"],"½ × (4 + 6) × 5 = 25."],["Circumference of a circle with diameter 10 cm (to 1 dp)?",["31.4 cm","78.5 cm","62.8 cm","15.7 cm"],"π × 10 ≈ 31.4."],["Angles on a straight line add up to…",["180°","90°","360°","270°"],"A straight line is half a full turn."]],"bonus":["A ladder 5 m long reaches 4 m up a wall. How far is its foot from the wall?",["3 m","1 m","6.4 m","9 m"],"5² − 4² = 25 − 16 = 9, √9 = 3."]}]},{"id":"english-lang","name":"English Language","weeks":[{"t":"Paper 2 reading: Q1 retrieval & Q2 synthesis","intro":"Paper 2 gives you two non-fiction sources, usually one from the 19th century and one modern, on a similar topic. Q1 and Q2 test whether you can find information and combine ideas from both texts. They're worth 12 marks in about 13 minutes.","how":["Use the 15 minutes' reading time: read the questions first, then the sources.","Use GAPT for each source: Genre, Audience, Purpose, Tone.","Q1 (4 marks, 4 min): shade exactly four true statements. Check each against the lines given.","Q2 (8 marks, 9 min): write about differences or similarities using BOTH sources in every paragraph.","In Q2, make inferences (what it suggests) but don't analyse language. There are no marks for that."],"example":{"q":"Q2: Using both sources, write a summary of the differences between the two boys' experiences of school.","steps":["Point: In Source A the boy is miserable at school, whereas in Source B the girl enjoys it.","Evidence A: 'I dreaded the cold, grey mornings' suggests…","Evidence B: 'she skipped through the gates' suggests…","Inference: link both to a bigger idea, e.g. how attitudes to childhood have changed."],"a":"Two paragraphs, each with a clear difference, a quote from each source and an inference."},"walk":[{"h":"Chunking the sources","b":"Give each paragraph a short subheading in the margin, like 'arrival', 'fear of teacher', 'escape'. This makes finding quotes quick and helps you see how each writer's ideas move through the text."},{"h":"Making inferences","b":"An inference reads between the lines. 'He ate the crust in silence' tells you he's eating, but it suggests he's hungry, poor and perhaps afraid to complain. Use words like suggests, implies and reveals."},{"h":"Linking words for synthesis","b":"Use comparison connectives to tie the sources together: whereas, however, similarly, in contrast, likewise, on the other hand. Each Q2 paragraph should feel like one point about both texts."}],"qs":[["How many statements should you shade in Q1?",["Four","Two","Five","As many as are true"],"Q1 asks you to choose exactly four true statements."],["How long should you spend on Q2?",["About 9 minutes","About 20 minutes","About 4 minutes","About 45 minutes"],"Roughly a minute per mark: 8 marks, 9 minutes."],["What does Q2 ask you to do?",["Summarise similarities or differences using both sources","Analyse language in one source","Write your own opinion","Compare writers' methods"],"Q2 is a synthesis question about content."],["Which earns NO credit in Q2?",["Language analysis","Quotes from both sources","Inferences","Comparison words"],"The mark scheme says language analysis gets no credit in Q2."],["What does the A in GAPT stand for?",["Audience","Analysis","Attitude","Argument"],"Genre, Audience, Purpose, Tone."],["Which word introduces an inference?",["Suggests","Says","States","Writes"],"'Suggests' shows you're reading between the lines."],["Which connective shows a difference?",["Whereas","Similarly","Likewise","Also"],"'Whereas' introduces a contrast."],["How long is the reading time at the start of Paper 2?",["15 minutes","5 minutes","30 minutes","None"],"The school guide says you get 15 minutes' reading time."],["Paper 2 sources are usually…",["One 19th-century and one modern non-fiction text","Two poems","Two novels","One play and one poem"],"Paper 2 is 'Writers' viewpoints and perspectives' using non-fiction."],["How many paragraphs does the guide suggest for Q2?",["About two","One sentence","Five or more","Ten"],"Two developed paragraphs, each using both texts."]],"bonus":["Which is the best Q2 sentence?",["Source A's boy feels trapped, whereas Source B's girl feels free, suggesting schooling has become kinder.","The writer uses a metaphor to create imagery.","I think school is good.","Source A is old."],"It makes a clear difference, uses both texts and makes an inference."]},{"t":"Q3 language analysis","intro":"Q3 asks how a writer uses language in one part of a source. It's worth 12 marks in about 14 minutes. Top answers pick precise words and explain their effect on the reader in detail.","how":["Box the line numbers the question gives you. Only use quotes from those lines.","Pick 4 to 6 short quotes: single words or phrases are best.","Name the technique where it helps, such as a metaphor, a verb choice or a list.","Zoom in on one word and explore what it suggests, including connotations.","Track how the language changes across the extract and link to the writer's big idea."],"example":{"q":"How does the writer use language to describe the storm?","steps":["Quote: 'the sea clawed at the harbour wall'.","Technique: personification and the verb 'clawed'.","Zoom: 'clawed' makes the sea seem like a wild animal attacking.","Effect: the reader feels the storm is violent, alive and dangerous."],"a":"Quote → technique → zoom on a word → effect on the reader, repeated 3 times."},"walk":[{"h":"Useful techniques","b":"Look for metaphors, similes, personification, emotive language, sensory language, lists, rhetorical questions, repetition, contrast, hyperbole and sentence types. Naming a technique only gets marks if you then explain its effect."},{"h":"Zooming in","b":"Take one word and unpack it. In 'the city groaned', 'groaned' suggests pain and tiredness, as if the city were an old, suffering person. This creates sympathy and a gloomy mood."},{"h":"Linking to big ideas","b":"Top-band answers ask why the writer chose these words. Is the writer warning us, criticising society or celebrating nature? Tie your final paragraph to that purpose."}],"qs":[["How many marks is Q3?",["12","8","16","4"],"Q3 is worth 12 marks."],["How many quotes does the guide suggest for Q3?",["About 4 to 6","1","About 15","None"],"The school's guide says about 4 to 6 quotes or techniques."],["'The wind howled' is an example of…",["Personification","Simile","Alliteration","Rhetorical question"],"Wind can't howl like a person or animal, so it's personification."],["What makes a Q3 point strong?",["Explaining the effect of a precise word","Naming as many techniques as possible","Retelling the story","Using quotes from the whole text"],"Effect on the reader is what earns the marks."],["'As cold as ice' is a…",["Simile","Metaphor","Oxymoron","Pun"],"It compares using 'as'."],["Where must your Q3 quotes come from?",["The lines given in the question","Anywhere in both sources","Source B only","Your own ideas"],"Q3 focuses on a specific section of one source."],["Connotations are…",["The ideas and feelings a word suggests","The dictionary meaning","Spelling rules","Paragraph topics"],"e.g. 'crimson' suggests blood, danger or passion."],["Which is the best analysis?",["'Clawed' presents the sea as a predator, making the storm feel deliberately cruel.","There is a verb here.","The writer uses good words.","It is a sentence about the sea."],"It zooms in on a word and explains the effect."],["A list of short sentences can create…",["Tension or urgency","No effect","Only humour","Rhyme"],"Short, rapid sentences speed up the pace."],["Should Q3 compare the two sources?",["No, it focuses on one source","Yes, always","Only in the conclusion","Only if they're similar"],"Comparison is Q4. Q3 is one source."]],"bonus":["'The silence was deafening' is an example of…",["Oxymoron","Simile","Onomatopoeia","Alliteration"],"It puts two opposite ideas together."]},{"t":"Q4 comparing writers' viewpoints","intro":"Q4 is the biggest reading question on Paper 2: 16 marks in about 18 minutes. You compare how the two writers convey their attitudes to the same topic, so you need what they think AND how they show it.","how":["Work out each writer's viewpoint in one sentence before you start writing.","Find evidence of attitude at the start, middle and end of each source.","Each paragraph: writer A's view + method + quote, then compare with writer B.","Discuss methods: language, tone, structure and even form (letter, article, diary).","Write three detailed comparative paragraphs that track how views develop."],"example":{"q":"Compare how the writers convey their different attitudes to city life.","steps":["Viewpoints: A finds London dirty and frightening. B finds it exciting.","A: 'choking fog' uses sensory language to show disgust.","In contrast, B: 'buzzing streets' suggests energy and joy.","Develop: A's tone darkens at the end, while B ends hopeful."],"a":"Three comparative paragraphs covering viewpoint, methods and how attitudes develop."},"walk":[{"h":"Viewpoint vs perspective","b":"A viewpoint is the writer's opinion (city life is awful). Perspective is the background shaping it, like being a Victorian visitor or a modern young worker. Mentioning perspective helps you explain why their views differ."},{"h":"Tone words","b":"Have a bank of tone words ready: critical, scornful, nostalgic, enthusiastic, sarcastic, anxious, sympathetic, outraged, reflective, humorous. 'The writer's tone is scornful' is more precise than 'the writer doesn't like it'."},{"h":"Structure for 16 marks","b":"Open with an overview comparing both viewpoints. Then three paragraphs, each comparing one aspect with quotes from both. Keep comparing all the way through, rather than writing about A and then B separately."}],"qs":[["How many marks is Q4?",["16","12","8","40"],"Q4 is worth 16 marks."],["What does Q4 focus on?",["Writers' attitudes and how they convey them","Spelling and grammar","Only facts from Source A","Your own opinion"],"It's about viewpoints and methods."],["How long should you spend on Q4?",["About 18 minutes","About 5 minutes","About 45 minutes","About 9 minutes"],"Roughly a minute a mark plus planning."],["Which is a tone word?",["Scornful","Paragraph","Noun","Source"],"Scornful describes the writer's attitude."],["A writer's perspective is shaped by…",["Their background, era and experience","The font used","The exam board","The reader's age"],"When and who they are affects their view."],["Best Q4 structure?",["Compare both sources in every paragraph","All of Source A, then all of Source B","Only write about Source B","One long quote"],"Integrated comparison scores highest."],["Which is a 'method'?",["A rhetorical question","The writer's age","The date of publication only","The number of lines"],"Methods are the language and structural choices writers make."],["What should Q4 track?",["How each writer's view develops through the text","Only the first line","Only the title","Word count"],"The guide says track and compare how views develop."],["Which connective works for comparing?",["Conversely","Firstly","Finally","Because"],"'Conversely' shows an opposite view."],["How many paragraphs does the school suggest?",["Three detailed paragraphs","One","Six short ones","Just a conclusion"],"The guide says three detailed comparative paragraphs."]],"bonus":["A Victorian writer calls factory children 'brave little souls'. The tone is most likely…",["Sympathetic","Scornful","Humorous","Neutral"],"'Brave little souls' shows pity and admiration."]},{"t":"Q5 writing to argue & persuade","intro":"Q5 is worth 40 marks, half the paper. You'll get a statement and write an article, letter, speech or essay giving your view. Spend 45 minutes on it and plan first.","how":["Spend 5 minutes planning: decide your view and 4 or 5 points.","Match the form: letters need Dear…, articles need a headline, speeches address the audience.","Use DAFOREST: Direct address, Anecdote, Facts, Opinion, Rhetorical questions, Emotive language, Statistics, Triples.","Vary sentences and punctuation: short punchy sentences, colons, semicolons, questions.","Leave 5 minutes to proofread for spelling, punctuation and full stops."],"example":{"q":"'Homework is a waste of time and should be banned.' Write an article for a broadsheet newspaper giving your view.","steps":["Headline: Homework: Help or Hindrance?","Opening hook: a short anecdote about a student working until midnight.","Body: three points with DAFOREST, including one counter-argument you rebut.","Ending: circle back to the anecdote and finish with a strong call to action."],"a":"A planned, well-structured article with a clear viewpoint and varied techniques."},"walk":[{"h":"Content and organisation (24 marks)","b":"Examiners want a clear, convincing voice and paragraphs that link logically. Use discourse markers like furthermore, admittedly and ultimately. A strong structure might go: hook, problem, evidence, counter-argument, solution, ending."},{"h":"Technical accuracy (16 marks)","b":"These marks are for spelling, punctuation, grammar and vocabulary. Show a range: ambitious words used correctly, semicolons, colons, dashes and question marks. Accurate simple writing beats ambitious but messy writing."},{"h":"Big picture ideas","b":"Read good journalism (BBC News, The Guardian, The Telegraph) so you have current examples. Linking a topic to bigger issues like mental health, the environment or fairness makes your argument more mature."}],"qs":[["How many marks is Q5?",["40","16","24","12"],"It's half of the 80 marks on the paper."],["How long should you spend on Q5?",["About 45 minutes","About 15 minutes","About 90 minutes","About 9 minutes"],"The guide says 45 minutes."],["In DAFOREST, what does the R stand for?",["Rhetorical questions","Repetition","Rhyme","Reasons"],"Rhetorical questions make the reader think."],["Which is a triple (rule of three)?",["'It is cruel, costly and completely pointless.'","'It is bad.'","'Why?'","'Dear Sir.'"],"Three linked items for impact."],["An article usually needs…",["A headline","Dear Sir/Madam","Stage directions","Rhyme"],"Articles have a headline and often a subheading."],["What is a counter-argument?",["An opposing view that you then argue against","A list of facts","Your conclusion","A spelling check"],"Acknowledging the other side then rebutting it makes you more convincing."],["Technical accuracy is worth…",["16 marks","24 marks","40 marks","4 marks"],"24 for content and organisation, 16 for technical accuracy."],["Which punctuation shows range?",["Semicolons and colons used correctly","Only commas","Lots of exclamation marks!!!","No full stops"],"Accurate variety scores well."],["Which is the best opening hook?",["'At 11:47pm, Maya was still crying over her algebra.'","'In this article I will write about homework.'","'Homework.'","'Hello.'"],"An anecdote draws the reader in."],["What should you do in the last 5 minutes?",["Proofread","Start a new essay","Rewrite Q1","Count words"],"Fixing errors gains technical accuracy marks."]],"bonus":["'Is it fair that young people pay the price for adults' mistakes?' is…",["A rhetorical question","A statistic","An anecdote","A fact"],"It's asked for effect, not for an answer."]}]},{"id":"english-lit","name":"English Literature","weeks":[{"t":"A Christmas Carol: plot by stave","intro":"Your Literature mock on A Christmas Carol is sat in lesson time before the mock period, so revise it first. Knowing exactly what happens in each stave lets you pick evidence from across the whole novella.","how":["Learn the five staves in order and one key event from each.","Memorise at least two short quotes per stave.","Practise opening the book at random and saying what happens just before and after.","Link each stave to Scrooge's transformation: cold, shaken, softening, terrified, redeemed.","In the exam, use the extract and then refer to other parts of the novella."],"example":{"q":"What happens in Stave 1?","steps":["Scrooge refuses to give to charity and rejects Fred's invitation.","He grudgingly gives Bob Cratchit Christmas Day off.","Marley's ghost appears in chains and warns him.","Marley says three spirits will visit."],"a":"Stave 1 shows Scrooge at his worst and sets up the warning."},"walk":[{"h":"Staves 1 and 2","b":"Stave 1: Scrooge is 'a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous old sinner'. Marley's ghost wears 'the chain I forged in life'. Stave 2: the Ghost of Christmas Past shows lonely young Scrooge at school, Fezziwig's joyful party, and Belle leaving him because 'another idol has displaced me'."},{"h":"Stave 3","b":"The Ghost of Christmas Present shows the Cratchits' happy but poor Christmas dinner and Tiny Tim's illness. Scrooge's own words come back at him: 'decrease the surplus population'. The ghost reveals two children, Ignorance and Want, under his robe."},{"h":"Staves 4 and 5","b":"The silent Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come shows businessmen joking about a dead man, thieves selling his belongings, and Tiny Tim's death. Scrooge sees his own grave and begs to change. In Stave 5 he wakes 'as light as a feather', buys the prize turkey, gives to charity, visits Fred and raises Bob's salary."}],"qs":[["Whose ghost visits Scrooge in Stave 1?",["Jacob Marley","Fezziwig","Fred","Belle"],"Marley was Scrooge's dead business partner."],["Which ghost shows Fezziwig's party?",["Christmas Past","Christmas Present","Christmas Yet to Come","Marley"],"Stave 2 revisits Scrooge's youth."],["Who ends her engagement to Scrooge?",["Belle","Mrs Cratchit","Fan","Martha"],"Belle says money has become his 'idol'."],["Which two children hide under the Ghost of Christmas Present's robe?",["Ignorance and Want","Fan and Fred","Tiny Tim and Martha","Hope and Joy"],"They symbolise society's neglect of the poor."],["What does Scrooge buy in Stave 5?",["The prize turkey","A goose","A new coat","A coffin"],"He sends it to the Cratchits anonymously."],["What does Marley wear?",["A chain he forged in life","A crown","A white robe","A green robe"],"The chain is made of cash-boxes and ledgers, a symbol of greed."],["How many staves are there?",["Five","Three","Four","Ten"],"Dickens called the chapters staves, like verses of a carol."],["What does the last ghost do that's unusual?",["It never speaks","It sings","It laughs constantly","It wears chains"],"Its silence makes it more frightening."],["Who invites Scrooge to Christmas dinner every year?",["Fred, his nephew","Bob Cratchit","Fezziwig","Belle"],"Fred keeps inviting him despite his rudeness."],["What does Scrooge see in Stave 4 that terrifies him most?",["His own neglected grave","Fezziwig's party","His school","The prize turkey"],"It forces him to promise to change."]],"bonus":["'Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?' Who says this?",["Scrooge","Fred","Marley","Bob Cratchit"],"Scrooge says it to the charity collectors in Stave 1. His words are later thrown back at him."]},{"t":"A Christmas Carol: characters","intro":"Character questions are common. You need to know how Dickens presents each character and how they change, with a quote bank for each one.","how":["For Scrooge, track the change: miserly → regretful → generous.","For other characters, know what they represent: Fred = Christmas spirit, Bob = the hard-working poor.","Make a list of 3 to 5 quotes for each character and learn them.","Always say what Dickens is using the character to show.","Use 'Dickens presents…' to show you understand characters are constructed."],"example":{"q":"How does Dickens present Scrooge as cold at the start?","steps":["Quote: 'The cold within him froze his old features'.","Method: pathetic fallacy and a weather metaphor.","Effect: his meanness is so strong it affects the world around him.","Purpose: sets up the contrast with his warm transformation."],"a":"Use quote, method, effect and Dickens's purpose."},"walk":[{"h":"Scrooge","b":"At first he's 'solitary as an oyster' and 'hard and sharp as flint'. In Stave 2 he weeps at his lonely childhood. In Stave 5 he's 'as merry as a schoolboy' and becomes 'a second father' to Tiny Tim. Dickens uses him to show anyone can change."},{"h":"The Cratchits and Fred","b":"Bob Cratchit is underpaid but loyal and cheerful, showing the dignity of the poor. Tiny Tim ('God bless us, every one!') makes readers feel the human cost of poverty. Fred is warm and forgiving and represents the true spirit of Christmas."},{"h":"Marley and the ghosts","b":"Marley is a warning: his chains show the punishment for greed. The Ghost of Christmas Past is gentle and bright, Present is a generous giant of plenty, and Yet to Come is a silent, dark figure like Death. Each one moves Scrooge further."}],"qs":[["Scrooge is 'solitary as an…'?",["oyster","island","owl","orphan"],"The simile suggests he's closed off and hard on the outside."],["What does Fred represent?",["The spirit of Christmas and family","Greed","Death","The law"],"He's cheerful, forgiving and generous."],["Which character says 'God bless us, every one!'?",["Tiny Tim","Scrooge","Fred","Belle"],"His goodness makes his possible death more moving."],["What does Bob Cratchit show?",["The poor can be kind and dignified","The rich are always good","Christmas is pointless","Ghosts are real"],"Dickens challenges negative views of the poor."],["What does Scrooge become to Tiny Tim?",["A second father","An enemy","A teacher","A ghost"],"He helps the family and Tim lives."],["Which ghost is described like a giant with a cornucopia-like feast?",["Christmas Present","Christmas Past","Yet to Come","Marley"],"He represents generosity and plenty."],["Why does Marley carry chains?",["As punishment for a life of greed","To scare children","Because he was a prisoner","To lock Scrooge's door"],"'I wear the chain I forged in life.'"],["Fezziwig is presented as…",["A kind, generous employer","A cruel miser","A ghost","Scrooge's nephew"],"He contrasts with how Scrooge treats Bob."],["'Hard and sharp as flint' describes…",["Scrooge","Fred","Bob","Fan"],"It shows he's cold and unfeeling."],["How should you refer to characters in essays?",["'Dickens presents…' them","As real people","Only by quotes","By their age"],"It shows you know the writer made deliberate choices."]],"bonus":["Who is Fan?",["Scrooge's younger sister, Fred's mother","Bob's wife","Belle's daughter","The first ghost"],"She rescues young Scrooge from school, showing he was once loved."]},{"t":"A Christmas Carol: themes","intro":"Many questions are about a theme. The big ones are transformation and redemption, social responsibility, poverty and greed, family and Christmas. Track how each develops across the staves.","how":["Know 3 or 4 quotes for each theme (they can overlap with character quotes).","Track the theme from start to end, showing how it develops.","Link every theme to Dickens's message to Victorian readers.","Use the extract first, then refer to the rest of the novella.","Plan quickly: 3 or 4 points that move through the text."],"example":{"q":"How does Dickens explore social responsibility?","steps":["Start: Scrooge rejects the poor: 'decrease the surplus population'.","Middle: Ignorance and Want warn about society's neglect.","End: Scrooge gives to charity and helps the Cratchits.","Message: the rich have a duty to help the poor."],"a":"Track the theme across the novella and finish with Dickens's message."},"walk":[{"h":"Redemption","b":"Scrooge's change shows that anyone can be redeemed if they choose. Dickens uses the ghosts as a structure: each one forces Scrooge to face something, and by the end he 'will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year'."},{"h":"Social responsibility and poverty","b":"Marley says 'Mankind was my business'. The Cratchits and Tiny Tim make poverty personal. Ignorance and Want, 'this boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both', warn that neglecting the poor will harm everyone."},{"h":"Family and Christmas","b":"Christmas is shown as a time of warmth, generosity and togetherness: Fred's party, Fezziwig's ball, the Cratchits' dinner. Scrooge's isolation shows what greed takes away from a person."}],"qs":[["Who says 'Mankind was my business'?",["Marley","Scrooge","Fred","Bob"],"Marley regrets ignoring his duty to others."],["Ignorance and Want represent…",["Society's neglect of poor children","Scrooge's childhood","Christmas spirits","The rich"],"They are a warning to readers."],["Which theme is shown by Scrooge's change?",["Redemption","War","Romance","Revenge"],"He's redeemed by the end."],["What is Dickens's main message?",["The rich should help the poor","Ghosts are dangerous","Money brings happiness","Christmas is for children only"],"He wanted readers to act."],["'Decrease the surplus population' shows Scrooge's…",["Cold view of the poor","Kindness","Love of Christmas","Fear of ghosts"],"He suggests the poor should die."],["Which scene best shows family warmth?",["The Cratchits' Christmas dinner","Scrooge's counting house","The thieves selling goods","Scrooge's grave"],"They're poor but happy together."],["Which theme links to Scrooge's broken engagement?",["Greed","Education","Science","Travel"],"Money became his 'idol'."],["What should a theme answer do?",["Track the theme across the whole novella","Only use one quote","Retell the plot","Ignore the extract"],"Show development from start to end."],["Scrooge promises to 'honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it…'",["all the year","for one day","for Fred","a secret"],"It shows lasting change."],["Isolation is shown through…",["Scrooge eating alone and his lonely childhood","Fezziwig's party","Fred's games","The Cratchits' pudding"],"Greed leaves Scrooge cut off from others."]],"bonus":["Why is it called A Christmas 'Carol'?",["Dickens wanted it to spread a joyful, moral message like a song","It was written to be sung","Carol is a character","It rhymes"],"The staves are like verses of a carol."]},{"t":"Context & exam technique","intro":"Context (AO3) shows you understand why Dickens wrote the novella in 1843. Link context to the text, then practise a full 45-minute answer using a past paper.","how":["Learn key context: the class divide, the Poor Law, workhouses, Malthus, the Industrial Revolution.","Link context to a quote rather than writing a history lesson.","'Explode' the question: highlight the key words and make sure every paragraph answers them.","Plan 3 or 4 points, using the extract and the whole text.","Cover AO1 (response and quotes), AO2 (language, form, structure) and AO3 (context)."],"example":{"q":"How would you add context to 'Are there no workhouses?'","steps":["Quote: 'Are there no workhouses?'","Analysis: rhetorical questions show Scrooge's cold dismissal.","Context: the 1834 Poor Law Amendment Act forced the poor into harsh workhouses.","Link: Dickens criticises rich Victorians who thought workhouses were enough."],"a":"Context woven into analysis, linked to Dickens's purpose."},"walk":[{"h":"Victorian poverty","b":"The 1834 Poor Law Amendment Act meant help for the poor mostly came through workhouses, which were deliberately grim. Thomas Malthus argued that population growth would outstrip food. Scrooge's 'surplus population' echoes him."},{"h":"Dickens's life","b":"At 12, Dickens worked in a blacking factory while his father was in a debtors' prison. He never forgot it. He wrote A Christmas Carol after seeing ragged schools and child labour, calling it a 'sledgehammer' blow for the poor child."},{"h":"Exam technique","b":"You get one question with an extract and 45 minutes. Spend 5 minutes planning. Start with the extract, move to other staves, and finish with Dickens's message. Embed short quotes, and memorise them, because it's a closed-book exam."}],"qs":[["When was A Christmas Carol published?",["1843","1943","1743","1903"],"It came out in December 1843."],["What did the 1834 Poor Law Amendment Act lead to?",["More harsh workhouses","Free schools","Higher wages","Christmas holidays"],"The poor had to enter workhouses to get help."],["Whose ideas does 'surplus population' echo?",["Thomas Malthus","Charles Darwin","Queen Victoria","Karl Marx"],"Malthus wrote about population outgrowing resources."],["Where did Dickens work as a child?",["A blacking factory","A bank","A school","A church"],"This shaped his sympathy for poor children."],["What is AO3?",["Context","Spelling","Personal response","Language analysis"],"AO1 response, AO2 methods, AO3 context."],["How long is the exam answer?",["45 minutes","15 minutes","2 hours","90 minutes"],"The guide says 45 minutes for one question."],["Can you take the book into the exam?",["No, it's closed book","Yes","Only the extract pages","Only notes"],"That's why the school says you must memorise quotes."],["Best way to use context?",["Link it to a quote and Dickens's purpose","Write a page of history","Skip it","Put it only in the conclusion"],"Context should support your argument."],["'Explode the question' means…",["Highlight and unpick the key words","Write as much as possible","Ignore the question","Answer a different one"],"It keeps every paragraph focused."],["The Industrial Revolution meant…",["Fast-growing cities with huge gaps between rich and poor","Everyone became rich","No factories","Less work for children"],"Wealth grew for some while many lived in poverty."]],"bonus":["Dickens called the novella a 'sledgehammer'. What did he mean?",["It would hit readers hard with a message about poverty","It was about building","It was very long","It was violent"],"He wanted a powerful moral effect."]}]},{"id":"biology","name":"Biology","weeks":[{"t":"Genes, inheritance & evolution (B1, B6)","intro":"This covers chapters B1 and B6 of your OCR Twenty First Century course: what genes are, how features are inherited, and how evolution by natural selection works.","how":["The genome is all the genetic material of an organism. A gene is a section of DNA that codes for a protein.","DNA is a polymer made of nucleotides. The bases pair up: A with T, and C with G.","Alleles are different versions of a gene. Dominant alleles show with one copy, recessive ones need two.","Use a Punnett square to predict offspring ratios.","Natural selection: variation → competition → best-adapted survive and reproduce → pass on alleles."],"example":{"q":"Two parents are both Bb (B = brown eyes, dominant). What fraction of children could have blue eyes (bb)?","steps":["Gametes from each parent: B and b.","Punnett square: BB, Bb, Bb, bb.","Only bb shows blue eyes.","1 out of 4 = ¼, or 25%."],"a":"¼ (25%) chance of blue eyes"},"walk":[{"h":"Genotype and phenotype","b":"The genotype is the alleles you have (e.g. Bb). The phenotype is the characteristic you see (brown eyes). Homozygous means two of the same allele (BB or bb). Heterozygous means two different (Bb)."},{"h":"Protein synthesis","b":"In the nucleus, a gene is copied into mRNA (transcription). The mRNA travels to a ribosome, which reads the bases in threes and links amino acids into a protein (translation). A change in the bases, a mutation, can change the protein."},{"h":"Evolution and evidence","b":"Darwin's theory explains how species change over time. Evidence includes fossils, which show gradual change, and antibiotic-resistant bacteria, which evolve quickly as resistant bacteria survive treatment and multiply."}],"qs":[["What is a gene?",["A section of DNA that codes for a protein","A whole chromosome","A type of cell","A protein"],"Each gene codes for a sequence of amino acids."],["Which base pairs with A in DNA?",["T","C","G","U"],"A pairs with T and C pairs with G."],["What does heterozygous mean?",["Two different alleles, e.g. Bb","Two dominant alleles","Two recessive alleles","No alleles"],"Hetero means different."],["What is a phenotype?",["The characteristic shown","The alleles present","A sex cell","A mutation"],"Phenotype = what you see."],["Bb × bb. What fraction of offspring are bb?",["½","¼","¾","0"],"Punnett square gives Bb, Bb, bb, bb."],["Where are proteins made?",["Ribosomes","Nucleus","Cell wall","Vacuole"],"Ribosomes join amino acids together."],["What is a mutation?",["A change in the DNA base sequence","A type of protein","A new species","A dominant allele"],"Mutations create new alleles and variation."],["Which is evidence for evolution?",["Fossils","Vaccines","Photosynthesis","Enzymes"],"Fossils show how organisms have changed."],["Why do antibiotic-resistant bacteria spread?",["Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce","Antibiotics create them on purpose","Viruses help them","They are all killed"],"That's natural selection in action."],["Who proposed the theory of evolution by natural selection?",["Charles Darwin","Louis Pasteur","Isaac Newton","Gregor Mendel"],"Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859."]],"bonus":["What does the genome mean?",["All the genetic material of an organism","One gene","The nucleus","A chromosome pair"],"The whole set of DNA."]},{"t":"Keeping healthy: disease & immunity (B2)","intro":"B2 is about what causes disease, how your body fights pathogens, and how we prevent and treat infections. Expect questions on the immune system, vaccines and antibiotics.","how":["Communicable diseases are caused by pathogens: bacteria, viruses, fungi and protists.","Non-communicable diseases, like heart disease, are not passed on but are linked to lifestyle and genes.","First defences: skin, mucus, cilia, stomach acid.","White blood cells engulf pathogens (phagocytosis), make antibodies and make antitoxins.","Vaccines contain dead or weakened pathogens, so memory cells form and respond fast next time."],"example":{"q":"Explain how a vaccine protects against measles.","steps":["The vaccine contains a dead or weakened measles virus with its antigens.","White blood cells (lymphocytes) make antibodies against it.","Memory cells remain in the blood.","If the real virus enters, memory cells make antibodies quickly, before you get ill."],"a":"Memory cells give a fast, strong second response."},"walk":[{"h":"How pathogens spread","b":"Pathogens spread by air (flu), water (cholera), direct contact (athlete's foot) and vectors like mosquitoes (malaria). We reduce spread with hygiene, isolation, vaccination and killing vectors."},{"h":"Treating disease","b":"Antibiotics kill bacteria but NOT viruses. Overusing them leads to resistant strains like MRSA. Painkillers treat symptoms but don't kill pathogens. New drugs go through preclinical testing on cells and animals, then clinical trials, including double-blind trials with placebos."},{"h":"Monoclonal antibodies","b":"These are identical antibodies made from one clone of cells. They bind to one specific antigen, so they're used in pregnancy tests, to detect diseases, and to carry drugs straight to cancer cells."}],"qs":[["Which of these is a pathogen?",["A bacterium","A white blood cell","A red blood cell","An antibody"],"Pathogens are microorganisms that cause disease."],["Do antibiotics work against viruses?",["No","Yes","Only flu","Only if strong"],"Antibiotics only kill bacteria."],["What is phagocytosis?",["White blood cells engulfing pathogens","Making antibodies","Blood clotting","Cell division"],"Phagocytes surround and digest pathogens."],["What do vaccines cause the body to make?",["Memory cells","Red blood cells","Antibiotics","Toxins"],"Memory cells give quick immunity later."],["Malaria is spread by…",["Mosquitoes (vectors)","Water","Handshakes","Air"],"The mosquito carries the protist pathogen."],["Which is a non-communicable disease?",["Coronary heart disease","Measles","Flu","Malaria"],"It can't be caught from someone else."],["What is a placebo?",["A fake treatment with no drug","A strong antibiotic","A vaccine","A type of virus"],"It's used to compare against the real drug."],["In a double-blind trial, who knows who gets the real drug?",["Neither the doctor nor the patient","Only the doctor","Only the patient","Everyone"],"This stops bias."],["What do antitoxins do?",["Neutralise toxins made by bacteria","Kill viruses directly","Carry oxygen","Clot blood"],"They cancel out harmful poisons."],["Overuse of antibiotics can lead to…",["Resistant bacteria","Stronger immune systems","Fewer viruses","Vaccines"],"Resistant bacteria survive and multiply."]],"bonus":["Why are monoclonal antibodies useful against cancer?",["They bind only to specific antigens on cancer cells","They kill all cells","They are antibiotics","They make red blood cells"],"They can deliver drugs straight to the cancer cells."]},{"t":"Photosynthesis, respiration & ecosystems (B3, B4)","intro":"Photosynthesis traps energy and respiration releases it. Ecosystems questions look at how organisms depend on each other. You need the equations and the factors that change the rate.","how":["Photosynthesis: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen (needs light, happens in chloroplasts).","Limiting factors for photosynthesis: light intensity, CO₂ concentration and temperature.","Aerobic respiration: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water (releases energy, in mitochondria).","Anaerobic respiration in muscles: glucose → lactic acid. In yeast: glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide.","In food chains, energy is lost at each level, so there are fewer organisms higher up."],"example":{"q":"Why does the rate of photosynthesis stop increasing at high light intensity?","steps":["As light increases, the rate goes up at first.","Then the graph levels off.","Another factor, like CO₂ or temperature, is now limiting the rate.","Increasing that factor would raise the rate again."],"a":"Something other than light becomes the limiting factor."},"walk":[{"h":"Respiration is exothermic","b":"Respiration releases energy, so it's exothermic. Cells use it for muscle contraction, keeping warm and building molecules. During hard exercise, anaerobic respiration builds an oxygen debt that you repay by breathing hard afterwards."},{"h":"Interdependence","b":"If one population changes, others are affected. If foxes are removed, rabbit numbers rise, then fall as they run out of grass. Biotic factors are living (predators, disease). Abiotic factors are non-living (light, temperature, water)."},{"h":"Practicals","b":"Measure the rate of photosynthesis by counting oxygen bubbles from pondweed at different distances from a lamp. Use quadrats and transects to sample plants in a field. Keep other variables the same for a fair test."}],"qs":[["Where does photosynthesis happen?",["Chloroplasts","Mitochondria","Nucleus","Ribosomes"],"Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll to absorb light."],["What are the products of photosynthesis?",["Glucose and oxygen","Carbon dioxide and water","Lactic acid","Ethanol and CO₂"],"CO₂ + water → glucose + oxygen."],["Where does aerobic respiration happen?",["Mitochondria","Chloroplasts","Cell wall","Vacuole"],"Mitochondria release energy from glucose."],["What does anaerobic respiration in muscles produce?",["Lactic acid","Ethanol","Oxygen","Glucose"],"It happens when oxygen runs short."],["Yeast respiring anaerobically makes…",["Ethanol and carbon dioxide","Lactic acid","Oxygen","Water only"],"That's fermentation, used in bread and brewing."],["Which is NOT a limiting factor of photosynthesis?",["Oxygen concentration","Light intensity","CO₂ concentration","Temperature"],"Oxygen is a product, not a limiting factor."],["Respiration is…",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Neither","Only in animals"],"It releases energy."],["Which is an abiotic factor?",["Temperature","Predators","Disease","Competition"],"Abiotic = non-living."],["Why are food chains usually short?",["Energy is lost at each level","Animals are lazy","Plants run out","There's too much energy"],"Energy is lost as heat and waste."],["What equipment is used to sample plants in a field?",["Quadrat","Bunsen burner","Pipette","Ammeter"],"A quadrat is a square frame for counting."]],"bonus":["Why does photosynthesis slow down at very high temperatures?",["Enzymes become denatured","Light stops","CO₂ disappears","Leaves fall off"],"High heat changes the enzymes' active sites."]},{"t":"The human body: nerves, hormones & homeostasis (B5)","intro":"B5 covers how your body moves substances around, responds to change and keeps conditions steady. Learn the reflex arc, key hormones and blood sugar control.","how":["Reflex arc: stimulus → receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → effector.","Reflexes are fast and automatic and don't involve conscious thought.","Hormones are chemical messages carried in the blood from glands to target organs.","Insulin from the pancreas lowers blood glucose by making the liver store it as glycogen.","Homeostasis keeps internal conditions like temperature, water and glucose constant."],"example":{"q":"Describe what happens after a meal raises blood glucose.","steps":["The pancreas detects high blood glucose.","It releases insulin into the blood.","Insulin makes cells take up glucose and the liver store it as glycogen.","Blood glucose falls back to normal: negative feedback."],"a":"Insulin lowers blood glucose."},"walk":[{"h":"Diabetes","b":"Type 1 diabetes: the pancreas makes little or no insulin, so it's treated with insulin injections. Type 2: body cells stop responding to insulin, often linked to obesity, and is treated with diet, exercise and medicine. If glucose is too low, glucagon makes the liver release glucose."},{"h":"Surface area and transport","b":"Large organisms have a small surface area to volume ratio, so they need exchange surfaces (alveoli, villi) and transport systems (heart and blood vessels). To calculate SA:V for a 2 cm cube: SA = 24 cm², volume = 8 cm³, ratio 3:1."},{"h":"Reproductive hormones","b":"Oestrogen and progesterone control the menstrual cycle. FSH causes an egg to mature and LH triggers its release. Contraceptive pills use hormones to stop eggs maturing."}],"qs":[["Which neurone carries impulses from receptors?",["Sensory neurone","Motor neurone","Relay neurone","Effector"],"Sensory neurones go to the CNS."],["What is an effector?",["A muscle or gland that responds","A sense organ","A hormone","A nerve"],"Effectors carry out the response."],["Which organ releases insulin?",["Pancreas","Liver","Kidney","Thyroid"],"The pancreas monitors blood glucose."],["What does insulin do?",["Lowers blood glucose","Raises blood glucose","Raises heart rate","Controls growth"],"It helps cells take in glucose."],["How are hormones carried?",["In the blood","Along nerves","In the lymph only","Through the air"],"Endocrine glands release them into the blood."],["Type 1 diabetes is treated with…",["Insulin injections","Antibiotics","Vaccines","Painkillers"],"The body can't make enough insulin."],["What is homeostasis?",["Keeping internal conditions constant","Cell division","Digestion","Breathing out"],"e.g. body temperature at about 37°C."],["Why are reflexes fast?",["They skip conscious thought","They use hormones","They use the blood","They're slow"],"The relay neurone links straight to the motor neurone."],["Which hormone triggers egg release?",["LH","Insulin","Adrenaline","Glucagon"],"LH causes ovulation."],["SA:V ratio of a 1 cm cube?",["6:1","1:6","1:1","3:1"],"SA = 6 cm², volume = 1 cm³."]],"bonus":["Which hormone raises blood glucose?",["Glucagon","Insulin","Oestrogen","Testosterone"],"Glucagon makes the liver turn glycogen back into glucose."]}]},{"id":"chemistry","name":"Chemistry","weeks":[{"t":"Air, water & energy changes (C1)","intro":"C1 covers how Earth's atmosphere formed and changed, energy changes in reactions, climate change and making water safe to drink.","how":["The early atmosphere was mostly carbon dioxide and water vapour from volcanoes.","Oceans formed as water vapour condensed. Plants then took in CO₂ and released oxygen.","Today's air is about 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen and 0.04% CO₂.","Exothermic reactions release energy (temperature rises). Endothermic reactions take it in (temperature falls).","Potable water is made by filtration then chlorination. Seawater needs desalination."],"example":{"q":"Why did the amount of CO₂ in the atmosphere decrease over time?","steps":["CO₂ dissolved in the oceans.","Plants and algae used CO₂ in photosynthesis.","Carbon was locked into sedimentary rocks and fossil fuels.","So the CO₂ percentage fell to about 0.04%."],"a":"Oceans, photosynthesis and rocks and fossil fuels removed CO₂."},"walk":[{"h":"Greenhouse effect","b":"Greenhouse gases (CO₂, methane, water vapour) absorb infrared radiation given off by Earth, keeping it warm. Burning fossil fuels and farming have increased them, leading to global warming and climate change."},{"h":"Pollutants","b":"Burning fuels makes carbon monoxide (toxic), sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides (acid rain, breathing problems) and particulates (global dimming, lung damage)."},{"h":"Energy profiles","b":"On an energy profile diagram, exothermic reactions end lower than they start. Activation energy is the minimum energy needed for particles to react. Breaking bonds takes in energy and making bonds releases it."}],"qs":[["What is the main gas in today's atmosphere?",["Nitrogen","Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Argon"],"About 78% nitrogen."],["Where did the early atmosphere come from?",["Volcanoes","Plants","Animals","Oceans"],"Volcanic gases, mainly CO₂ and water vapour."],["What made oxygen in the atmosphere increase?",["Photosynthesis","Volcanoes","Combustion","Respiration"],"Algae and plants released oxygen."],["An exothermic reaction makes the surroundings…",["Hotter","Colder","No change","Wetter"],"Energy is released."],["Which pollutant causes acid rain?",["Sulfur dioxide","Oxygen","Nitrogen","Argon"],"SO₂ dissolves in rainwater."],["Which gas is a greenhouse gas?",["Methane","Nitrogen","Oxygen","Helium"],"Methane traps heat strongly."],["How is drinking water sterilised?",["Chlorination","Distillation only","Adding salt","Freezing"],"Chlorine kills microbes."],["What is activation energy?",["Minimum energy needed to react","Energy released","Heat of the room","Energy in bonds"],"Particles need enough energy when they collide."],["Why is carbon monoxide dangerous?",["It stops blood carrying oxygen","It causes acid rain","It smells","It's a greenhouse gas"],"It binds to haemoglobin."],["Making bonds is…",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Neither","Impossible"],"Energy is released when bonds form."]],"bonus":["What is desalination?",["Removing salt from seawater","Adding chlorine","Filtering mud","Boiling tap water"],"It uses lots of energy, e.g. distillation."]},{"t":"Atoms, the periodic table & bonding (C2, C4)","intro":"Atomic structure and bonding explain why substances behave as they do. Learn the particles in an atom, how the periodic table is arranged, and the types of bonding.","how":["Protons (+1, mass 1) and neutrons (0, mass 1) are in the nucleus. Electrons (−1) are in shells.","Atomic number = protons. Mass number = protons + neutrons.","Electron shells fill 2, 8, 8. Group number = outer electrons. Period = number of shells.","Ionic bonding: a metal gives electrons to a non-metal, forming oppositely charged ions.","Covalent bonding: non-metals share pairs of electrons."],"example":{"q":"Sodium has atomic number 11 and mass number 23. How many protons, neutrons and electrons?","steps":["Protons = atomic number = 11.","Electrons = protons in an atom = 11.","Neutrons = 23 − 11 = 12.","Electron arrangement: 2, 8, 1."],"a":"11 protons, 12 neutrons, 11 electrons"},"walk":[{"h":"Models of the atom","b":"Dalton: atoms were solid spheres. Thomson: plum pudding model with electrons in positive charge. Rutherford's gold foil experiment showed a tiny dense nucleus. Bohr: electrons in fixed shells. Chadwick later discovered the neutron."},{"h":"Groups","b":"Group 1 alkali metals get more reactive down the group and react with water to make hydrogen. Group 7 halogens get less reactive down the group. Group 0 noble gases have full shells and are unreactive."},{"h":"Structure and properties","b":"Giant ionic lattices have high melting points and conduct when molten or dissolved. Simple molecules have low melting points because forces between molecules are weak. Diamond and graphite are giant covalent. Graphite conducts because of delocalised electrons."}],"qs":[["What charge does a proton have?",["+1","−1","0","+2"],"Protons are positive."],["Mass number minus atomic number gives…",["Neutrons","Protons","Electrons","Shells"],"Mass number = protons + neutrons."],["Electron arrangement of chlorine (17)?",["2, 8, 7","2, 8, 8","2, 7, 8","8, 8, 1"],"2 + 8 + 7 = 17."],["Group 1 metals get… down the group",["More reactive","Less reactive","Unchanged","Non-metallic"],"Outer electron is further from the nucleus, so it's lost more easily."],["Why are noble gases unreactive?",["Full outer shells","They're metals","No protons","Too heavy"],"They don't need to gain or lose electrons."],["Ionic bonding happens between…",["A metal and a non-metal","Two non-metals","Two metals","Noble gases"],"Electrons are transferred."],["What is shared in a covalent bond?",["A pair of electrons","Protons","Neutrons","Ions"],"Non-metals share electrons."],["Which experiment discovered the nucleus?",["Rutherford's gold foil","Thomson's cathode ray","Dalton's spheres","Chadwick's neutron"],"Some alpha particles bounced back."],["Why does graphite conduct electricity?",["Delocalised electrons","It's ionic","It's a metal","It has no bonds"],"Each carbon has one free electron."],["Simple molecular substances have…",["Low melting points","High melting points","Giant lattices","Free ions"],"Weak forces between molecules."]],"bonus":["Sodium chloride conducts electricity when…",["Molten or dissolved","Solid","Never","Frozen"],"Ions must be free to move."]},{"t":"Metals, electrolysis & crude oil (C3)","intro":"C3 is about getting useful materials from the natural environment: extracting metals, using electrolysis and turning crude oil into fuels and plastics.","how":["Reactivity series (most to least): potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium, aluminium, (carbon), zinc, iron, copper, gold.","Metals less reactive than carbon are extracted by heating with carbon (reduction).","More reactive metals, like aluminium, are extracted by electrolysis.","In electrolysis, positive ions go to the negative electrode (cathode) and negative ions go to the anode.","Crude oil is separated by fractional distillation into fractions with different boiling points."],"example":{"q":"Why is iron extracted with carbon but aluminium by electrolysis?","steps":["Iron is less reactive than carbon.","So carbon can take oxygen from iron oxide (reduction).","Aluminium is more reactive than carbon.","So carbon can't reduce it, and electrolysis must be used."],"a":"It depends on reactivity compared with carbon."},"walk":[{"h":"Oxidation and reduction","b":"Oxidation is gain of oxygen (or loss of electrons). Reduction is loss of oxygen (or gain of electrons). Remember OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain of electrons."},{"h":"Electrolysis of solutions","b":"In aqueous solutions, hydrogen forms at the cathode if the metal is more reactive than hydrogen. At the anode, oxygen forms unless the solution contains halide ions, in which case you get the halogen, e.g. chlorine."},{"h":"Crude oil","b":"Crude oil is a mixture of hydrocarbons, mostly alkanes (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂). Longer chains have higher boiling points, are more viscous and are less flammable. Cracking breaks long chains into shorter, more useful ones plus alkenes, which make polymers."}],"qs":[["Which is most reactive?",["Potassium","Iron","Copper","Gold"],"Potassium is at the top of the series."],["Iron is extracted by…",["Heating with carbon","Electrolysis","Digging up pure iron","Distillation"],"Iron is below carbon."],["Aluminium is extracted by…",["Electrolysis","Heating with carbon","Filtering","Cracking"],"It's more reactive than carbon."],["Reduction is…",["Loss of oxygen","Gain of oxygen","Loss of protons","Burning"],"And gain of electrons."],["Which electrode do positive ions go to?",["Cathode (negative)","Anode (positive)","Both","Neither"],"Opposites attract."],["Crude oil is separated by…",["Fractional distillation","Filtration","Electrolysis","Chromatography"],"Fractions condense at different temperatures."],["General formula of alkanes?",["CₙH₂ₙ₊₂","CₙH₂ₙ","CₙHₙ","CₙH₂ₙ₋₂"],"e.g. methane CH₄."],["What does cracking produce?",["Shorter alkanes and alkenes","Crude oil","Metals","Salt"],"Long chains are broken up."],["Electrolysis of salt solution gives what at the anode?",["Chlorine","Oxygen","Sodium","Hydrogen"],"Halide ions are present."],["Long-chain hydrocarbons are…",["More viscous","More flammable","Lower boiling","Gases"],"They're thick and harder to ignite."]],"bonus":["What does OIL RIG stand for?",["Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons)","Oil Is Light, Rock Is Grey","Oxygen In, Liquid Removed In Gas","Oxidation Increases Light"],"It's about electrons."]},{"t":"Calculations & acids (C5, C6)","intro":"Chemical calculations and acids are high-mark areas. Learn relative formula mass, moles, concentration, and how acids react.","how":["Relative formula mass (Mr) = add the relative atomic masses of all atoms in the formula.","Moles = mass ÷ Mr.","Concentration (g/dm³) = mass ÷ volume in dm³. 1 dm³ = 1000 cm³.","Acid + metal → salt + hydrogen. Acid + base → salt + water. Acid + carbonate → salt + water + CO₂.","pH below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral and above 7 is alkaline. Strong acids ionise completely."],"example":{"q":"How many moles are in 22 g of CO₂? (C = 12, O = 16)","steps":["Mr of CO₂ = 12 + 16 + 16 = 44.","Moles = mass ÷ Mr.","22 ÷ 44 = 0.5.","So there are 0.5 moles."],"a":"0.5 mol"},"walk":[{"h":"Separating mixtures","b":"Filtration separates insoluble solids. Crystallisation gets a soluble salt from a solution. Distillation separates a liquid from a solution. Chromatography separates dyes. Rf = distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent."},{"h":"Making salts","b":"Hydrochloric acid makes chlorides, sulfuric acid makes sulfates and nitric acid makes nitrates. To make a pure dry salt, add excess insoluble base to acid, filter out the excess, then evaporate and crystallise."},{"h":"Strong and weak acids","b":"Strong acids (HCl) ionise completely in water. Weak acids (ethanoic acid) only partly ionise. At the same concentration, a weak acid has a higher pH and reacts more slowly. pH is a measure of the concentration of H⁺ ions."}],"qs":[["Mr of H₂O? (H = 1, O = 16)",["18","17","34","16"],"1 + 1 + 16."],["Moles in 40 g of NaOH (Mr 40)?",["1","40","0.5","1600"],"40 ÷ 40 = 1."],["How many cm³ in 1 dm³?",["1000","100","10","1"],"1 dm³ = 1000 cm³."],["20 g dissolved in 0.5 dm³. Concentration?",["40 g/dm³","10 g/dm³","20 g/dm³","0.025 g/dm³"],"20 ÷ 0.5 = 40."],["Acid + metal gives…",["Salt + hydrogen","Salt + water","Salt + CO₂","Just water"],"e.g. magnesium + HCl."],["Acid + carbonate gives…",["Salt + water + carbon dioxide","Salt + hydrogen","Only salt","Oxygen"],"It fizzes with CO₂."],["Sulfuric acid makes which salts?",["Sulfates","Chlorides","Nitrates","Carbonates"],"e.g. copper sulfate."],["pH 2 is…",["Strongly acidic","Neutral","Alkaline","Weakly alkaline"],"Low pH = acidic."],["A strong acid…",["Ionises completely","Doesn't ionise","Has pH 7","Is always concentrated"],"Strong is about ionisation, not concentration."],["Spot moves 3 cm, solvent 6 cm. Rf value?",["0.5","2","3","18"],"3 ÷ 6."]],"bonus":["Mr of CaCO₃? (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)",["100","68","84","56"],"40 + 12 + 48 = 100."]}]},{"id":"physics","name":"Physics","weeks":[{"t":"Waves, radiation & energy resources (P1, P2)","intro":"This week covers how waves behave, the electromagnetic spectrum and its risks, and how we generate electricity. Learn the wave equation by heart.","how":["Wave speed (m/s) = frequency (Hz) × wavelength (m).","Transverse waves (light, water) vibrate at right angles to travel. Longitudinal waves (sound) vibrate along the direction of travel.","EM spectrum, low to high frequency: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma.","Higher-frequency EM radiation (UV, X-rays, gamma) is ionising and can damage cells.","Renewable resources (wind, solar, hydro) won't run out. Fossil fuels release CO₂."],"example":{"q":"A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 4 m. What is its speed?","steps":["Use v = f × λ.","v = 50 × 4.","v = 200 m/s."],"a":"200 m/s"},"walk":[{"h":"Wave features","b":"Amplitude is the maximum height from the middle. Wavelength is the distance from one peak to the next. Frequency is the number of waves per second. Waves transfer energy, not matter."},{"h":"Radiation risks","b":"UV causes sunburn and skin cancer. X-rays and gamma can damage DNA and cause cancer. Doctors weigh risks against benefits, like an X-ray to find a broken bone. Radiation dose is measured in sieverts."},{"h":"Electricity generation","b":"Most power stations turn a turbine, which turns a generator. Energy is wasted as heat. Efficiency = useful output ÷ total input. The National Grid uses step-up transformers to transmit at high voltage, which reduces energy loss."}],"qs":[["Wave speed equation?",["v = f × λ","v = f ÷ λ","v = λ ÷ f","v = f + λ"],"Speed = frequency × wavelength."],["Which is a longitudinal wave?",["Sound","Light","Radio","Water ripples"],"Sound vibrates along the direction of travel."],["Which EM wave has the highest frequency?",["Gamma","Radio","Visible","Infrared"],"Gamma is at the high end."],["Which is ionising?",["X-rays","Radio","Microwaves","Infrared"],"UV, X-rays and gamma are ionising."],["What do waves transfer?",["Energy","Matter","Atoms","Water"],"The medium vibrates but doesn't travel along."],["Frequency is measured in…",["Hertz","Metres","Watts","Joules"],"1 Hz = 1 wave per second."],["Which is renewable?",["Wind","Coal","Gas","Oil"],"Wind won't run out."],["Why does the National Grid use high voltage?",["To reduce energy losses","To make it dangerous","To increase current","It's cheaper to make"],"High voltage means low current, so less heating."],["Speed of a wave with f = 10 Hz and λ = 3 m?",["30 m/s","3.3 m/s","13 m/s","0.3 m/s"],"10 × 3."],["Which EM wave is used for TV and radio?",["Radio waves","Gamma","X-rays","UV"],"Long-wavelength radio waves."]],"bonus":["A machine takes in 200 J and gives out 150 J usefully. Efficiency?",["75%","25%","133%","50%"],"150 ÷ 200 = 0.75."]},{"t":"Electric circuits (P3)","intro":"Circuits are a big part of combined physics. Learn the equations, the rules for series and parallel circuits, and how to read I-V graphs.","how":["Potential difference (V) = current (A) × resistance (Ω).","Charge (C) = current (A) × time (s).","Power (W) = potential difference × current. Energy (J) = power × time.","Series: current is the same everywhere, and voltages add up to the supply.","Parallel: voltage is the same across each branch, and currents add up to the total."],"example":{"q":"A 12 V battery drives 3 A through a lamp. What is the lamp's resistance?","steps":["Use V = I × R, so R = V ÷ I.","R = 12 ÷ 3.","R = 4 Ω."],"a":"4 Ω"},"walk":[{"h":"I-V graphs","b":"A resistor at constant temperature gives a straight line through the origin (ohmic). A filament lamp curves because it gets hotter and its resistance rises. A diode only lets current flow one way."},{"h":"Components","b":"An LDR's resistance falls as light increases, which is useful for night lights. A thermistor's resistance falls as temperature increases, which is useful for thermostats. Ammeters go in series and voltmeters go in parallel."},{"h":"Resistors together","b":"In series, total resistance = R₁ + R₂. In parallel, the total resistance is LESS than the smallest single resistor, because current has more paths to take."}],"qs":[["V = 6 V, R = 2 Ω. Current?",["3 A","12 A","4 A","0.33 A"],"I = V ÷ R."],["Power of a device with 230 V and 2 A?",["460 W","115 W","232 W","228 W"],"P = V × I."],["In a series circuit, current is…",["The same everywhere","Split between branches","Zero","Only in the battery"],"There's one path."],["In parallel, the voltage across each branch is…",["The same as the supply","Halved","Zero","Added up"],"Each branch connects across the supply."],["How is an ammeter connected?",["In series","In parallel","Either","Not at all"],"It measures the current through it."],["An LDR's resistance in bright light is…",["Low","High","Infinite","Unchanged"],"More light, less resistance."],["Why does a filament lamp's I-V graph curve?",["It heats up and resistance increases","It's a diode","Its voltage is zero","It's ohmic"],"Higher temperature raises resistance."],["Charge flowing at 2 A for 10 s?",["20 C","5 C","0.2 C","12 C"],"Q = I × t."],["Two 4 Ω resistors in series give…",["8 Ω","2 Ω","4 Ω","16 Ω"],"Add them in series."],["A diode allows current to flow…",["In one direction only","Both ways equally","Never","Only in AC"],"It has very high resistance in reverse."]],"bonus":["A 100 W bulb is on for 60 s. Energy transferred?",["6000 J","1.67 J","160 J","600 J"],"E = P × t = 100 × 60."]},{"t":"Forces & motion (P4)","intro":"P4 covers forces, describing motion with graphs, Newton's laws and energy in motion. There are lots of equations to learn by heart.","how":["Speed = distance ÷ time. Acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time.","Resultant force = mass × acceleration (F = ma).","Weight = mass × gravitational field strength (g = 10 N/kg on Earth).","Kinetic energy = ½ × mass × speed². Gravitational PE = mass × g × height.","Momentum = mass × velocity. Work done = force × distance."],"example":{"q":"A 1200 kg car accelerates at 2 m/s². What is the resultant force?","steps":["Use F = m × a.","F = 1200 × 2.","F = 2400 N."],"a":"2400 N"},"walk":[{"h":"Motion graphs","b":"On a distance-time graph the gradient is the speed, and a flat line means stopped. On a velocity-time graph the gradient is acceleration, and the area under the line is the distance travelled."},{"h":"Newton's laws","b":"First law: if forces are balanced, an object stays still or keeps moving at a steady speed. Second: F = ma. Third: when A pushes B, B pushes A back with an equal and opposite force."},{"h":"Stopping distance","b":"Stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance. Tiredness, alcohol and phones increase thinking distance. Wet or icy roads, worn tyres and higher speed increase braking distance."}],"qs":[["F = ma. Mass 5 kg, acceleration 3 m/s². Force?",["15 N","8 N","1.67 N","2 N"],"5 × 3."],["Weight of a 60 kg person (g = 10 N/kg)?",["600 N","60 N","6 N","6000 N"],"W = m × g."],["What does the gradient of a distance-time graph show?",["Speed","Acceleration","Distance","Force"],"Distance ÷ time."],["Area under a velocity-time graph gives…",["Distance travelled","Acceleration","Force","Time"],"Velocity × time = distance."],["KE of a 2 kg ball at 3 m/s?",["9 J","6 J","18 J","3 J"],"½ × 2 × 3² = 9."],["A car goes 0 to 20 m/s in 5 s. Acceleration?",["4 m/s²","100 m/s²","25 m/s²","15 m/s²"],"20 ÷ 5."],["Newton's third law says…",["Every force has an equal and opposite force","F = ma","Objects stay still","Weight = mg"],"Forces come in pairs."],["Which increases thinking distance?",["Using a phone","Icy roads","Worn tyres","Wet brakes"],"It slows reaction time."],["Momentum of a 50 kg skater at 4 m/s?",["200 kg m/s","54 kg m/s","12.5 kg m/s","400 kg m/s"],"p = m × v."],["Work done pushing with 20 N for 5 m?",["100 J","4 J","25 J","15 J"],"W = F × d."]],"bonus":["GPE gained lifting 3 kg by 2 m (g = 10 N/kg)?",["60 J","6 J","15 J","30 J"],"m × g × h = 3 × 10 × 2."]},{"t":"Radioactivity & the particle model (P5, P6)","intro":"P5 covers radioactive decay and safety. P6 uses the particle model to explain states of matter, heating and stretching. Both include calculations.","how":["Alpha = 2 protons + 2 neutrons (stopped by paper). Beta = fast electron (stopped by aluminium). Gamma = EM wave (reduced by thick lead).","Half-life is the time for half of the radioactive nuclei to decay.","Contamination means radioactive material is on or in you. Irradiation means you're exposed to radiation from outside.","Density = mass ÷ volume.","Energy to heat = mass × specific heat capacity × temperature change. Spring force = spring constant × extension."],"example":{"q":"A sample has a count rate of 800 Bq and a half-life of 2 hours. What is it after 6 hours?","steps":["6 hours is 3 half-lives.","800 → 400 (1 half-life).","400 → 200 (2).","200 → 100 (3)."],"a":"100 Bq"},"walk":[{"h":"Isotopes and decay","b":"Isotopes have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Unstable nuclei decay randomly. In alpha decay, mass number drops by 4 and atomic number by 2. In beta decay, a neutron turns into a proton, so atomic number goes up by 1."},{"h":"States of matter","b":"Solids have particles in fixed positions that vibrate. Liquids have close particles that move past each other. Gases have far-apart particles moving fast. During melting or boiling the temperature stays the same: energy breaks bonds instead. That's latent heat."},{"h":"Stretching springs","b":"F = k × x until the limit of proportionality. Elastic deformation means it returns to shape. Plastic deformation means it stays stretched. Energy stored in a spring = ½ × k × x²."}],"qs":[["Which radiation is stopped by paper?",["Alpha","Beta","Gamma","X-rays"],"Alpha is large and highly ionising."],["What is half-life?",["Time for half the nuclei to decay","Half the age of the sample","Time for all to decay","Half the mass"],"The count rate halves."],["1600 Bq, half-life 1 day. After 2 days?",["400 Bq","800 Bq","200 Bq","0 Bq"],"1600 → 800 → 400."],["Isotopes have different numbers of…",["Neutrons","Protons","Electrons","Elements"],"Same element, different mass."],["Which radiation is an EM wave?",["Gamma","Alpha","Beta","Neutron"],"Gamma has no mass or charge."],["Density of 200 g in 50 cm³?",["4 g/cm³","250 g/cm³","0.25 g/cm³","10 000 g/cm³"],"200 ÷ 50."],["During boiling, the temperature…",["Stays the same","Rises fast","Falls","Doubles"],"Energy breaks bonds between particles."],["Spring constant 50 N/m, extension 0.1 m. Force?",["5 N","500 N","0.002 N","50.1 N"],"F = k × x."],["Contamination means…",["Radioactive material is on or inside you","Being near a source","Wearing a lead apron","Using a Geiger counter"],"It keeps irradiating you."],["In which state are particles furthest apart?",["Gas","Solid","Liquid","All equal"],"Gas particles move freely."]],"bonus":["Energy to heat 2 kg of water by 10°C (c = 4200 J/kg°C)?",["84 000 J","8400 J","42 000 J","840 J"],"2 × 4200 × 10."]}]},{"id":"re-christianity","name":"RE: Christianity","group":"re","short":"Christianity","weeks":[{"t":"The nature of God, the Trinity & creation","intro":"Christians believe in one God who is all-powerful, all-loving and just. This week covers what God is like, how God can be three in one, and what the creation story teaches.","how":["Omnipotent = all-powerful. Omnibenevolent = all-loving. Just = fair, the perfect judge.","The Trinity is one God in three persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. Each is fully God.","In Genesis 1, God creates the world in six days and rests on the seventh. Humans are made 'in the image of God'.","John 1 says 'In the beginning was the Word': Jesus (the Word) was present at creation.","Learn one source of authority for each belief so you can use it in 5-mark answers."],"example":{"q":"Explain two Christian beliefs about the nature of God. (5 marks)","steps":["Belief 1: God is omnipotent and created everything from nothing.","Develop: so Christians trust God is in control, even in hard times.","Belief 2: God is omnibenevolent and shows love through Jesus.","Source: 'God is love' (1 John 4:8)."],"a":"Two developed beliefs plus a source of authority."},"walk":[{"h":"The problem of evil","b":"If God is all-powerful and all-loving, why is there suffering? Christians answer that God gave humans free will, and evil comes from misusing it (the Fall in Genesis 3). Suffering can also build character and faith, as in the story of Job."},{"h":"The Trinity","b":"The Nicene Creed says Christians believe in 'one God, the Father almighty… and in one Lord, Jesus Christ… and in the Holy Spirit'. At Jesus's baptism all three appear together: the Son is baptised, the Spirit descends like a dove and the Father speaks."},{"h":"Creation views","b":"Some Christians (creationists) read Genesis literally. Many others read it as symbolic and accept the Big Bang and evolution, believing God started and guides the process. Both agree humans have a special role as stewards of creation."}],"qs":[["What does omnipotent mean?",["All-powerful","All-loving","All-knowing","Everywhere at once"],"Omni = all, potent = powerful."],["How many persons are in the Trinity?",["Three","One","Two","Seven"],"Father, Son and Holy Spirit."],["Which book contains the creation story?",["Genesis","Exodus","Psalms","Revelation"],"Genesis 1 and 2."],["What does 'made in the image of God' suggest?",["Humans are special and have value","Humans look like God physically","Humans are gods","Animals are more important"],"It's a basis for the sanctity of life."],["At Jesus's baptism, how did the Holy Spirit appear?",["Like a dove","As fire","As a cloud","As a lamb"],"All three persons of the Trinity were present."],["What is the problem of evil?",["Why a loving, powerful God allows suffering","Why people go to church","How the Bible was written","Why there are many churches"],"It challenges belief in God."],["What is the Fall?",["Adam and Eve disobeying God in Genesis 3","Jesus's crucifixion","The flood","The end of the world"],"It brought sin into the world."],["Who is 'the Word' in John 1?",["Jesus","Moses","The Bible","The Holy Spirit"],"Present at creation."],["What does 1 John 4:8 say?",["'God is love'","'Love your enemies'","'In the beginning'","'I am the way'"],"Shows God's omnibenevolence."],["What is a steward?",["Someone who looks after creation for God","A priest","A type of angel","A creationist"],"Humans care for the world."]],"bonus":["What does it mean to call God 'just'?",["God is fair and judges rightly","God is only for some people","God is strict and cruel","God is far away"],"Justice is part of God's nature."]},{"t":"Jesus: incarnation, crucifixion, resurrection & ascension","intro":"Jesus is at the centre of Christian belief. Learn what Christians believe about his birth, death, rising from the dead and return to heaven, and why each matters.","how":["Incarnation: God became human in Jesus, born of the Virgin Mary.","Jesus is fully God and fully human: the Son of God.","Crucifixion: Jesus was executed on a cross. Christians believe he died for humanity's sins.","Resurrection: Jesus rose from the dead on the third day (Easter Sunday).","Ascension: 40 days later, Jesus returned to heaven."],"example":{"q":"Explain why the resurrection is important to Christians. (4 marks)","steps":["It shows Jesus defeated death and sin.","So Christians believe they too can have eternal life.","It proves Jesus was the Son of God.","It gives Christians hope and is celebrated every Easter."],"a":"Two developed reasons."},"walk":[{"h":"The incarnation","b":"The Gospels of Matthew and Luke describe Jesus's birth to Mary, a virgin, by the power of the Holy Spirit. John 1:14 says 'The Word became flesh'. Christians believe that in Jesus, God shared human life and suffering."},{"h":"The crucifixion","b":"Jesus was arrested, tried and crucified under the Roman governor Pontius Pilate. His words 'Father, forgive them' show forgiveness. Christians believe his death was a sacrifice that made it possible for humans to be forgiven."},{"h":"Resurrection and ascension","b":"Women including Mary Magdalene found the tomb empty. Jesus appeared to the disciples. At the ascension he told them to spread his message. Then at Pentecost the Holy Spirit came to give them courage."}],"qs":[["What does incarnation mean?",["God becoming human in Jesus","Rising from the dead","Going to heaven","Being baptised"],"God 'in the flesh'."],["Who was Jesus's mother?",["Mary","Martha","Elizabeth","Ruth"],"Christians believe she was a virgin."],["Who was the Roman governor at Jesus's trial?",["Pontius Pilate","Herod","Caesar","Caiaphas"],"He sentenced Jesus."],["On which day did Jesus rise?",["The third day","The first day","The seventh day","The fortieth day"],"Easter Sunday."],["Who first discovered the empty tomb?",["Women including Mary Magdalene","Pilate","Roman soldiers only","Peter alone"],"According to the Gospels."],["What is the ascension?",["Jesus returning to heaven","Jesus's birth","Jesus's baptism","The Last Supper"],"Forty days after Easter."],["'Father, forgive them' shows…",["Jesus's forgiveness","Jesus's anger","The resurrection","The Trinity"],"Said on the cross."],["Why do Christians believe Jesus died?",["To make forgiveness of sin possible","By accident","To start a war","To become king"],"A sacrifice for humanity."],["When did the Holy Spirit come to the disciples?",["Pentecost","Christmas","Easter","Lent"],"It gave them courage to preach."],["Jesus is believed to be…",["Fully God and fully human","Only human","Only God","An angel"],"Both natures together."]],"bonus":["Which Gospel says 'The Word became flesh'?",["John","Mark","Matthew","Luke"],"John 1:14."]},{"t":"Sin, salvation & atonement","intro":"Salvation means being saved from sin and its consequences. This week covers original sin, how Christians believe they are saved, and what Jesus's death achieved.","how":["Sin is any thought or action that separates people from God.","Original sin: humans are born with a tendency to sin, inherited from Adam and Eve.","Atonement: Jesus's death restores the relationship between God and humans.","Salvation through grace: a free gift of God's love, received through faith.","Some Christians stress good works too: faith shows itself in how you live."],"example":{"q":"Explain two ways Christians believe they can achieve salvation. (5 marks)","steps":["Way 1: through faith and God's grace.","Develop: it is a gift that can't be earned, received by believing in Jesus.","Way 2: through living a good life, following the Ten Commandments and Jesus's teaching.","Source: 'For it is by grace you have been saved, through faith' (Ephesians 2:8)."],"a":"Two developed ways plus a source."},"walk":[{"h":"Law and grace","b":"Some Christians believe salvation comes through obeying God's law: the Ten Commandments and Jesus's teaching. Others, especially many Protestants, stress that no one can be good enough, so salvation is by grace alone through faith."},{"h":"The role of the Spirit","b":"Christians believe the Holy Spirit helps them live good lives and grow in faith. Through the sacraments, such as baptism and Holy Communion, many Christians believe they receive God's grace."},{"h":"Why atonement matters","b":"Jesus is called the 'Lamb of God'. Just as lambs were once sacrificed for sins, Christians believe Jesus's death paid the price of sin once and for all, opening the way to heaven."}],"qs":[["What is sin?",["Anything that separates people from God","A church service","A prayer","A type of angel"],"Thoughts or actions against God's will."],["Original sin comes from…",["Adam and Eve's disobedience","Cain and Abel","Moses","The disciples"],"The Fall."],["What is atonement?",["Restoring the relationship between God and humans","A punishment","A festival","Confession only"],"Achieved through Jesus's death."],["What is grace?",["God's free, undeserved love","A reward for good deeds","A prayer before meals","A church"],"It can't be earned."],["Ephesians 2:8 says salvation is by grace through…",["Faith","Money","Works only","Baptism only"],"Key text for Protestants."],["Jesus is called the 'Lamb of…'",["God","Israel","Heaven","Peace"],"A sacrifice for sin."],["What does salvation mean?",["Being saved from sin and its consequences","Going to church","Becoming a priest","Reading the Bible"],"Leads to eternal life."],["Which sacrament welcomes people into the Church?",["Baptism","Marriage","Confession","Ordination"],"It washes away sin, many believe."],["Some Christians believe salvation needs faith and…",["Good works","Wealth","Fasting only","Pilgrimage"],"Faith shown in actions."],["Who helps Christians live good lives?",["The Holy Spirit","The Pope only","Angels only","Moses"],"Third person of the Trinity."]],"bonus":["Which commandments sum up the law, according to Jesus?",["Love God and love your neighbour","Do not steal and do not lie","Keep the Sabbath and honour parents","Fast and pray"],"The Great Commandments."]},{"t":"Life after death, judgement, heaven & hell","intro":"Christian beliefs about life after death shape how they live now. Learn the views on resurrection, judgement, heaven, hell and purgatory.","how":["Christians believe the soul lives on after death.","Resurrection of the body: at the end of time the dead will be raised, as Jesus was.","Judgement: God (through Jesus) will judge everyone on their faith and actions.","Heaven: eternal life with God. Hell: eternal separation from God.","Catholics also believe in purgatory, a place of cleansing before heaven."],"example":{"q":"Explain how beliefs about judgement influence Christians today. (4 marks)","steps":["Christians believe they'll be judged on how they treated others.","So they help those in need, e.g. volunteering at food banks.","The Parable of the Sheep and the Goats says helping the poor is helping Jesus.","This gives a reason to live morally every day."],"a":"Two developed influences."},"walk":[{"h":"The Sheep and the Goats","b":"In Matthew 25, Jesus describes the Son of Man separating people like sheep from goats. Those who fed the hungry, welcomed strangers and visited prisoners are rewarded: 'whatever you did for one of the least of these… you did for me'."},{"h":"Views of heaven and hell","b":"Some Christians picture heaven and hell as real places. Others see them as states of being: heaven as being with God, hell as being without God. Many today struggle to believe a loving God would send anyone to eternal punishment."},{"h":"Funerals","b":"Christian funerals express hope. Words like 'I am the resurrection and the life' (John 11:25) remind mourners that death is not the end. Prayers commend the person to God."}],"qs":[["What is the resurrection of the body?",["The dead will be raised at the end of time","Reincarnation","Becoming an angel","Ghosts"],"Like Jesus's resurrection."],["What is heaven?",["Eternal life with God","A cloud","A church","Earth"],"Reward for the faithful."],["What is hell?",["Eternal separation from God","Purgatory","A sacrament","A festival"],"Many see it as a state."],["Which Christians believe in purgatory?",["Catholics","All Protestants","Quakers","None"],"A place of cleansing."],["Which parable is about judgement?",["The Sheep and the Goats","The Prodigal Son","The Mustard Seed","The Sower"],"Matthew 25."],["'I am the resurrection and the life' is from…",["John 11:25","Genesis 1","Exodus 20","Psalm 23"],"Read at funerals."],["Who will judge, according to Christians?",["God through Jesus","The Pope","Angels","Moses"],"At the end of time."],["How do beliefs about judgement affect Christians?",["They try to live morally and help others","They stop praying","They ignore the poor","No effect"],"Actions matter."],["Some Christians see heaven and hell as…",["States of being rather than places","Myths only","The same thing","On Earth"],"With or without God."],["'Whatever you did for one of the least of these…'",["you did for me","you did for yourself","is forgotten","is a sin"],"Jesus identifies with the poor."]],"bonus":["What gives Christian funerals a tone of hope?",["Belief in resurrection and eternal life","Belief in reincarnation","Fear of hell","Nothing"],"Death is not the end."]}]},{"id":"re-islam","name":"RE: Islam","group":"re","short":"Islam","weeks":[{"t":"Tawhid, the nature of God & Sunni and Shi'a beliefs","intro":"Tawhid, the oneness of God, is the most important belief in Islam. This week also covers God's qualities and how Sunni and Shi'a Muslims differ and agree.","how":["Tawhid: God (Allah) is one and unique, with no partners. Surah 112: 'He is God, the One'.","Shirk (associating anything with God) is the greatest sin.","God's qualities include: immanent (close), transcendent (beyond the world), omnipotent, beneficent, merciful, fair and just.","Sunni Muslims follow the six articles of faith.","Shi'a Muslims follow the five roots of Usul ad-Din: Tawhid, Adalat (justice), Nubuwwah (prophethood), Imamah (Imamate), Mi'ad (Day of Judgement)."],"example":{"q":"Explain two ways belief in Tawhid influences Muslims today. (4 marks)","steps":["Muslims worship God alone, so they avoid shirk.","For example, mosques have no pictures of God or people.","They submit their whole lives to God's will.","So they pray five times a day and follow God's guidance."],"a":"Two developed influences."},"walk":[{"h":"The 99 names","b":"Muslims describe God using the 99 names, such as Ar-Rahman (the Most Compassionate) and Ar-Rahim (the Most Merciful). Every surah but one begins 'In the name of God, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful'."},{"h":"Immanent and transcendent","b":"God is transcendent: beyond and outside the universe, not limited by time or space. God is also immanent: 'closer to him than his jugular vein' (Qur'an 50:16). Both describe God's greatness."},{"h":"Sunni and Shi'a","b":"About 85–90% of Muslims are Sunni. Both share Tawhid, the Qur'an and Muhammad as the final prophet. The split began over who should lead after Muhammad: Sunnis accepted Abu Bakr as the first caliph, Shi'a believe Ali was chosen."}],"qs":[["What is Tawhid?",["The oneness of God","Prayer","Fasting","Charity"],"The central belief of Islam."],["What is shirk?",["Associating anything with God","A type of prayer","Pilgrimage","A holy book"],"The greatest sin."],["Which surah says 'He is God, the One'?",["Surah 112","Surah 1","Surah 2","Surah 36"],"Al-Ikhlas."],["Transcendent means…",["Beyond and outside the universe","Very close","All-loving","Fair"],"Not limited by time or space."],["God is 'closer than the jugular vein' shows God is…",["Immanent","Transcendent","Just","Unknown"],"Qur'an 50:16."],["Ar-Rahim means…",["The Most Merciful","The Creator","The Judge","The King"],"One of the 99 names."],["Which group follows the six articles of faith?",["Sunni Muslims","Shi'a Muslims","Sufis only","Christians"],"Shi'a follow Usul ad-Din."],["Adalat means…",["God's justice","Prayer","Prophethood","The Imamate"],"A root of Usul ad-Din."],["Who did Sunnis accept as the first caliph?",["Abu Bakr","Ali","Umar","Husayn"],"Muhammad's close companion."],["Roughly what share of Muslims are Sunni?",["85–90%","10%","50%","25%"],"The majority."]],"bonus":["Why do mosques contain no images of God?",["To avoid shirk and because God can't be pictured","To save money","Because of fashion","Images aren't allowed in any building"],"Nothing can be compared to God."]},{"t":"Angels, predestination & life after death","intro":"Muslims believe in angels, God's decree (Al-Qadr) and the afterlife (Akhirah). These beliefs shape how Muslims see responsibility and the Day of Judgement.","how":["Angels (mala'ikah) are made from light, have no free will and always obey God.","Jibril brought revelation. Mika'il brings nourishment and rain. Israfil will blow the trumpet on the Last Day. Izra'il is the angel of death.","Two recording angels note every good and bad deed.","Al-Qadr: God knows and decides everything. Humans still have free will and are responsible for their choices.","Akhirah: after death, the Day of Judgement, then paradise (Jannah) or hell (Jahannam)."],"example":{"q":"Explain how predestination and free will can both be true for Muslims.","steps":["God knows everything that will happen, as God is beyond time.","But knowing is not forcing: humans still choose freely.","Sunnis stress God's will; Shi'a stress that God, being just, gives free will.","So humans are judged fairly for their choices."],"a":"God's knowledge and human choice exist together."},"walk":[{"h":"Barzakh and the grave","b":"After death the soul waits in barzakh, a state between death and the Day of Judgement. Many Muslims believe two angels question the person in the grave about their faith."},{"h":"The Day of Judgement","b":"Israfil blows the trumpet, the dead are raised, and each person is given their book of deeds. Those who receive it in their right hand go to paradise. Jannah is described as a garden of peace. Jahannam is a place of punishment."},{"h":"Why it matters","b":"Belief in the afterlife gives life a purpose and makes Muslims responsible for their actions. It encourages prayer, charity and honesty, and gives hope that injustice in this life will be put right."}],"qs":[["Angels are made from…",["Light","Clay","Fire","Water"],"Humans from clay, jinn from fire."],["Which angel brought the Qur'an to Muhammad?",["Jibril","Mika'il","Israfil","Izra'il"],"Gabriel."],["Which angel will blow the trumpet on the Last Day?",["Israfil","Jibril","Mika'il","Izra'il"],"It starts the resurrection."],["What is Al-Qadr?",["God's predestination","The Day of Judgement","Paradise","Charity"],"God's decree."],["Do angels have free will?",["No","Yes","Only Jibril","Sometimes"],"They always obey God."],["What is Akhirah?",["Life after death","A prayer","The mosque","A pilgrimage"],"Belief in the afterlife."],["What is barzakh?",["The state between death and judgement","Hell","Paradise","A festival"],"A waiting state."],["Jannah is…",["Paradise","Hell","The grave","An angel"],"A garden of peace."],["What do the recording angels do?",["Record every good and bad deed","Bring rain","Take souls","Guard Makkah"],"Used on the Day of Judgement."],["Why are humans judged fairly?",["They have free will","Everything is random","God doesn't know the future","Angels decide"],"Responsibility needs choice."]],"bonus":["Receiving your book of deeds in the right hand means…",["Going to paradise","Going to hell","Being reincarnated","Becoming an angel"],"A sign of a good life."]},{"t":"Prophethood: Adam, Ibrahim, Muhammad & the Imamate","intro":"Risalah is God's communication through prophets. Learn why prophets matter and the stories of Adam, Ibrahim and Muhammad, and what Shi'a Muslims believe about the Imamate.","how":["Risalah (prophethood): God sent prophets to every people to guide them.","Adam was the first human and first prophet. God taught him the names of things.","Ibrahim rejected idol worship and, with Isma'il, rebuilt the Ka'aba in Makkah.","Muhammad is the final prophet, the 'Seal of the Prophets'. He received the Qur'an over 23 years.","Imamate: Shi'a believe God chose Imams, starting with Ali, to lead after Muhammad."],"example":{"q":"Explain why Ibrahim is important to Muslims. (4 marks)","steps":["He rejected idols and believed in one God, showing Tawhid.","He was willing to sacrifice his son, showing total submission.","He and Isma'il rebuilt the Ka'aba.","Muslims remember him on Hajj and at Id-ul-Adha."],"a":"Two developed reasons."},"walk":[{"h":"Adam and Iblis","b":"God told the angels to bow to Adam. Iblis refused out of pride and was cast out, becoming Shaytan, who tempts humans. Adam and Hawwa were tempted, but they repented and God forgave them. Islam doesn't teach original sin."},{"h":"Muhammad","b":"Muhammad was born in Makkah in 570 CE. In 610 CE, in the Cave of Hira, Jibril revealed the first words of the Qur'an: 'Read!'. He moved to Madinah in 622 CE (the Hijrah) and later returned to Makkah peacefully."},{"h":"The Imamate","b":"Shi'a believe Muhammad named Ali as his successor at Ghadir Khumm. The Imams are seen as sinless guides. Twelver Shi'a believe the twelfth Imam, al-Mahdi, is in hiding and will return."}],"qs":[["What is Risalah?",["Prophethood","Prayer","Charity","Fasting"],"God's messengers."],["Who was the first prophet?",["Adam","Ibrahim","Musa","Nuh"],"Also the first human."],["Who refused to bow to Adam?",["Iblis","Jibril","Mika'il","Hawwa"],"Out of pride."],["Who rebuilt the Ka'aba?",["Ibrahim and Isma'il","Muhammad and Ali","Adam and Hawwa","Musa and Harun"],"In Makkah."],["Where did Muhammad receive the first revelation?",["Cave of Hira","Madinah","Jerusalem","Mount Sinai"],"In 610 CE."],["What was the Hijrah?",["Muhammad's move to Madinah","The first revelation","A battle","The pilgrimage"],"622 CE, start of the Islamic calendar."],["Muhammad is called the…",["Seal of the Prophets","First prophet","Imam","Caliph"],"The final prophet."],["Shi'a believe the first Imam was…",["Ali","Abu Bakr","Umar","Husayn"],"Named at Ghadir Khumm."],["Does Islam teach original sin?",["No","Yes","Only Shi'a","Only Sunni"],"Adam was forgiven."],["Which festival remembers Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son?",["Id-ul-Adha","Id-ul-Fitr","Ashura","Ramadan"],"During Hajj."]],"bonus":["Who do Twelver Shi'a believe will return?",["The twelfth Imam, al-Mahdi","Ali","Ibrahim","Jibril"],"He is in hiding (occultation)."]},{"t":"Holy books & sources of authority","intro":"Muslims believe God revealed several holy books, with the Qur'an as the final, perfect revelation. This week also covers how the Qur'an is used and other sources of authority.","how":["The Qur'an was revealed to Muhammad by Jibril, in Arabic, and is believed to be God's exact words.","Other holy books: Tawrat (Torah, to Musa), Zabur (Psalms, to Dawud), Injil (Gospel, to Isa), Sahifah (scrolls of Ibrahim).","Muslims believe the earlier books were changed or lost over time. The Qur'an remains unchanged.","The Sunnah (Muhammad's example) and Hadith (his recorded sayings) are also sources of authority.","Shari'ah law is based on the Qur'an and Sunnah."],"example":{"q":"Explain why the Qur'an is so important to Muslims. (4 marks)","steps":["It is believed to be the exact words of God.","So it is treated with great respect: kept high, read after washing.","It gives guidance on every part of life.","It is recited in Arabic in every daily prayer."],"a":"Two developed reasons."},"walk":[{"h":"How the Qur'an was compiled","b":"Muhammad's companions memorised and wrote down the revelations. Under the caliph Uthman, an official copy was made so that all versions were the same. Many Muslims memorise the whole Qur'an and are called hafiz."},{"h":"Respect for the Qur'an","b":"Muslims wash before touching it, keep it on a high shelf, and often place it on a stand (a kursi). It is read in Arabic because translations are seen as interpretations, not the Qur'an itself."},{"h":"Using authority today","b":"When the Qur'an doesn't directly answer a modern issue, scholars use the Hadith, consensus (ijma) and reasoning by comparison (qiyas). Shi'a Muslims also follow the teachings of the Imams and their scholars."}],"qs":[["In what language was the Qur'an revealed?",["Arabic","Hebrew","Greek","Urdu"],"Recited in Arabic in prayer."],["The Tawrat was given to…",["Musa","Isa","Dawud","Ibrahim"],"The Torah."],["The Injil was given to…",["Isa","Musa","Dawud","Adam"],"The Gospel."],["The Zabur was given to…",["Dawud","Musa","Isa","Ibrahim"],"The Psalms."],["What is the Sunnah?",["Muhammad's example and way of life","A holy book","A prayer","A mosque"],"A source of authority."],["What is a hafiz?",["Someone who has memorised the whole Qur'an","A judge","A prayer leader","An angel"],"A great honour."],["Which caliph had an official copy of the Qur'an made?",["Uthman","Abu Bakr","Ali","Umar"],"To keep it uniform."],["Shari'ah law is based on…",["The Qur'an and Sunnah","Government law only","The Torah only","Local custom"],"Islamic law."],["Why do Muslims believe earlier books aren't reliable now?",["They were changed or lost over time","They were never revealed","They're in the wrong language","They're too long"],"The Qur'an is unchanged."],["What do Muslims do before touching the Qur'an?",["Wash","Fast","Give charity","Travel"],"A sign of respect."]],"bonus":["What is ijma?",["Consensus of scholars","A prayer","A pilgrimage","A festival"],"Used for modern questions."]}]},{"id":"re-judaism","name":"RE: Judaism","group":"re","short":"Judaism","weeks":[{"t":"The nature of God: one, creator, lawgiver & judge","intro":"Jews believe in one God who created the world, gave the law and judges fairly. Learn the Shema and how God's presence (Shekhinah) is understood.","how":["God is one: the Shema says 'Hear O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One' (Deuteronomy 6:4).","Many Jews write G-d instead of 'God' to show respect for God's name.","God as creator: God created the world in six days and rested on the seventh (Shabbat).","God as lawgiver: God gave the 613 mitzvot (commandments), including the Ten Commandments.","God as judge: God judges people fairly but with mercy, especially at Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur."],"example":{"q":"Explain two Jewish beliefs about God. (5 marks)","steps":["Belief 1: God is One and indivisible.","Develop: so Jews worship only God, and recite the Shema every morning and evening.","Belief 2: God is the lawgiver who gave the Torah.","Develop: so Jews follow the mitzvot. Source: Exodus 20 (the Ten Commandments)."],"a":"Two developed beliefs plus a source."},"walk":[{"h":"The Shekhinah","b":"The Shekhinah is God's divine presence on Earth. In the Tenakh it was seen in the pillar of cloud and fire that led the Israelites, and it filled the Temple in Jerusalem. Many Jews believe it is present when people pray together."},{"h":"Creation and evil","b":"Genesis teaches that God created a good world. Humans have free will and both a good inclination (yetzer hatov) and a bad inclination (yetzer hara). Evil comes from humans misusing free will."},{"h":"God as sustainer","b":"Jews believe God didn't just create the world but continues to keep it going. This is why Jews say blessings over food and everyday events, thanking God as the source of everything."}],"qs":[["What does the Shema declare?",["God is One","God is three","There are many gods","God is unknown"],"Deuteronomy 6:4."],["Why do some Jews write G-d?",["To show respect for God's name","To save ink","It's a spelling rule","To hide their faith"],"God's name is holy."],["How many mitzvot are in the Torah?",["613","10","99","1000"],"Including the Ten Commandments."],["What is the Shekhinah?",["God's divine presence","A prayer","A festival","A holy book"],"Seen in cloud and fire."],["What day of rest recalls creation?",["Shabbat","Passover","Sukkot","Purim"],"God rested on the seventh day."],["Yetzer hara is…",["The bad inclination","The good inclination","A prayer","The Messiah"],"Humans can choose to resist it."],["When does God especially judge, in Jewish belief?",["Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur","Passover","Hanukkah","Purim"],"The High Holy Days."],["Where are the Ten Commandments found?",["Exodus 20","Genesis 1","Psalm 23","Ruth 1"],"Given at Sinai."],["Why do Jews say blessings over food?",["God sustains everything","It's the law of the land","To start a meal quickly","For luck"],"God as sustainer."],["The pillar of cloud and fire was a sign of…",["The Shekhinah","Evil","The Messiah","Free will"],"Leading the Israelites."]],"bonus":["What is yetzer hatov?",["The good inclination","The bad inclination","God's name","A synagogue"],"Humans have both."]},{"t":"Life after death & the Messiah","intro":"Judaism focuses more on this life than the next, so Jews hold a range of views about the afterlife. Beliefs about the Messiah also vary between Orthodox and Reform Jews.","how":["Judaism teaches that how you live now matters most.","Many Jews believe in Olam Ha-Ba (the world to come) and the resurrection of the dead.","Some believe in Gan Eden (paradise) and Sheol, or a time of purification (Gehinnom).","The Messiah (Mashiach) is an anointed leader descended from King David who will bring peace.","Orthodox Jews expect a personal Messiah. Many Reform Jews hope for a Messianic Age of peace instead."],"example":{"q":"Explain two Jewish beliefs about the Messiah. (5 marks)","steps":["Belief 1: the Messiah will be a human leader descended from King David.","Develop: he will rebuild the Temple and gather Jews to Israel.","Belief 2: some Reform Jews believe in a Messianic Age instead of a person.","Source: Isaiah 2:4: 'Nation will not take up sword against nation'."],"a":"Two developed beliefs plus a source."},"walk":[{"h":"Judgement","b":"Many Jews believe God judges people after death based on their actions and faith. Gehinnom is often seen as a place of cleansing for up to 12 months, not eternal punishment. That is one reason mourners say the Kaddish prayer for 11 months."},{"h":"Death and mourning","b":"Burial happens quickly, ideally within 24 hours. The family sits shiva for seven days of intense mourning. Life is to be cherished, and the focus is on honouring the dead and supporting the living."},{"h":"The Messianic Age","b":"The Messianic Age will be a time of peace, when all people recognise God, Jews return to Israel, and the Temple is rebuilt. Jews don't believe Jesus was the Messiah, because the world isn't yet at peace."}],"qs":[["What is Olam Ha-Ba?",["The world to come","The Messiah","A festival","A prayer"],"The afterlife."],["The Messiah will be descended from…",["King David","Moses","Abraham","Noah"],"A key belief."],["What does Judaism focus on most?",["Living well in this life","Only the afterlife","Wealth","Fasting"],"Actions now matter."],["What is shiva?",["Seven days of mourning","A funeral prayer","A festival","Paradise"],"The family stays at home."],["How long is Gehinnom often believed to last?",["Up to 12 months","Forever","One day","100 years"],"A place of purification."],["Why don't Jews accept Jesus as the Messiah?",["The world isn't yet at peace","He wasn't Jewish","He lived too long ago","He wasn't from Bethlehem"],"The Messianic Age hasn't come."],["Many Reform Jews hope for…",["A Messianic Age of peace","A warrior king","Nothing at all","Reincarnation"],"Rather than a person."],["Isaiah 2:4 describes…",["Peace between nations","Creation","The Ten Commandments","The Exodus"],"'Nation will not take up sword against nation.'"],["How soon do Jews try to bury the dead?",["Ideally within 24 hours","After a week","After a month","Any time"],"Out of respect."],["What prayer do mourners say?",["Kaddish","Shema","Amidah","Kiddush"],"It praises God."]],"bonus":["What will the Messiah rebuild, according to Orthodox Jews?",["The Temple in Jerusalem","Noah's ark","The Tower of Babel","Mount Sinai"],"Destroyed in 70 CE."]},{"t":"Covenants: Abraham, Moses & the Promised Land","intro":"A covenant is a two-way agreement between God and the Jewish people. Learn the covenants with Abraham and at Sinai, the Exodus, and the importance of the Promised Land.","how":["Covenant with Abraham: God promised land, many descendants and blessing. Abraham promised faithfulness.","The sign of Abraham's covenant is circumcision (brit milah) for baby boys.","The Exodus: Moses led the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt, remembered at Pesach (Passover).","Covenant at Sinai: God gave Moses the Ten Commandments and the Torah.","The Promised Land (Israel) was promised to Abraham's descendants."],"example":{"q":"Explain why the covenant at Sinai is important to Jews. (4 marks)","steps":["God gave the Ten Commandments and the Torah.","So Jews have God's law to guide every part of life.","It made the Israelites God's chosen people with a special duty.","Jews celebrate it at the festival of Shavuot."],"a":"Two developed reasons."},"walk":[{"h":"Abraham","b":"God called Abram to leave his home and go to a new land (Genesis 12). God promised to make him a great nation and changed his name to Abraham, 'father of many'. Abraham is seen as the father of the Jewish people."},{"h":"Moses and the Exodus","b":"The Israelites were slaves in Egypt. God sent ten plagues. In the last, the angel of death 'passed over' Jewish homes marked with lamb's blood. Moses led the people across the Red Sea. At the Pesach seder meal, families retell the story."},{"h":"The Ten Commandments","b":"The first commandments are about God: no other gods, no idols, don't misuse God's name, keep Shabbat. The others are about people: honour your parents, do not murder, commit adultery, steal, lie or covet."}],"qs":[["What is a covenant?",["An agreement between God and people","A prayer","A festival","A synagogue"],"A two-way promise."],["What was promised to Abraham?",["Land, descendants and blessing","Wealth only","Eternal life","A temple"],"Genesis 12."],["What is the sign of Abraham's covenant?",["Circumcision (brit milah)","Baptism","A ring","Fasting"],"For baby boys at eight days old."],["Who led the Israelites out of Egypt?",["Moses","Abraham","David","Noah"],"The Exodus."],["Which festival remembers the Exodus?",["Pesach (Passover)","Hanukkah","Purim","Sukkot"],"With a seder meal."],["Where did Moses receive the Ten Commandments?",["Mount Sinai","Mount Zion","Jerusalem","Egypt"],"The covenant at Sinai."],["Which festival celebrates the giving of the Torah?",["Shavuot","Pesach","Yom Kippur","Rosh Hashanah"],"Seven weeks after Pesach."],["What does 'Abraham' mean?",["Father of many","Chosen one","King","Prophet"],"His name was changed from Abram."],["Which commandment is about God?",["Have no other gods","Do not steal","Do not lie","Honour your parents"],"The first commandments are about God."],["The Promised Land is…",["Israel (Canaan)","Egypt","Babylon","Rome"],"Promised to Abraham's descendants."]],"bonus":["Why is it called 'Passover'?",["The angel of death passed over Jewish homes","They passed over the Red Sea","They passed over a meal","Moses passed away"],"The tenth plague."]},{"t":"Moral principles, sanctity of life & mitzvot","intro":"Jewish moral principles include justice, healing the world and kindness. This week also covers the sanctity of life, pikuach nefesh, free will and the mitzvot.","how":["Justice (tzedek): bringing about what is fair. Tzedakah (charity) is seen as a duty.","Tikkun olam: healing or repairing the world through good actions.","Gemilut hasadim: acts of loving kindness.","Sanctity of life: life is holy because God created it. Only God should decide when life ends.","Pikuach nefesh: saving a life overrides almost every other mitzvah, including Shabbat laws."],"example":{"q":"Explain how pikuach nefesh affects Jewish practice. (4 marks)","steps":["Saving a life is more important than keeping most commandments.","So a Jewish doctor can work on Shabbat to save a patient.","Jews can break food laws if needed to survive.","This shows how highly Judaism values human life."],"a":"Two developed examples."},"walk":[{"h":"The mitzvot","b":"There are 613 mitzvot: 248 positive ('do') and 365 negative ('don't'). They cover worship, food (kashrut), relationships and how to treat others. Following them is how Jews keep their side of the covenant."},{"h":"Free will","b":"God gave humans free will to choose between good and evil. The mitzvot guide those choices. Jews believe they will be judged on how they used their free will, which is why Yom Kippur focuses on repentance."},{"h":"Tzedakah and tikkun olam","b":"Many Jews give at least 10% of their income to charity. Maimonides described levels of charity, the highest being helping someone become self-sufficient. Tikkun olam inspires work on poverty, the environment and justice."}],"qs":[["What is tikkun olam?",["Repairing or healing the world","A prayer","A festival","The Torah"],"Making the world better."],["What is tzedakah?",["Charity as a duty of justice","A meal","A synagogue","A prayer shawl"],"Giving to those in need."],["What is pikuach nefesh?",["Saving a life overrides other laws","Keeping Shabbat","Eating kosher food","Prayer"],"Life comes first."],["Gemilut hasadim means…",["Acts of loving kindness","Food laws","Day of rest","Judgement"],"Helping others without expecting reward."],["How many mitzvot are positive ('do')?",["248","365","613","10"],"And 365 negative."],["Can a Jewish doctor work on Shabbat to save a life?",["Yes","No","Only on Friday","Only if paid"],"Pikuach nefesh."],["What are kashrut laws about?",["Food","Clothes","Prayer times","Marriage"],"Kosher food."],["Sanctity of life means…",["Life is holy because God created it","Life is short","Only Jews matter","Life is cheap"],"Only God should take life."],["What did Maimonides say is the highest level of charity?",["Helping someone become self-sufficient","Giving anonymously once","Giving reluctantly","Giving only to family"],"Eight levels of giving."],["Which day focuses on repentance?",["Yom Kippur","Pesach","Purim","Shavuot"],"The Day of Atonement."]],"bonus":["Why does free will matter for judgement?",["People are responsible for choices they freely make","It doesn't","God decides everything","Only angels have free will"],"The mitzvot guide good choices."]}]},{"id":"re-buddhism","name":"RE: Buddhism","group":"re","short":"Buddhism","weeks":[{"t":"The life of the Buddha","intro":"Buddhism began with Siddhartha Gautama, a prince who gave up luxury to find the end of suffering. Learn the key events of his life and why each one matters to Buddhists.","how":["Birth: Queen Maya dreamed of a white elephant. A wise man predicted Siddhartha would be a great king or a great holy man.","His father kept him in luxury in the palace to stop him seeing suffering.","The Four Sights: an old man, a sick man, a dead man and a holy man.","He left the palace and lived as an ascetic, nearly starving himself, but this didn't work.","He meditated under the Bodhi tree and gained enlightenment, becoming the Buddha ('awakened one')."],"example":{"q":"Explain why the Four Sights are important to Buddhists. (4 marks)","steps":["They showed Siddhartha that everyone faces old age, sickness and death.","This is the reality of suffering (dukkha).","The holy man showed there might be a way to find peace.","So they started his search for enlightenment, which Buddhists follow today."],"a":"Two developed reasons."},"walk":[{"h":"The Middle Way","b":"Siddhartha tried extreme luxury and extreme asceticism (fasting, self-denial), and neither brought peace. He realised the answer lies in the Middle Way between them, which became central to Buddhist practice."},{"h":"Enlightenment","b":"Sitting under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya, he meditated through the night. He resisted Mara, who tempted him with fear and desire. At dawn he understood the truth about suffering and how to end it."},{"h":"Teaching","b":"The Buddha gave his first sermon at Deer Park in Sarnath, teaching the Four Noble Truths. He taught for about 45 years, founding the sangha, the community of monks, nuns and followers."}],"qs":[["What was the Buddha's name before enlightenment?",["Siddhartha Gautama","Ananda","Mara","Ashoka"],"A prince of the Shakya clan."],["What did Queen Maya dream of?",["A white elephant","A lotus","A tiger","A star"],"Before Siddhartha's birth."],["Which was NOT one of the Four Sights?",["A rich merchant","An old man","A sick man","A dead man"],"The fourth was a holy man."],["What is an ascetic?",["Someone who gives up comforts to seek spiritual truth","A king","A merchant","A soldier"],"Siddhartha nearly starved."],["Under which tree did he gain enlightenment?",["The Bodhi tree","An oak tree","A palm tree","A fig in the palace"],"At Bodh Gaya."],["What does 'Buddha' mean?",["Awakened or enlightened one","King","Teacher","Monk"],"He woke up to the truth."],["Who tempted Siddhartha during his meditation?",["Mara","Ananda","Maya","Brahma"],"A demon of desire and fear."],["What is the Middle Way?",["Avoiding the extremes of luxury and self-denial","Living in a city","Following the king","Fasting completely"],"Balance."],["Where was the first sermon?",["Deer Park, Sarnath","Bodh Gaya","The palace","Lumbini"],"He taught the Four Noble Truths."],["What is the sangha?",["The Buddhist community","A meditation","A temple","A holy book"],"One of the Three Refuges."]],"bonus":["Why did Siddhartha's father keep him in the palace?",["To stop him seeing suffering and becoming a holy man","Because he was ill","To train him as a monk","To hide him from enemies"],"Due to the prediction."]},{"t":"Dhamma, dependent arising & the three marks of existence","intro":"The Dhamma is the Buddha's teaching about how things really are. Learn dependent arising (paticcasamuppada) and the three marks of existence: dukkha, anicca and anatta.","how":["Dhamma: the Buddha's teaching and the truth about life. It's one of the Three Refuges.","Dependent arising: everything depends on other things to exist and changes as they change.","Dukkha: suffering or unsatisfactoriness, which is part of all life.","Anicca: impermanence. Everything changes.","Anatta: no fixed, unchanging self or soul."],"example":{"q":"Explain how the story of Kisa Gotami teaches anicca and dukkha.","steps":["Kisa Gotami's baby died and she begged the Buddha to bring him back.","He asked for a mustard seed from a house where no one had died.","She couldn't find one: every family had known death.","She understood that death and change (anicca) come to all, and suffering (dukkha) is universal."],"a":"Everyone faces loss, because nothing lasts."},"walk":[{"h":"The Tibetan Wheel of Life","b":"The Wheel of Life shows dependent arising. In the centre, the three poisons (a pig for ignorance, a cockerel for greed, a snake for hatred) keep the wheel turning. The outer ring shows 12 links that keep beings trapped in samsara."},{"h":"Anatta and the chariot","b":"The monk Nagasena explained anatta to King Milinda: a chariot is just wheels, axle and frame put together. No single part is 'the chariot'. In the same way a person is made of the five aggregates (skandhas): form, sensation, perception, mental formations and consciousness."},{"h":"Three types of dukkha","b":"Ordinary suffering (pain, illness). Suffering from change (happy times end). Suffering from attachment to an unchanging self. Understanding this helps Buddhists let go of craving."}],"qs":[["What is the Dhamma?",["The Buddha's teaching","A temple","A monk","A festival"],"One of the Three Refuges."],["What does anicca mean?",["Impermanence","Suffering","No self","Enlightenment"],"Everything changes."],["What does anatta mean?",["No fixed self","Suffering","Change","Rebirth"],"No permanent soul."],["What is dukkha?",["Suffering or unsatisfactoriness","Happiness","Meditation","A monk"],"The first mark."],["What did the Buddha ask Kisa Gotami to find?",["A mustard seed from a house where no one had died","A lotus","A white elephant","A golden bowl"],"She found none."],["Which animal stands for greed on the Wheel of Life?",["Cockerel","Pig","Snake","Tiger"],"Pig = ignorance, snake = hatred."],["What does dependent arising teach?",["Everything depends on other things to exist","God created everything","Nothing changes","Only humans exist"],"Paticcasamuppada."],["Who explained anatta using a chariot?",["Nagasena","Ananda","Mara","Siddhartha's father"],"To King Milinda."],["How many aggregates (skandhas) make up a person?",["Five","Three","Eight","Twelve"],"Form, sensation, perception, formations, consciousness."],["What are the three poisons?",["Ignorance, greed and hatred","Fear, anger and pride","Hunger, thirst and pain","Old age, sickness and death"],"They keep the wheel turning."]],"bonus":["What is samsara?",["The cycle of birth, death and rebirth","Enlightenment","A temple","Meditation"],"Buddhists aim to escape it."]},{"t":"The Four Noble Truths & the Eightfold Path","intro":"The Four Noble Truths are the heart of the Buddha's teaching, like a doctor's diagnosis and cure. The fourth truth is the Eightfold Path, a practical guide to living.","how":["1. Dukkha: life involves suffering.","2. Samudaya: suffering is caused by craving (tanha).","3. Nirodha: suffering can end. This is nibbana (nirvana).","4. Magga: the way to end suffering is the Eightfold Path.","The Path has three parts: wisdom (right view, right intention), ethics (right speech, action, livelihood) and meditation (right effort, mindfulness, concentration)."],"example":{"q":"Explain how the Four Noble Truths are like a doctor treating an illness.","steps":["Diagnosis: there is suffering (dukkha).","Cause: craving (tanha).","Prognosis: it can be cured (nirodha).","Treatment: follow the Eightfold Path (magga)."],"a":"Illness, cause, cure possible, medicine."},"walk":[{"h":"Three types of craving","b":"Craving for sensual pleasures, craving to exist or become something, and craving not to exist (to escape). All of these cause suffering because what we crave never lasts."},{"h":"Nibbana","b":"Nibbana means 'blowing out', like a candle: the fires of greed, hatred and ignorance are extinguished. Some see it as a state of peace reached in this life; others as a release from rebirth after death."},{"h":"Using the Path","b":"Right speech means no lying or gossip. Right livelihood means a job that doesn't harm others, so not selling weapons. Right mindfulness means being fully aware of the present moment. The parts are practised together, not one after another."}],"qs":[["What is the first Noble Truth?",["Life involves suffering (dukkha)","Craving causes suffering","Suffering can end","Follow the Path"],"The diagnosis."],["What causes suffering, according to the second truth?",["Craving (tanha)","God","Bad luck","Other people"],"Samudaya."],["What is nibbana?",["The end of suffering","A type of craving","A god","A temple"],"'Blowing out'."],["How many parts are in the Eightfold Path?",["Eight","Four","Three","Ten"],"The fourth Noble Truth."],["Which part of the Path is ethics?",["Right speech","Right view","Right concentration","Right mindfulness"],"With action and livelihood."],["Which part belongs to wisdom?",["Right intention","Right speech","Right effort","Right action"],"With right view."],["Right livelihood means…",["A job that doesn't harm others","Earning lots of money","Never working","Being a monk"],"e.g. not selling weapons."],["What is the fourth Noble Truth called?",["Magga","Dukkha","Nirodha","Samudaya"],"The Path."],["Which is a type of craving?",["Craving for sensual pleasure","Craving for wisdom","Craving for meditation","Craving for the Dhamma"],"One of three."],["The Four Noble Truths were first taught at…",["Sarnath","Bodh Gaya","Lumbini","Rome"],"The first sermon."]],"bonus":["Right mindfulness means…",["Being fully aware of the present moment","Thinking about the future","Remembering the past","Ignoring feelings"],"A meditation part of the Path."]},{"t":"Theravada, Mahayana & Pure Land Buddhism","intro":"Buddhism has different traditions. Learn the main ideas of Theravada and Mahayana, the arhat and bodhisattva ideals, Buddha-nature, sunyata, and Pure Land Buddhism.","how":["Theravada ('way of the elders'): found in Sri Lanka, Thailand and Myanmar. Uses the Pali Canon.","Theravada goal: become an arhat, a perfected person who has reached nibbana.","Mahayana ('great vehicle'): found in China, Japan, Korea and Tibet.","Mahayana goal: become a bodhisattva, who delays full nibbana to help all beings.","Pure Land Buddhism: devotion to Amitabha Buddha, hoping for rebirth in his Pure Land."],"example":{"q":"Explain the difference between an arhat and a bodhisattva. (4 marks)","steps":["An arhat has reached enlightenment through their own effort and won't be reborn.","This is the Theravada ideal.","A bodhisattva vows to help all beings reach enlightenment first.","This is the Mahayana ideal, based on compassion (karuna)."],"a":"Personal liberation vs helping all beings."},"walk":[{"h":"Sunyata","b":"Sunyata means emptiness. Mahayana Buddhists teach that nothing has a fixed, independent existence: everything is 'empty' of separate self because it depends on other things. This develops the idea of anatta."},{"h":"Buddha-nature","b":"Many Mahayana Buddhists believe every being has Buddha-nature: the potential to become a Buddha. Enlightenment is uncovering what is already there, like cleaning a dusty mirror."},{"h":"Pure Land","b":"Pure Land Buddhists believe Amitabha Buddha created a Pure Land where it is easier to reach enlightenment. They chant 'Namu Amida Butsu' (I take refuge in Amitabha Buddha), trusting in his compassion rather than only their own effort."}],"qs":[["Which tradition aims to become an arhat?",["Theravada","Mahayana","Pure Land","Zen"],"'Way of the elders'."],["What is a bodhisattva?",["Someone who delays nibbana to help others","A monk who never speaks","A god","A Buddhist king"],"Mahayana ideal."],["What does sunyata mean?",["Emptiness","Suffering","Compassion","Rebirth"],"Nothing has separate existence."],["Theravada uses which scriptures?",["The Pali Canon","The Bible","The Vedas","The Qur'an"],"Oldest Buddhist texts."],["Which Buddha is central to Pure Land Buddhism?",["Amitabha","Maitreya","Siddhartha only","Kuan Yin"],"Amida in Japanese."],["What is Buddha-nature?",["The potential in all beings to become a Buddha","A statue","A temple","A meditation"],"Mahayana belief."],["Where is Theravada mainly found?",["Sri Lanka and Thailand","Japan and China","Tibet only","India only"],"Southern Buddhism."],["Mahayana means…",["Great vehicle","Way of the elders","Pure land","Emptiness"],"Northern Buddhism."],["What is karuna?",["Compassion","Wisdom","Emptiness","Craving"],"Central to the bodhisattva path."],["Pure Land Buddhists chant…",["'Namu Amida Butsu'","'Om mani padme hum' only","The Shema","The Lord's Prayer"],"Trusting Amitabha's compassion."]],"bonus":["Which tradition stresses personal effort to reach nibbana in this life?",["Theravada","Pure Land","All equally","None"],"Through meditation and the Path."]}]},{"id":"re-hinduism","name":"RE: Hinduism","group":"re","short":"Hinduism","weeks":[{"t":"Brahman, the Trimurti & the deities","intro":"Hindus believe in Brahman, the ultimate reality behind everything. Brahman is worshipped through many deities, especially the Trimurti of Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva.","how":["Brahman is the supreme, unchanging reality: everywhere and in everything.","The symbol of Brahman is Aum (Om), the sacred sound.","Brahman can be understood as nirguna (without form) or saguna (with qualities and forms).","The Trimurti: Brahma (creator), Vishnu (preserver), Shiva (destroyer and transformer).","The deities are seen as aspects or forms of the one Brahman, not separate gods competing."],"example":{"q":"Explain two Hindu beliefs about the Trimurti. (5 marks)","steps":["Belief 1: Brahma creates the universe.","Develop: he is shown with four heads, facing all directions.","Belief 2: Vishnu preserves the universe and comes to Earth as avatars when needed.","Source: the Bhagavad Gita, where Krishna (Vishnu's avatar) says he comes to restore dharma."],"a":"Two developed beliefs plus a source."},"walk":[{"h":"Vaishnavism and Shaivism","b":"Vaishnavites worship Vishnu, often through his avatars Rama and Krishna, as the supreme form of God. Shaivites worship Shiva as supreme. Shiva is often shown as Nataraja, Lord of the Dance, destroying and recreating the universe."},{"h":"The goddesses","b":"Three main female deities are Saraswati (learning and the arts, consort of Brahma), Lakshmi (wealth and good fortune, consort of Vishnu) and Parvati (consort of Shiva). The power of the goddess is called Shakti, also worshipped as Durga and Kali."},{"h":"Symbols","b":"The lotus flower grows from mud but stays clean, symbolising purity and spiritual growth. Brahma is often shown seated on a lotus that grows from Vishnu's navel."}],"qs":[["What is Brahman?",["The ultimate reality behind everything","A priest","A temple","A festival"],"Supreme and unchanging."],["What is the sacred symbol of Brahman?",["Aum (Om)","The lotus","The trident","The wheel"],"The sacred sound."],["Which deity preserves the universe?",["Vishnu","Brahma","Shiva","Ganesh"],"He comes as avatars."],["Which deity is the creator?",["Brahma","Vishnu","Shiva","Lakshmi"],"Shown with four heads."],["Shiva as Nataraja is the Lord of…",["The Dance","Wealth","Learning","War"],"Destroying and recreating."],["Lakshmi is the goddess of…",["Wealth and good fortune","Learning","Death","Rain"],"Consort of Vishnu."],["Saraswati is the goddess of…",["Learning and the arts","Wealth","Destruction","War"],"Consort of Brahma."],["Nirguna Brahman means Brahman…",["Without form or qualities","With many forms","As Shiva only","As a statue"],"Beyond description."],["Vaishnavites mainly worship…",["Vishnu","Shiva","Brahma","Durga"],"Often as Rama or Krishna."],["What does the lotus symbolise?",["Purity and spiritual growth","War","Wealth only","Death"],"Grows clean from mud."]],"bonus":["What is Shakti?",["The power of the goddess","A holy book","A festival","A caste"],"Worshipped as Durga and Kali."]},{"t":"Avatars & Hindu cosmology","intro":"Avatars are forms in which a deity, usually Vishnu, comes to Earth. Hindu cosmology describes a universe that is created, sustained and destroyed in endless cycles.","how":["An avatar is a 'descent' of a deity into the world, usually to restore dharma.","Vishnu has ten main avatars (the Dashavatara), including Rama and Krishna.","The tenth avatar, Kalki, is still to come at the end of this age.","Time is cyclical: the universe goes through endless cycles of creation and destruction.","Each cycle has four yugas (ages). We live in the Kali Yuga, the age of decline."],"example":{"q":"Explain why Krishna is important to many Hindus. (4 marks)","steps":["Krishna is an avatar of Vishnu.","In the Bhagavad Gita he teaches Arjuna about duty and devotion.","His teaching on bhakti (devotion) is followed by millions.","His birth is celebrated at Janmashtami."],"a":"Two developed reasons."},"walk":[{"h":"The ten avatars","b":"They include Matsya (fish), Kurma (tortoise), Varaha (boar), Narasimha (man-lion), Vamana (dwarf), Parashurama, Rama, Krishna and Kalki. Some lists include the Buddha or Balarama. Each avatar saved the world from a particular danger."},{"h":"Rama","b":"Rama is the hero of the Ramayana. He rescues his wife Sita from the demon king Ravana. He is seen as the perfect example of duty, honour and goodness. Diwali celebrates his return home."},{"h":"Cycles of time","b":"A day of Brahma lasts billions of years, then the universe dissolves and is recreated. The four yugas are Satya, Treta, Dvapara and Kali, each worse than the last. At the end of Kali Yuga, Kalki will come to restore goodness."}],"qs":[["What is an avatar?",["A descent of a deity to Earth","A priest","A holy book","A ritual"],"To restore dharma."],["How many main avatars does Vishnu have?",["Ten","Three","Seven","Twelve"],"The Dashavatara."],["Which avatar is still to come?",["Kalki","Rama","Krishna","Matsya"],"At the end of Kali Yuga."],["Who rescued Sita from Ravana?",["Rama","Krishna","Shiva","Arjuna"],"In the Ramayana."],["Which festival celebrates Rama's return?",["Diwali","Holi","Janmashtami","Navaratri"],"Festival of lights."],["Who teaches Arjuna in the Bhagavad Gita?",["Krishna","Rama","Brahma","Ganesh"],"About duty and devotion."],["Hindus see time as…",["Cyclical","A straight line with one beginning","Unimportant","Ending forever soon"],"Endless cycles."],["Which age do we live in now?",["Kali Yuga","Satya Yuga","Treta Yuga","Dvapara Yuga"],"The age of decline."],["Matsya was a…",["Fish","Tortoise","Boar","Lion"],"The first avatar."],["Narasimha was a…",["Man-lion","Dwarf","Fish","Boar"],"He defeated a demon king."]],"bonus":["What is bhakti?",["Loving devotion to God","Meditation alone","Charity","Fasting"],"Taught by Krishna in the Gita."]},{"t":"Atman, karma, samsara & moksha","intro":"Hindus believe each living being has an eternal soul (atman) that is reborn many times. Karma decides future lives, and the goal is moksha: freedom from rebirth.","how":["Atman: the eternal soul or true self in every living being.","Samsara: the cycle of birth, death and rebirth (reincarnation).","Karma: actions have consequences. Good actions lead to good results, bad to bad, in this life or the next.","Moksha: liberation from samsara, when the atman is united with or realises Brahman.","Bhagavad Gita 2:22: like a person changing worn-out clothes, the soul takes on new bodies."],"example":{"q":"Explain how belief in karma affects how Hindus live. (4 marks)","steps":["Hindus believe every action has consequences for future lives.","So they try to act kindly and selflessly.","Many avoid harming living things (ahimsa), e.g. vegetarianism.","Good karma helps move closer to moksha."],"a":"Two developed influences."},"walk":[{"h":"Atman and Brahman","b":"Many Hindus (Advaita) believe atman and Brahman are ultimately the same: 'Tat tvam asi', 'you are that'. Others (Dvaita) believe the soul is separate from God and moksha means a loving relationship with God."},{"h":"Paths to moksha","b":"There are several paths (margas): jnana yoga (knowledge), karma yoga (selfless action), bhakti yoga (devotion) and raja yoga (meditation). Hindus can follow the path that suits them."},{"h":"Human life","b":"Human life is precious because it is the best chance to reach moksha. Animals and plants also have atman, which is one reason many Hindus respect all life and practise ahimsa (non-violence)."}],"qs":[["What is atman?",["The eternal soul","A god","A temple","A festival"],"The true self."],["What is samsara?",["The cycle of birth, death and rebirth","Liberation","Duty","Prayer"],"Reincarnation."],["What is karma?",["The law that actions have consequences","A god","A prayer","A holy book"],"Affects future lives."],["What is moksha?",["Liberation from samsara","Rebirth","A temple","Karma"],"The ultimate goal."],["The Gita compares rebirth to…",["Changing worn-out clothes","Crossing a river","Climbing a mountain","Lighting a lamp"],"Bhagavad Gita 2:22."],["What is ahimsa?",["Non-violence","Devotion","Duty","Knowledge"],"Respect for all life."],["Bhakti yoga is the path of…",["Devotion","Knowledge","Meditation","Action"],"Love for God."],["Karma yoga is the path of…",["Selfless action","Devotion","Knowledge","Fasting"],"Acting without selfish desire."],["'Tat tvam asi' means…",["You are that","God is great","Peace be with you","I am a soul"],"Atman is Brahman."],["Why is human life especially precious for Hindus?",["It's the best chance to reach moksha","Humans don't have atman","Animals are evil","It's the last life"],"Human birth is rare."]],"bonus":["Do animals have atman in Hindu belief?",["Yes","No","Only cows","Only humans and gods"],"All living things have a soul."]},{"t":"Dharma, virtue, free will & suffering","intro":"Dharma means duty and right living. This week covers sanatana dharma, personal virtues, free will and Hindu ideas about why suffering happens.","how":["Dharma: duty, the right way to live according to your role and stage of life.","Sanatana dharma ('eternal law') is how many Hindus describe their religion.","Key virtues: ahimsa (non-violence), satya (truthfulness), daya (compassion), dana (generosity), self-control.","Free will: humans choose their actions and so create their own karma.","Suffering is often explained as the result of past karma, and can be a chance to grow spiritually."],"example":{"q":"Explain two Hindu teachings about suffering. (5 marks)","steps":["Teaching 1: suffering results from karma from past actions or lives.","Develop: so Hindus accept it and try to act well now.","Teaching 2: suffering comes from ignorance (avidya) of the true self.","Source: the Bhagavad Gita teaches that the wise are not disturbed by pleasure or pain."],"a":"Two developed teachings plus a source."},"walk":[{"h":"The four aims of life","b":"Hindus aim for dharma (duty), artha (earning a living honestly), kama (pleasure in moderation) and moksha (liberation). Dharma guides the other aims."},{"h":"Stages of life (ashramas)","b":"Traditionally life has four stages: student (brahmacharya), householder (grihastha), retirement (vanaprastha) and renunciation (sannyasa). Each stage has different duties."},{"h":"Duty in the Bhagavad Gita","b":"Arjuna doesn't want to fight his relatives. Krishna tells him to do his dharma as a warrior without attachment to the results. Doing your duty selflessly is a key Hindu teaching."}],"qs":[["What is dharma?",["Duty and right living","Liberation","A god","A temple"],"Depends on role and stage of life."],["What does sanatana dharma mean?",["Eternal law","New religion","Holy book","Sacred river"],"A name for Hinduism."],["What is satya?",["Truthfulness","Non-violence","Wealth","Pleasure"],"A key virtue."],["Which aim is about earning a living honestly?",["Artha","Kama","Moksha","Dharma"],"One of the four aims."],["The householder stage is called…",["Grihastha","Sannyasa","Brahmacharya","Vanaprastha"],"Raising a family and working."],["Why did Arjuna not want to fight?",["He would be fighting his relatives","He was ill","He was afraid of Krishna","He had no weapons"],"Krishna teaches him about duty."],["Hindus often explain suffering as the result of…",["Karma from past actions","God's anger","Bad luck only","The devil"],"Cause and effect."],["What is avidya?",["Ignorance","Knowledge","Devotion","Liberation"],"Not knowing the true self."],["Daya means…",["Compassion","Wealth","Duty","Pleasure"],"Caring for others."],["Free will means Hindus…",["Create their own karma by their choices","Have no control","Can't change anything","Are judged by others"],"Responsibility for actions."]],"bonus":["What is sannyasa?",["Renouncing worldly life to seek moksha","Being a student","Getting married","Retiring with family"],"The final ashrama."]}]},{"id":"re-sikhism","name":"RE: Sikhism","group":"re","short":"Sikhism","weeks":[{"t":"The nature of God & the Mool Mantra","intro":"Sikhism was founded by Guru Nanak in the Punjab over 500 years ago. Sikhs believe in one God, described in the Mool Mantra at the start of the Guru Granth Sahib.","how":["Sikhs believe in one God (Waheguru, 'wonderful Lord').","The Mool Mantra opens the Guru Granth Sahib and sums up beliefs about God.","It begins 'Ik Onkar': there is only one God.","God is the creator, without fear or hatred, timeless, not born, self-existent.","God is nirgun (without form, beyond understanding) and sargun (present in creation)."],"example":{"q":"Explain two Sikh beliefs about God from the Mool Mantra. (5 marks)","steps":["Belief 1: there is only one God (Ik Onkar).","Develop: so Sikhs believe all people worship the same God.","Belief 2: God is the creator who is timeless and was never born.","Source: the Mool Mantra, the opening of the Guru Granth Sahib."],"a":"Two developed beliefs plus a source."},"walk":[{"h":"The Mool Mantra","b":"'There is one God, eternal truth is his name, creator of all things, without fear, without hatred, timeless, not born, self-existent, known by the Guru's grace.' Sikhs recite it daily."},{"h":"God and creation","b":"Sikhs believe God created the universe and is present within it (immanent) but also beyond it (transcendent). Everything depends on God's will (hukam). Creation is an expression of God, so all life deserves respect."},{"h":"Origins of Sikhism","b":"Guru Nanak was born in 1469 in what is now Pakistan. After a spiritual experience in a river, he taught: 'There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim', meaning all are equal before God. Ten human Gurus followed, then the Guru Granth Sahib."}],"qs":[["Who founded Sikhism?",["Guru Nanak","Guru Gobind Singh","Guru Arjan","Guru Angad"],"Born 1469."],["What does Ik Onkar mean?",["There is one God","God is great","Wonderful Lord","Truth is eternal"],"The first words of the Mool Mantra."],["Where is the Mool Mantra found?",["At the start of the Guru Granth Sahib","At the end of the Bible","In the Qur'an","In the Vedas"],"Opening of the scripture."],["What does Waheguru mean?",["Wonderful Lord","One God","Great teacher","Holy book"],"A Sikh name for God."],["Which is in the Mool Mantra?",["Without fear, without hatred","God has a son","God is three","God was born"],"Qualities of God."],["What is hukam?",["God's will","A prayer","A temple","A festival"],"Everything follows God's will."],["Where did Sikhism begin?",["The Punjab","Egypt","Japan","Arabia"],"North India and Pakistan."],["Nirgun means God is…",["Without form, beyond understanding","In every object","A human","Angry"],"Sargun means present in creation."],["How many human Gurus were there?",["Ten","Five","Seven","Twelve"],"Then the Guru Granth Sahib."],["'There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim' teaches…",["All are equal before God","Sikhs are better","Religion doesn't matter","There's no God"],"Said by Guru Nanak."]],"bonus":["What does 'known by the Guru's grace' mean?",["People understand God through God's grace and the Guru's teaching","Only Gurus know God","God can't be known","You must pay to know God"],"Last line of the Mool Mantra."]},{"t":"Human life, karma, rebirth & mukti","intro":"Sikhs believe the soul goes through many lives. The goal is mukti: liberation and union with God. Learn the barriers to mukti and how to become God-centred.","how":["Karma and rebirth: actions affect future lives in the cycle of samsara.","Mukti: liberation from rebirth and union with God, which can happen in this life.","Gurmukh: a God-centred person. Manmukh: a self-centred person.","Haumai (ego, self-centredness) is the biggest barrier to mukti.","The five evils (thieves): lust, anger, greed, attachment and pride."],"example":{"q":"Explain how a Sikh can move from manmukh to gurmukh. (4 marks)","steps":["By meditating on God's name (nam japna).","This helps overcome haumai (ego).","By serving others selflessly (sewa).","And by earning an honest living and sharing (kirat karni, vand chhakna)."],"a":"Two developed ways."},"walk":[{"h":"The five stages of liberation","b":"The five khands are stages towards mukti: duty (dharam khand), knowledge (gian khand), effort (saram khand), grace (karam khand) and truth (sach khand), where the soul is united with God."},{"h":"Maya","b":"Maya is illusion: thinking the material world (money, possessions, status) is what really matters. It keeps people self-centred and attached, and so trapped in rebirth."},{"h":"Virtues","b":"Sikhs aim to develop five virtues to counter the five evils: truthfulness, contentment, compassion, humility and love. Living this way, as a householder rather than a hermit, is the Sikh path."}],"qs":[["What is mukti?",["Liberation and union with God","A prayer","A festival","A Guru"],"Freedom from rebirth."],["What is a gurmukh?",["A God-centred person","A self-centred person","A priest","A temple"],"The ideal Sikh."],["What is haumai?",["Ego or self-centredness","God's will","Charity","Meditation"],"The main barrier to mukti."],["Which is one of the five evils?",["Greed","Compassion","Humility","Truth"],"Lust, anger, greed, attachment, pride."],["What is maya?",["Illusion about what matters","A goddess","A prayer","A sword"],"Attachment to material things."],["What is nam japna?",["Meditating on God's name","Charity","Earning honestly","Fasting"],"Remembering God."],["What is the final stage of liberation?",["Sach khand (realm of truth)","Dharam khand","Gian khand","Saram khand"],"Union with God."],["Kirat karni means…",["Earning an honest living","Sharing with others","Prayer","Pilgrimage"],"One of three key duties."],["Vand chhakna means…",["Sharing with others","Meditation","Fighting injustice","Fasting"],"Giving to those in need."],["Can mukti be reached in this life?",["Yes","No","Only after 100 lives","Only for Gurus"],"Through God's grace and living well."]],"bonus":["Which virtue counters pride?",["Humility","Greed","Anger","Lust"],"Five virtues counter five evils."]},{"t":"Equality: Guru Nanak, Guru Gobind Singh & the Guru Granth Sahib","intro":"Equality is at the heart of Sikhism. Learn how Guru Nanak, Guru Gobind Singh and the Guru Granth Sahib all teach that everyone is equal before God.","how":["Guru Nanak rejected the caste system and taught that all people are equal.","The langar (free community kitchen) feeds everyone together, whatever their background.","Guru Gobind Singh, the tenth Guru, founded the Khalsa in 1699.","Khalsa men take the name Singh (lion) and women Kaur (princess), removing caste surnames.","The Guru Granth Sahib includes writings by Hindu and Muslim saints, and is now the eternal Guru."],"example":{"q":"Explain how the langar shows equality. (4 marks)","steps":["Everyone sits together on the floor.","So no one is placed above anyone else, rich or poor.","Food is free and vegetarian, so all can eat it.","Volunteers of all backgrounds cook and serve."],"a":"Two developed points."},"walk":[{"h":"The river experience","b":"Guru Nanak disappeared into a river for three days. When he came back, he said he had been taken to God's court and given a mission. His first words were 'There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim'."},{"h":"The Khalsa and the five Ks","b":"At Vaisakhi in 1699 Guru Gobind Singh asked who would give their head for the faith. Five men volunteered: the Panj Pyare. Khalsa members wear the five Ks: kesh (uncut hair), kangha (comb), kara (steel bangle), kachera (shorts) and kirpan (sword)."},{"h":"The eternal Guru","b":"Before he died in 1708, Guru Gobind Singh named the Guru Granth Sahib as the final, eternal Guru. It is treated like a living Guru: placed on a throne, covered with cloth, and laid to rest at night."}],"qs":[["Who founded the Khalsa?",["Guru Gobind Singh","Guru Nanak","Guru Arjan","Guru Tegh Bahadur"],"In 1699."],["What is the langar?",["A free community kitchen","A prayer","A sword","A holy book"],"Everyone eats together."],["What surname do Khalsa women take?",["Kaur","Singh","Khan","Devi"],"Meaning princess."],["What does Singh mean?",["Lion","Prince","Teacher","Warrior"],"Taken by Khalsa men."],["Who were the Panj Pyare?",["The five beloved ones who volunteered in 1699","Five holy books","Five Gurus","Five rivers"],"The first Khalsa members."],["What is kesh?",["Uncut hair","A comb","A bangle","A sword"],"One of the five Ks."],["What is the kara?",["A steel bangle","A turban","A comb","Shorts"],"A reminder of God's oneness."],["Who is the eternal Guru now?",["The Guru Granth Sahib","Guru Nanak","The Khalsa","The Panj Pyare"],"Named in 1708."],["Which system did Guru Nanak reject?",["Caste","Democracy","Monarchy","Charity"],"All are equal."],["The Guru Granth Sahib includes writings by…",["Sikh Gurus and Hindu and Muslim saints","Only Guru Nanak","Only Muslims","Only Christians"],"Shows equality."]],"bonus":["Why do Sikhs sit on the floor in the langar?",["So everyone is at the same level","There are no chairs anywhere","It's cheaper","To pray"],"Equality in practice."]},{"t":"Sewa & the sangat","intro":"Sewa (selfless service) and the sangat (community) are central to Sikh life. Learn the three types of sewa and why the sangat matters.","how":["Sewa: selfless service to others without expecting reward.","Tan (physical) sewa: e.g. cooking or cleaning in the langar.","Man (mental) sewa: e.g. teaching others about Sikhism or studying the scriptures.","Dhan (material) sewa: giving money or goods. Many Sikhs give 10% of income (dasvandh).","Sangat: the Sikh community, especially when gathered to worship in the gurdwara."],"example":{"q":"Explain why sewa is important to Sikhs. (4 marks)","steps":["It helps overcome haumai (ego), as you put others first.","So it moves a Sikh towards becoming gurmukh.","It shows love for God by serving God's creation.","Guru Nanak and the Gurus taught it by their example."],"a":"Two developed reasons."},"walk":[{"h":"The sangat","b":"The sangat began with Guru Nanak's followers gathering at Kartarpur. Worshipping together gives support and encouragement, and keeps Sikhs on the right path. Gurbani says good company helps people grow closer to God."},{"h":"Amritdhari Sikhs","b":"Amritdhari Sikhs have been initiated into the Khalsa in the Amrit ceremony. They wear the five Ks and follow a strict code, including not cutting hair and not drinking alcohol or using tobacco."},{"h":"Symbols of the gurdwara","b":"The Nishan Sahib is the flag flown outside every gurdwara, showing Sikhs are welcome and the langar is open. The Khanda symbol shows a double-edged sword, a circle (chakkar) and two swords for spiritual and worldly power."}],"qs":[["What is sewa?",["Selfless service","Prayer","A festival","A sword"],"Helping without reward."],["Cleaning the gurdwara is which sewa?",["Tan (physical)","Man (mental)","Dhan (material)","None"],"Physical service."],["Teaching others about Sikhism is…",["Man sewa","Tan sewa","Dhan sewa","Langar"],"Mental service."],["What is dasvandh?",["Giving 10% of income","A prayer","A festival","A Guru"],"Dhan sewa."],["What is the sangat?",["The Sikh community","A sword","A kitchen","A flag"],"Especially gathered in worship."],["What is the Nishan Sahib?",["The Sikh flag outside a gurdwara","A holy book","A prayer","A meal"],"Shows all are welcome."],["Amritdhari Sikhs are…",["Initiated into the Khalsa","New to Sikhism","Gurus","Not religious"],"Through the Amrit ceremony."],["Why does sewa help a Sikh?",["It reduces haumai (ego)","It earns money","It replaces prayer","It's required by law"],"Putting others first."],["Where did Guru Nanak's early sangat gather?",["Kartarpur","Amritsar","Delhi","London"],"The first Sikh community."],["The Khanda symbol includes…",["A double-edged sword, a circle and two swords","A lotus","A crescent moon","A cross"],"Sikh emblem."]],"bonus":["What is a gurdwara?",["A Sikh place of worship","A Sikh holy book","A Sikh festival","A Sikh flag"],"'Door to the Guru'."]}]},{"id":"re-themes","name":"RE: Themes (B & D)","weeks":[{"t":"Theme B: origins & value of the universe and life","intro":"Theme B starts with where the universe and human life came from, and why life is valuable. You'll need religious and scientific views, plus sanctity and quality of life.","how":["Big Bang: the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago from a tiny, hot, dense point.","Evolution: life developed over billions of years by natural selection (Darwin).","Many religious people accept science, believing God started or guided the process.","Sanctity of life: life is holy and God-given. Quality of life: how good or fulfilling a life is.","Stewardship: humans should look after the world for God and future generations."],"example":{"q":"'Science has proved God did not create the universe.' Evaluate. (12 marks)","steps":["For: the Big Bang and evolution explain origins without needing God (many Humanists).","Against: science explains how, religion explains why (many Christians and Muslims).","Against: something must have caused the Big Bang (cosmological argument).","Conclusion: judge which side is stronger and why."],"a":"Both sides, religious and non-religious views, justified conclusion."},"walk":[{"h":"Religious views on origins","b":"Christians: Genesis 1 describes creation in six days. Literalists read it as fact; many others as symbolic. Muslims: the Qur'an says God created the heavens and earth, and some verses are seen as fitting the Big Bang. Hindus and Buddhists see time as cyclical."},{"h":"Value of human life","b":"Christians and Jews believe humans are made in God's image. Muslims believe God breathed his spirit into Adam. Sikhs and Hindus believe every being has a soul. These ideas underpin the sanctity of life."},{"h":"Use and abuse of the environment","b":"Religions teach stewardship and respect for creation. Pollution, climate change and loss of natural resources go against this duty. Many religious groups support recycling, renewable energy and sustainable living."}],"qs":[["When did the Big Bang happen?",["About 13.8 billion years ago","6000 years ago","4.5 million years ago","100 000 years ago"],"Scientific estimate."],["Who proposed evolution by natural selection?",["Charles Darwin","Isaac Newton","Albert Einstein","Galileo"],"1859."],["What does sanctity of life mean?",["Life is holy and God-given","Life should be enjoyable","Life is short","Only humans matter"],"A religious idea."],["What does quality of life mean?",["How good or fulfilling a person's life is","How holy life is","How long life is","Life after death"],"Often used in non-religious arguments."],["What is stewardship?",["Looking after the world for God","Owning the world","Using up resources","Building churches"],"A religious duty."],["Creationists read Genesis…",["Literally","Symbolically","Not at all","As science fiction"],"Six literal days."],["Many Christians see science and religion as…",["Compatible: science explains how, religion explains why","Always opposed","The same thing","Both wrong"],"A common view."],["Which religions see time as cyclical?",["Hinduism and Buddhism","Christianity and Islam","Judaism and Christianity","None"],"Endless cycles."],["What is a 12-mark answer expected to include?",["Arguments for and against and a justified conclusion","One argument only","Only facts","A diagram"],"Evaluation."],["Which group generally explains origins without God?",["Humanists","Catholics","Orthodox Jews","Sunni Muslims"],"Non-religious view."]],"bonus":["What is the cosmological argument?",["Everything has a cause, so the universe needs a first cause (God)","The world looks designed","People feel God","Miracles prove God"],"Used against the statement."]},{"t":"Theme B: abortion, euthanasia & life after death","intro":"This part of Theme B covers abortion, euthanasia and life after death. Know UK law, religious and non-religious views, and examples like Diane Pretty and Dignitas.","how":["The 1967 Abortion Act allows abortion up to 24 weeks if two doctors agree there are medical or social reasons.","Euthanasia (assisted dying) is illegal in the UK.","Arguments for: quality of life, the right to choose, relieving suffering.","Arguments against: sanctity of life, slippery slope, risk to vulnerable people.","Hospices offer palliative care: comfort and pain relief at the end of life."],"example":{"q":"Explain two religious beliefs about abortion. (5 marks)","steps":["Catholic belief: life begins at conception, so abortion is murder.","Develop: this is based on the sanctity of life.","Many Muslims: abortion may be allowed before ensoulment (often said to be 120 days) or to save the mother's life.","Source: 'Do not kill your children for fear of poverty' (Qur'an 17:31)."],"a":"Two developed beliefs plus a source."},"walk":[{"h":"Different religious views","b":"Catholics oppose abortion and euthanasia. Some Protestants accept abortion in hard cases. Judaism allows abortion when the mother's life is at risk. Hindus and Buddhists see abortion as harming a life (ahimsa), though some accept it to prevent greater suffering."},{"h":"Euthanasia cases","b":"Diane Pretty, who had motor neurone disease, asked the courts in 2002 to allow her husband to help her die without prosecution. She lost. Dignitas in Switzerland offers assisted dying, and some UK citizens travel there."},{"h":"Life after death","b":"Christians and Muslims believe in judgement, heaven and hell. Hindus, Buddhists and Sikhs believe in rebirth. Humanists believe there is no afterlife, so this life is the one that matters. Beliefs about the afterlife affect views on ending life."}],"qs":[["Up to how many weeks does the 1967 Act allow most abortions?",["24","12","36","40"],"Two doctors must agree."],["Is euthanasia legal in the UK?",["No","Yes","Only in Wales","Only for adults over 80"],"Assisted dying is illegal."],["What do hospices provide?",["Palliative care","Euthanasia","Abortions","Weddings"],"Comfort at the end of life."],["Catholics believe life begins at…",["Conception","Birth","24 weeks","Age 1"],"So abortion is wrong."],["Who was Diane Pretty?",["A woman who asked the courts to allow assisted dying","A doctor","A politician","A priest"],"She lost her case in 2002."],["What is Dignitas?",["A Swiss organisation offering assisted dying","A UK hospice","A charity for children","A religious book"],"Based in Switzerland."],["The 'slippery slope' argument says…",["Allowing euthanasia could lead to abuse of vulnerable people","Euthanasia is always good","Hospices are slippery","Life is short"],"Against euthanasia."],["Which religions believe in rebirth?",["Hinduism, Buddhism and Sikhism","Christianity and Islam","Judaism only","Humanism"],"Samsara."],["Humanists believe…",["There's no afterlife, so this life matters most","In heaven and hell","In reincarnation","In purgatory"],"Non-religious."],["Voluntary euthanasia means…",["The person asks for help to die","A doctor decides alone","A natural death","Life support continues"],"At the person's request."]],"bonus":["What is ensoulment?",["The moment the soul enters the foetus","Birth","Baptism","Death"],"Many Muslims say 120 days."]},{"t":"Theme D: prejudice, discrimination & human rights","intro":"Theme D starts with prejudice, discrimination, sexism and religious freedom. Link religious teachings to the law and real examples.","how":["Prejudice is an attitude (pre-judging). Discrimination is an action (treating someone unfairly).","The Equality Act 2010 makes discrimination illegal in the UK.","Human rights include freedom of religion, freedom of speech and the right to life.","Religious teachings on equality: 'all are one in Christ Jesus' (Galatians 3:28), the langar, 'no Arab is superior to a non-Arab' (Muhammad's last sermon).","Positive discrimination means treating a group more favourably to make up for past unfairness."],"example":{"q":"Explain two religious beliefs about racial prejudice. (5 marks)","steps":["Christian: all humans are made in God's image, so racism is wrong.","Source: 'There is neither Jew nor Gentile… all are one in Christ Jesus' (Galatians 3:28).","Muslim: all people come from Adam and are equal.","Source: Muhammad's last sermon: 'no Arab has superiority over a non-Arab'."],"a":"Two developed beliefs plus sources."},"walk":[{"h":"Sexism in religion","b":"Some churches ordain women (the Church of England since 1994) while others don't (the Catholic Church). In Islam men and women are equal before God but often have different roles. Sikhism teaches full equality: women can lead worship."},{"h":"Religious freedom","b":"In the UK people can follow any religion or none. Religious people should respect others' beliefs, though some feel free speech can offend religious beliefs. Some countries still persecute religious minorities."},{"h":"Responding to prejudice","b":"Religious people have campaigned against prejudice, like Martin Luther King Jr in the US civil rights movement and Desmond Tutu against apartheid in South Africa. Many teach forgiveness and love for enemies."}],"qs":[["What is the difference between prejudice and discrimination?",["Prejudice is an attitude, discrimination is an action","They're the same","Discrimination is a thought","Prejudice is illegal, discrimination isn't"],"Thought vs action."],["Which law bans discrimination in the UK?",["Equality Act 2010","Abortion Act 1967","Human Rights Act 1066","Poor Law 1834"],"Covers race, sex, religion and more."],["Galatians 3:28 says…",["All are one in Christ Jesus","Love your enemies","God is love","Do not judge"],"Used against prejudice."],["Muhammad's last sermon taught…",["No Arab is superior to a non-Arab","Men are superior to women","Muslims shouldn't travel","Charity is optional"],"Racial equality."],["Which Christian leader campaigned for civil rights in the USA?",["Martin Luther King Jr","Desmond Tutu","Mother Teresa","Pope Francis"],"1950s and 60s."],["Who campaigned against apartheid?",["Desmond Tutu","Martin Luther King Jr","Gandhi only","Malala"],"South Africa."],["Since when has the Church of England ordained women as priests?",["1994","1900","2014","Never"],"Women bishops from 2014."],["What is positive discrimination?",["Favouring a group to make up for past unfairness","Treating everyone badly","Racism","A type of prejudice"],"Controversial."],["Which religion lets women lead all worship equally?",["Sikhism","Catholic Christianity","Orthodox Judaism","None"],"Full equality is taught."],["Freedom of religion means…",["The right to follow any religion or none","Everyone must be religious","Only one religion is allowed","No religion is allowed"],"A human right."]],"bonus":["What is a human right?",["A basic right everyone has because they are human","A privilege for the rich","A religious rule only","A school rule"],"e.g. the right to life."]},{"t":"Theme D: wealth, poverty, charity & exploitation","intro":"The second half of Theme D covers wealth and poverty, charities, government help, and exploitation like people trafficking and unfair pay.","how":["Religions teach it's not wrong to be wealthy, but greed and ignoring the poor are wrong.","Christianity: 'The love of money is a root of all kinds of evil' (1 Timothy 6:10).","Islam: zakah (2.5% of savings) and sadaqah (voluntary charity).","Sikhism: dasvandh (10%) and vand chhakna (sharing). Judaism: tzedakah.","Exploitation: unfair pay, excessive interest, people trafficking, modern slavery."],"example":{"q":"'Religious people should give all their money to the poor.' Evaluate. (12 marks)","steps":["For: Jesus told the rich young man to sell everything and give to the poor.","Against: most religions teach giving a fair share (zakah 2.5%, tzedakah, dasvandh 10%) while supporting your family.","Against: charities can create dependency; long-term development may help more.","Conclusion: weigh the arguments and make a reasoned judgement."],"a":"Both sides, religious teachings and a justified conclusion."},"walk":[{"h":"Charities","b":"Religious charities include Christian Aid, CAFOD, Islamic Relief, Khalsa Aid and World Jewish Relief. They provide emergency aid after disasters and long-term development like wells and schools. Some argue governments should do more."},{"h":"Government help","b":"In the UK the welfare state provides benefits, free healthcare and housing support. Some say this is enough. Many religious people argue that individuals and communities also have a duty to help."},{"h":"Exploitation","b":"Exploitation includes paying less than the minimum wage, people trafficking and charging excessive interest. Islam forbids riba (interest). Christian groups campaign against modern slavery. All religions teach that every person has dignity."}],"qs":[["'The love of money is a root of all kinds of evil' is from…",["1 Timothy 6:10","Genesis 1","Qur'an 2","Psalm 23"],"Christian teaching on wealth."],["What percentage of savings is zakah?",["2.5%","10%","50%","1%"],"One of the Five Pillars."],["What is sadaqah?",["Voluntary charity in Islam","Compulsory tax","A prayer","Pilgrimage"],"Any act of kindness."],["What is dasvandh?",["Sikh giving of 10%","Jewish charity","A Hindu festival","A Buddhist meditation"],"Dhan sewa."],["Which is a Sikh charity?",["Khalsa Aid","CAFOD","Christian Aid","Islamic Relief"],"Works worldwide."],["What is riba?",["Interest on loans, forbidden in Islam","Charity","A prayer","A tax"],"Seen as exploitation."],["What is people trafficking?",["Moving people illegally to exploit them","Travelling abroad","Charity work","Fair trade"],"Modern slavery."],["What did Jesus tell the rich young man?",["Sell everything and give to the poor","Keep his wealth","Build a church","Become a priest"],"A challenge about wealth."],["What does the welfare state provide?",["Benefits and free healthcare","Free holidays","Only pensions","Nothing"],"Government help."],["One criticism of charity is…",["It can create dependency","It is always perfect","It never helps","It's illegal"],"Long-term development may be better."]],"bonus":["What is tzedakah?",["Jewish charity seen as a duty of justice","A Christian prayer","A Sikh flag","A Muslim festival"],"Righteous giving."]}]},{"id":"history","name":"History","weeks":[{"t":"Medicine c1250–c1700: medieval to Renaissance","intro":"Your Medicine through time thematic study starts in medieval England. Learn what people thought caused disease, how they treated it, and the key case studies: the Black Death, William Harvey and the Great Plague.","how":["Medieval causes: God, the Four Humours (Hippocrates and Galen), miasma (bad air), astrology.","Medieval treatments: prayer, bloodletting, purging, herbal remedies.","The Church controlled learning and supported Galen's ideas.","Renaissance: more questioning. Vesalius corrected Galen's anatomy and Harvey proved blood circulates.","Know the factors: religion, government, science and technology, individuals, attitudes."],"example":{"q":"Explain why the Church slowed medical progress in medieval England. (12 marks)","steps":["The Church taught that disease was punishment from God.","It supported Galen's ideas because they fitted Christian beliefs.","It discouraged dissection, so anatomy errors weren't corrected.","But monasteries also cared for the sick and kept medical books."],"a":"Two or three explained reasons, linked back to the question."},"walk":[{"h":"The Black Death 1348","b":"People blamed God's anger, miasma and the planets. Treatments included prayer, flagellation, carrying sweet-smelling herbs and bleeding. Nothing worked, because no one knew about germs or rats' fleas."},{"h":"William Harvey","b":"In 1628 Harvey proved the heart pumps blood around the body in a circuit. This disproved Galen's idea that blood was made in the liver and used up. It had little immediate effect on treatment, but changed anatomy."},{"h":"The Great Plague 1665","b":"Most people still blamed miasma, but the government took more action: quarantine, shutting houses with a red cross, killing cats and dogs, and banning public gatherings."}],"qs":[["Who developed the Four Humours theory?",["Hippocrates","Harvey","Pasteur","Jenner"],"Galen developed it further."],["What was miasma?",["Bad air thought to cause disease","A type of germ","A medicine","A hospital"],"People believed bad smells spread illness."],["Which Roman doctor's ideas did the Church support?",["Galen","Vesalius","Harvey","Snow"],"His work fitted the idea of a designed body."],["When did the Black Death reach England?",["1348","1665","1066","1854"],"It killed about a third of the population."],["What did Harvey discover?",["Blood circulates around the body","Germs cause disease","Vaccination","Anaesthetics"],"Published in 1628."],["Who corrected Galen's anatomy by dissecting bodies?",["Vesalius","Jenner","Fleming","Lister"],"His book was published in 1543."],["What was a common medieval treatment?",["Bloodletting","Antibiotics","X-rays","Vaccines"],"To rebalance the humours."],["What did authorities do in the Great Plague of 1665?",["Shut infected houses","Gave vaccines","Used antibiotics","Built the NHS"],"Houses were marked with a red cross."],["What actually spread the Black Death?",["Fleas on rats","Bad air","Planets","Water"],"People didn't know this at the time."],["Which factor most held back medieval medicine?",["The Church's control of ideas","Too many doctors","Too much science","The printing press"],"It discouraged challenging Galen."]],"bonus":["Which invention helped spread new medical ideas in the Renaissance?",["The printing press","The microscope","The X-ray","The telephone"],"Books could be copied quickly and accurately."]},{"t":"Medicine c1700–present & the Western Front","intro":"From 1700 medicine changed fast: vaccination, germ theory, public health and modern drugs. The Western Front study looks at medical care in the First World War.","how":["Jenner developed vaccination against smallpox in 1796.","Pasteur's germ theory (1861) proved microbes cause disease. Koch identified specific bacteria.","John Snow linked cholera to a water pump on Broad Street (1854).","Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928. Florey and Chain mass-produced it in the 1940s.","Western Front: the RAMC, FANY, the chain of evacuation, new techniques like blood transfusion and the Thomas splint."],"example":{"q":"Explain why Jenner's vaccine was opposed at first.","steps":["He couldn't explain how it worked, because germ theory didn't exist yet.","Some people thought it was ungodly to use cowpox.","Doctors who made money from inoculation lost out.","Cartoons mocked it, showing people turning into cows."],"a":"Lack of explanation, religion, money and fear."},"walk":[{"h":"Lung cancer","b":"In the 1950s, Doll and Hill linked smoking to lung cancer. Modern treatment includes surgery, chemotherapy and radiotherapy. Government action includes banning advertising and smoking in public places."},{"h":"The chain of evacuation","b":"Wounded soldiers went from the Regimental Aid Post to the Dressing Station, then the Casualty Clearing Station, then Base Hospitals. Stretcher bearers, ambulances (including FANY drivers) and trains moved them."},{"h":"Wounds and new techniques","b":"Shrapnel and bullets caused infected wounds, and gas attacks caused burns. Conditions included trench foot and shell shock. New methods were the Thomas splint, which cut deaths from leg fractures, X-ray machines, and stored blood for transfusion."}],"qs":[["Who developed the smallpox vaccine?",["Edward Jenner","Louis Pasteur","John Snow","Alexander Fleming"],"In 1796, using cowpox."],["What did Pasteur's germ theory show?",["Microbes cause disease","Blood circulates","Smoking causes cancer","The humours were real"],"Published in 1861."],["How did John Snow show cholera spread?",["Mapping deaths around a water pump","Using a microscope","Testing on animals","Vaccines"],"Broad Street, 1854."],["Who discovered penicillin?",["Fleming","Florey","Chain","Koch"],"In 1928."],["Who mass-produced penicillin?",["Florey and Chain","Jenner","Pasteur","Harvey"],"With US help in the Second World War."],["What did the Thomas splint help with?",["Leg fractures","Gas attacks","Shell shock","Trench foot"],"It cut deaths from broken femurs dramatically."],["What was the FANY?",["First Aid Nursing Yeomanry","A field hospital","A gas mask","An army rank"],"Women who drove ambulances and nursed."],["What was the first stop on the chain of evacuation?",["Regimental Aid Post","Base Hospital","Casualty Clearing Station","Home hospital"],"Close to the front line."],["Who linked smoking to lung cancer?",["Doll and Hill","Pasteur and Koch","Florey and Chain","Jenner and Snow"],"In the 1950s."],["Trench foot was caused by…",["Standing in cold, wet mud","Gas","Bullets","Rats"],"Feet rotted from damp."]],"bonus":["Why was the Battle of the Somme hard for medical services?",["Huge numbers of casualties at once","No soldiers were hurt","It was in summer","There were too many doctors"],"About 57 000 British casualties on the first day."]},{"t":"Elizabethan England 1558–88","intro":"This depth study covers Elizabeth's problems in 1558, her religious settlement, plots involving Mary, Queen of Scots, war with Spain, and Elizabethan society and exploration.","how":["1558 problems: her legitimacy, gender and marriage, the French threat, debt, religious division.","The 1559 Religious Settlement: Act of Supremacy, Act of Uniformity, Royal Injunctions.","Threats: Puritans, Catholics, the Revolt of the Northern Earls (1569), and the Ridolfi, Throckmorton and Babington plots.","Mary, Queen of Scots was executed in 1587 after the Babington Plot.","War with Spain: Netherlands, Drake's raids, the Armada of 1588."],"example":{"q":"Explain why the Spanish Armada failed. (12 marks)","steps":["English fireships at Calais broke the Spanish crescent formation.","English ships were faster, with better cannons.","Poor Spanish planning: Parma's army wasn't ready.","Storms (the 'Protestant wind') wrecked ships round Scotland and Ireland."],"a":"Explain 3 reasons and judge which mattered most."},"walk":[{"h":"Religious Settlement","b":"Elizabeth created a 'middle way'. The Act of Supremacy made her Supreme Governor of the Church. The Act of Uniformity set out a Protestant prayer book but allowed some Catholic features, like vestments. Recusants who didn't attend church were fined."},{"h":"Plots","b":"Ridolfi (1571), Throckmorton (1583) and Babington (1586) all aimed to put Mary, Queen of Scots on the throne with foreign help. Walsingham's spy network uncovered them. Babington's letters proved Mary's involvement, leading to her execution."},{"h":"Society and exploration","b":"Poverty grew because of population growth, bad harvests and enclosure. The Poor Laws treated the 'deserving' and 'idle' poor differently. Drake sailed round the world (1577–80). Raleigh tried to colonise Virginia, which failed due to poor supplies and conflict."}],"qs":[["When did Elizabeth become queen?",["1558","1588","1603","1547"],"After her half-sister Mary I died."],["What did the Act of Supremacy do?",["Made Elizabeth Supreme Governor of the Church","Banned Catholics","Started war with Spain","Ended the Poor Laws"],"Part of the 1559 settlement."],["Who ran Elizabeth's spy network?",["Walsingham","Dudley","Drake","Cecil"],"Sir Francis Walsingham."],["Which plot led to Mary, Queen of Scots' execution?",["Babington Plot","Ridolfi Plot","Gunpowder Plot","Throckmorton Plot"],"Her letters were intercepted."],["When was the Spanish Armada?",["1588","1558","1605","1569"],"It was defeated."],["What were recusants?",["Catholics who refused to attend Protestant church","Puritans","Spanish spies","Sailors"],"They were fined."],["Who sailed around the world 1577–80?",["Francis Drake","Walter Raleigh","Robert Dudley","John Hawkins"],"He returned with treasure."],["What was the Revolt of the Northern Earls?",["A Catholic rebellion in 1569","A Puritan protest","A Spanish invasion","A peasant revolt about bread"],"It aimed to restore Catholicism."],["Why did the Virginia colony fail?",["Poor supplies and conflict with Native Americans","Too much gold","The Armada destroyed it","Elizabeth banned it"],"Roanoke was abandoned."],["Who led English forces in the Netherlands in 1585?",["Robert Dudley","Francis Drake","Walsingham","Philip II"],"The Earl of Leicester."]],"bonus":["What was 'singeing the King of Spain's beard'?",["Drake's raid on Cadiz in 1587","The Armada battle","Mary's execution","A court joke"],"It delayed the Armada by a year."]},{"t":"Weimar & Nazi Germany 1918–39","intro":"This period study runs from the start of the Weimar Republic to life under the Nazis. Learn the key events, dates and reasons for Hitler's rise.","how":["1919 Treaty of Versailles: land lost, army limited, war guilt, reparations.","Early challenges: Spartacist Revolt (1919), Kapp Putsch (1920), hyperinflation (1923).","Recovery under Stresemann (1924–29): Rentenmark, Dawes Plan, Locarno.","Rise of the Nazis: Munich Putsch (1923), Depression (1929), propaganda, Hitler made Chancellor January 1933.","Nazi control: Reichstag Fire, Enabling Act, Night of the Long Knives (1934), the SS and Gestapo."],"example":{"q":"Explain why Hitler became Chancellor in January 1933.","steps":["The Depression caused mass unemployment and anger at Weimar.","Nazi propaganda and the SA made them seem strong.","The Nazis became the largest party in July 1932.","Von Papen and Hindenburg thought they could control Hitler."],"a":"Economic crisis, Nazi strengths and the backstairs deal."},"walk":[{"h":"Hyperinflation 1923","b":"Germany couldn't pay reparations, so France occupied the Ruhr. The government printed money, prices soared, and savings became worthless. A loaf of bread cost 200 billion marks by November 1923."},{"h":"Creating a dictatorship","b":"The Reichstag Fire (February 1933) was blamed on communists. The Enabling Act let Hitler make laws without the Reichstag. In the Night of the Long Knives (1934) Röhm and SA leaders were killed. When Hindenburg died, Hitler became Führer."},{"h":"Life in Nazi Germany","b":"Women were encouraged to focus on 'Kinder, Küche, Kirche'. Young people joined the Hitler Youth and League of German Maidens. Jews were persecuted through the Nuremberg Laws (1935) and Kristallnacht (1938). Unemployment fell with public works and rearmament."}],"qs":[["Which treaty ended WW1 for Germany?",["Versailles","Locarno","Paris","Munich"],"Signed in 1919."],["When was hyperinflation at its worst?",["1923","1929","1933","1919"],"After the Ruhr occupation."],["Who led Weimar's recovery 1924–29?",["Stresemann","Hitler","Hindenburg","Ebert"],"He introduced the Rentenmark."],["What was the Munich Putsch?",["Hitler's failed attempt to seize power in 1923","A 1933 election","A communist revolt","A Treaty"],"Hitler was jailed and wrote Mein Kampf."],["When did Hitler become Chancellor?",["January 1933","1923","1939","1929"],"Appointed by Hindenburg."],["What did the Enabling Act allow?",["Hitler to pass laws without the Reichstag","Women to vote","Jews to emigrate","Free elections"],"March 1933."],["What happened in the Night of the Long Knives?",["SA leaders including Röhm were killed","Synagogues burned","The Reichstag burned","Hitler was arrested"],"June 1934."],["Which laws took away Jews' citizenship?",["Nuremberg Laws 1935","Enabling Act","Versailles","Dawes Plan"],"They banned marriage between Jews and non-Jews."],["What was the Dawes Plan?",["US loans to Germany","A Nazi law","A rebellion","A youth group"],"1924."],["What did 'Kinder, Küche, Kirche' mean for women?",["Children, kitchen, church","Work, war, vote","School, sport, state","Family, farm, factory"],"The Nazi ideal role for women."]],"bonus":["What was Kristallnacht?",["A night of attacks on Jewish shops and synagogues in November 1938","Hitler's election win","The Reichstag Fire","A 1923 rebellion"],"'Night of Broken Glass'."]}]},{"id":"geography","name":"Geography","weeks":[{"t":"Natural hazards: tectonics & weather (Paper 1)","intro":"This matches CGP revision guide pages 2–18. Learn plate margins, earthquake case studies, tropical storms and climate change, and use named examples.","how":["Know the three plate margins: destructive, constructive and conservative.","Compare earthquake effects and responses in a richer and a poorer country (Christchurch vs Nepal or Haiti).","Tropical storms form over oceans above 27°C, between 5° and 30° from the Equator.","Learn Typhoon Haiyan (2013) and a UK example: the 2015 Yorkshire floods.","Climate change: natural causes (orbit, volcanoes, solar output) and human causes (fossil fuels, farming, deforestation)."],"example":{"q":"Why do people continue to live near volcanoes?","steps":["Fertile volcanic soil is good for farming.","Tourism brings jobs and income.","Geothermal energy can be used.","Many believe an eruption is unlikely in their lifetime, or can't afford to move."],"a":"Farming, tourism, energy, and staying out of choice or necessity."},"walk":[{"h":"Plate margins","b":"Destructive: plates move together, the oceanic plate sinks, causing volcanoes and earthquakes. Constructive: plates move apart, with gentle volcanoes. Conservative: plates slide past each other, causing earthquakes but no volcanoes."},{"h":"Reducing risk","b":"The four Ps: Monitoring (seismometers), Prediction, Protection (earthquake-proof buildings, cross-bracing) and Planning (evacuation routes, drills). Richer countries can afford more, so death tolls are usually lower."},{"h":"Tropical storms","b":"Warm moist air rises and condenses, releasing energy. The Earth's spin makes the storm rotate. There's a calm eye, a wall of strong winds, heavy rain and a storm surge. Climate change may make storms stronger."}],"qs":[["At which margin do plates move apart?",["Constructive","Destructive","Conservative","Collision"],"New crust forms."],["Which margin has earthquakes but no volcanoes?",["Conservative","Constructive","Destructive","None"],"Plates slide past each other."],["Minimum sea temperature for tropical storms?",["27°C","10°C","40°C","0°C"],"Warm water provides energy."],["Which typhoon is the case study?",["Haiyan 2013","Katrina 2005","Sandy 2012","Andrew 1992"],"It hit the Philippines."],["What is the calm centre of a tropical storm?",["The eye","The wall","The surge","The front"],"Air sinks in the eye."],["Which is a primary effect of an earthquake?",["Buildings collapse","Disease spreads later","Economy declines","Homelessness for years"],"Primary = caused directly by the shaking."],["Which is a human cause of climate change?",["Burning fossil fuels","Volcanoes","Solar output","Orbital change"],"Releases CO₂."],["Which UK extreme weather event is on your list?",["Yorkshire floods 2015","Boscastle 2004","Somerset Levels 2014","Beast from the East"],"Named in the school's guide."],["Why do fewer people die in earthquakes in richer countries?",["Better buildings and emergency services","Fewer earthquakes","No people","Luck"],"More money for protection."],["What is a storm surge?",["A rise in sea level caused by a storm","Heavy rain","Strong wind only","A tsunami"],"Low pressure and winds push water onshore."]],"bonus":["Name a constructive margin feature.",["Shield volcanoes","Ocean trenches","Fold mountains","Composite volcanoes"],"Runny lava forms gentle slopes."]},{"t":"The living world (Paper 1)","intro":"CGP pages 20–31: ecosystems, tropical rainforests and hot deserts. Learn the features, adaptations, threats and sustainable management.","how":["An ecosystem has biotic (living) and abiotic (non-living) parts that interact.","Food chains and webs show energy flow. Nutrient cycling recycles matter.","Rainforest: hot, wet, layered (emergent, canopy, understorey, shrub), very high biodiversity.","Deforestation causes: logging, farming, mining, roads, energy. Use your case study (e.g. Malaysia or the Amazon).","Hot deserts: under 250 mm rain a year, extreme temperatures, adapted plants like cacti."],"example":{"q":"Explain how a cactus is adapted to the desert.","steps":["Spines instead of leaves reduce water loss.","Thick, waxy stems store water.","Shallow, wide roots collect rain quickly.","Long tap roots reach groundwater."],"a":"Adaptations reduce water loss and maximise water gain."},"walk":[{"h":"Rainforest adaptations","b":"Drip-tip leaves shed heavy rain. Buttress roots support tall trees in shallow soil. Lianas climb to the light. Animals like sloths move slowly to save energy and are camouflaged."},{"h":"Sustainable management","b":"Selective logging, replanting, ecotourism, international agreements, debt reduction (debt-for-nature swaps) and education all help protect rainforests while people still make a living."},{"h":"Desert opportunities and challenges","b":"Opportunities: farming with irrigation, mining, energy (solar) and tourism. Challenges: extreme heat, water supply and poor access. Desertification is caused by overgrazing, overcultivation, population growth and climate change."}],"qs":[["Which is an abiotic factor?",["Rainfall","Trees","Monkeys","Fungi"],"Non-living."],["Which rainforest layer gets the most light?",["Emergent","Canopy","Understorey","Shrub"],"The tallest trees stick out above."],["Why do rainforest leaves have drip tips?",["To shed heavy rain","To catch insects","To store water","To stay warm"],"Stops fungi and algae growing."],["What is the main cause of Amazon deforestation?",["Cattle ranching","Tourism","Schools","Rainfall"],"Land is cleared for farming."],["What are buttress roots for?",["Supporting tall trees","Storing water","Catching rain","Attracting animals"],"Soil is shallow."],["How much rain does a hot desert get?",["Less than 250 mm a year","Over 2000 mm","1000 mm","500 mm"],"Very dry."],["Which is a sustainable rainforest strategy?",["Selective logging","Clear-felling","Cattle ranching","Building dams everywhere"],"Only certain trees are cut."],["What is desertification?",["Land turning into desert","Making deserts wetter","Building in deserts","Desert tourism"],"Often from overgrazing."],["What do decomposers do?",["Break down dead material and recycle nutrients","Make food by photosynthesis","Eat only plants","Hunt animals"],"Fungi and bacteria."],["Which is a desert opportunity?",["Solar energy","Heavy rainfall","Deep fertile soils","Dense forest"],"Lots of sunshine."]],"bonus":["What is ecotourism?",["Tourism that protects the environment and benefits locals","Mass beach holidays","Logging tours","Cruises"],"e.g. small rainforest lodges."]},{"t":"UK physical landscapes: coasts & rivers (Paper 1)","intro":"CGP pages 37–57: the UK's main uplands and rivers, coastal processes and landforms, and river processes and landforms. Diagrams earn marks here.","how":["Coastal erosion processes: hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition, solution.","Longshore drift moves sediment along the coast in the direction of the prevailing wind.","Erosion landforms: headlands and bays, cave → arch → stack → stump, wave-cut platforms.","Deposition landforms: beaches, sand dunes, spits, bars.","River landforms: waterfalls and gorges (upper), meanders and ox-bow lakes (middle), floodplains and levées (lower)."],"example":{"q":"Describe how a waterfall forms.","steps":["A river flows over hard rock lying on top of soft rock.","The soft rock erodes faster, undercutting the hard rock.","A plunge pool forms by hydraulic action and abrasion.","The overhang collapses and the waterfall retreats, leaving a gorge."],"a":"Differential erosion, undercutting, collapse and retreat."},"walk":[{"h":"Coastal management","b":"Hard engineering: sea walls, groynes and rock armour. They're effective but expensive and ugly. Soft engineering: beach nourishment and dune regeneration, which are cheaper and natural. Managed retreat lets land flood deliberately."},{"h":"River transportation","b":"Traction is rolling boulders. Saltation is bouncing pebbles. Suspension is small particles carried in the water. Solution is dissolved minerals. Rivers deposit sediment when they slow down, such as on the inside of a meander."},{"h":"Flood risk","b":"A flood hydrograph shows lag time: the gap between peak rain and peak discharge. Urban areas, steep slopes and saturated ground shorten lag time. Flood defences include dams, embankments, flood relief channels and planting trees."}],"qs":[["Which erosion process is rocks hitting the cliff?",["Abrasion","Attrition","Solution","Hydraulic action"],"Like sandpaper."],["What moves sediment along a coast?",["Longshore drift","Saltation","Traction","Meandering"],"In a zig-zag."],["Correct sequence?",["Cave → arch → stack → stump","Arch → cave → stump → stack","Stack → arch → cave → stump","Stump → stack → arch → cave"],"Erosion widens the crack over time."],["What is a spit?",["A ridge of sand extending into the sea","A cliff","A cave","A waterfall"],"Formed by longshore drift."],["Which is hard engineering?",["Sea wall","Beach nourishment","Dune regeneration","Managed retreat"],"A built structure."],["Where is a waterfall usually found?",["Upper course","Lower course","Estuary","Floodplain"],"Steep land with vertical erosion."],["What forms when a meander is cut off?",["Ox-bow lake","Waterfall","Spit","Gorge"],"Erosion narrows the neck until it breaks through."],["What is lag time?",["Time between peak rainfall and peak discharge","Length of the river","Time to build a dam","Time between floods"],"Shown on a hydrograph."],["What are groynes for?",["Trapping sand from longshore drift","Stopping rain","Building cliffs","Draining rivers"],"Keeps beaches wide."],["How does a river transport small particles?",["Suspension","Traction","Abrasion","Attrition"],"Carried in the flow."]],"bonus":["What are levées?",["Raised riverbanks formed by deposition during floods","River cliffs","Waterfalls","Ox-bow lakes"],"Heavier sediment drops first near the river."]},{"t":"Urban issues, development & resources (Paper 2)","intro":"Paper 2 is human geography: cities (Mumbai and London), the development gap, Nigeria, and food, water and energy in the UK. CGP pages 67–99.","how":["Urbanisation is fastest in LICs and NEEs, due to rural-urban migration and natural increase.","Mumbai: opportunities (jobs, Dharavi's recycling) and challenges (slums, water, waste, traffic).","London: regeneration (e.g. Olympic Park), inequality, transport, sustainable living.","Measures of development: GNI per head, HDI, life expectancy, literacy, birth rate.","UK resources: rising food imports, local sourcing, water deficit in the south-east, energy shift to renewables."],"example":{"q":"Explain why Mumbai's population has grown so quickly.","steps":["Rural-urban migration: people move for jobs, education and services.","Push factors: drought and few jobs in rural areas.","Natural increase: many migrants are young, so the birth rate is high.","Better healthcare means fewer deaths."],"a":"Migration and natural increase."},"walk":[{"h":"The development gap","b":"Causes include physical factors (climate, hazards), economic factors (trade, debt) and history (colonialism). Ways to reduce the gap: aid, fair trade, debt relief, tourism, microfinance and industrial development."},{"h":"Nigeria","b":"Nigeria is an NEE with a fast-growing economy based on oil but with big inequality. TNCs like Shell bring jobs but also oil spills. Aid helps but can be lost to corruption. Make sure you learn the case study your teacher used."},{"h":"Resource management","b":"The UK imports about half its food. Local sourcing reduces food miles. The north and west have a water surplus and the south-east a deficit, so water transfer is debated. Energy is moving from coal to renewables and gas."}],"qs":[["What is urbanisation?",["An increase in the share of people living in towns and cities","Building in the countryside","People leaving cities","Farming"],"Fastest in LICs and NEEs."],["What is Dharavi?",["A large informal settlement in Mumbai","A London borough","A Nigerian city","A river"],"Known for its recycling industry."],["What does HDI combine?",["Income, education and life expectancy","Only GNI","Birth and death rates","Literacy only"],"Human Development Index."],["Which is a push factor?",["Drought in rural areas","Jobs in the city","Better schools","Bright lights"],"Pushes people away."],["What does NEE stand for?",["Newly Emerging Economy","New European Economy","National Energy Exchange","Northern Economic Engine"],"e.g. Nigeria and India."],["Which area of the UK has a water deficit?",["South-east England","Scotland","Wales","Lake District"],"High population, lower rainfall."],["What are food miles?",["Distance food travels from farm to plate","Calories in food","Farm size","Price of food"],"More miles, more emissions."],["What is fair trade?",["Producers in poorer countries get a fair price","Free trade with no tax","Trading only locally","Bartering"],"It helps reduce the gap."],["Which London project is an example of regeneration?",["Olympic Park, Stratford","Dharavi","Lagos port","Hoover Dam"],"Built for 2012."],["Which is a renewable energy source?",["Wind","Coal","Gas","Oil"],"The UK has many offshore wind farms."]],"bonus":["What is microfinance?",["Small loans to people in poor countries to start businesses","Bank bailouts","Big government loans","Credit cards"],"e.g. Grameen Bank."]}]},{"id":"computing","name":"Computer Science","weeks":[{"t":"Systems architecture, memory & storage","intro":"This is the start of OCR Paper 1 (Computer systems). Learn what the CPU does, the parts inside it, how RAM and ROM differ, and how to choose storage.","how":["The CPU fetches, decodes and executes instructions: the fetch-execute cycle.","Von Neumann architecture stores data and instructions together in memory.","Registers: PC (next instruction address), MAR, MDR, accumulator. ALU does maths and logic. CU controls everything.","Performance depends on clock speed, number of cores and cache size.","RAM is volatile and holds programs in use. ROM is non-volatile and holds the boot-up instructions."],"example":{"q":"Explain why a computer with more cache may run faster.","steps":["Cache is very fast memory inside or near the CPU.","It stores frequently used instructions and data.","The CPU can fetch from cache faster than from RAM.","So less waiting time, and programs run faster."],"a":"Faster access to frequently used data."},"walk":[{"h":"Virtual memory and flash","b":"When RAM is full, part of secondary storage is used as virtual memory. This is slow, because data must be swapped. Flash memory is solid state, non-volatile and used in SSDs and USB sticks."},{"h":"Secondary storage","b":"Magnetic (HDD): cheap with large capacity, but has moving parts. Solid state (SSD): fast, durable and silent, but costs more per GB. Optical (CD/DVD): cheap and portable, but slow and small. Compare on capacity, speed, portability, durability, reliability and cost."},{"h":"Embedded systems","b":"An embedded system is a computer built into a larger device for one job, like a washing machine, microwave or car engine controller. They're usually small, cheap and low-power."}],"qs":[["What does the ALU do?",["Arithmetic and logic operations","Stores files","Controls the monitor","Connects to Wi-Fi"],"Arithmetic Logic Unit."],["Which register holds the address of the next instruction?",["Program Counter","MDR","Accumulator","Cache"],"The PC increments each cycle."],["RAM is…",["Volatile","Non-volatile","Read-only","Secondary storage"],"Data is lost when power is off."],["What does ROM store?",["Boot-up instructions (BIOS)","Your documents","Open programs","Web pages"],"Needed to start the computer."],["Which improves CPU performance?",["More cores","A bigger monitor","A faster printer","More USB ports"],"More instructions can run at once."],["What is virtual memory?",["Secondary storage used as extra RAM","Cache","The cloud","ROM"],"Used when RAM is full."],["Which storage has no moving parts?",["SSD","HDD","Floppy disk","Tape"],"Solid state."],["Clock speed is measured in…",["GHz","GB","Mbps","Pixels"],"Cycles per second."],["A washing machine controller is an example of…",["An embedded system","A server","Cloud storage","An operating system"],"A dedicated computer inside a device."],["In Von Neumann architecture, instructions and data are stored…",["Together in the same memory","Separately","Only in cache","Only in ROM"],"The stored program concept."]],"bonus":["What is the MDR?",["Memory Data Register, holding data fetched from or sent to memory","Main Disk Reader","Multiple Data Router","Memory Delete Register"],"It works with the MAR."]},{"t":"Networks, protocols & security","intro":"Networks and security make up a big chunk of Paper 1. Learn LANs and WANs, topologies, protocols, and the threats and defences.","how":["LAN covers a small area, like a school. WAN covers a large area. The internet is the biggest WAN.","Star topology: all devices connect to a central switch. Mesh: devices connect to each other.","Protocols are rules: HTTP/HTTPS (web), SMTP (send email), POP/IMAP (receive email), FTP (files).","IP addresses identify devices on a network. MAC addresses are unique hardware IDs.","Threats: malware, phishing, brute force, denial of service, SQL injection, social engineering."],"example":{"q":"Give one advantage and one disadvantage of a star topology.","steps":["Advantage: if one cable fails, only that device is affected.","Advantage: easy to add new devices.","Disadvantage: if the central switch fails, the whole network fails.","Disadvantage: needs a lot of cable."],"a":"Reliable per device, but depends on the switch."},"walk":[{"h":"Client-server vs peer-to-peer","b":"In client-server, a central server stores files and manages security, which is easier to back up but expensive. In peer-to-peer, all computers are equal and share files directly, which is cheap but harder to manage."},{"h":"Layers","b":"Networking is split into layers, each with its own job and protocols. Layers can be developed independently and make problems easier to fix. The TCP/IP model has application, transport, internet and link layers."},{"h":"Preventing attacks","b":"Firewalls block unauthorised traffic. Anti-malware finds and removes malicious software. Encryption scrambles data. Strong passwords and user access levels limit damage. Penetration testing finds weaknesses before attackers do."}],"qs":[["What does LAN stand for?",["Local Area Network","Large Access Network","Linked Area Node","Long Area Network"],"Covers a small area."],["Which protocol sends email?",["SMTP","HTTP","FTP","IMAP"],"Simple Mail Transfer Protocol."],["What does the S in HTTPS add?",["Encryption (secure)","Speed","Storage","Sound"],"Data is encrypted."],["In a star topology, everything connects to…",["A central switch","Each other","A single cable","The cloud"],"The hub of the star."],["What is phishing?",["Fake emails tricking people into giving details","A virus","A firewall","A protocol"],"A form of social engineering."],["What does a firewall do?",["Blocks unauthorised network traffic","Speeds up Wi-Fi","Stores passwords","Cools the CPU"],"It filters incoming and outgoing data."],["Which address is unique to hardware?",["MAC address","IP address","Email address","URL"],"Set by the manufacturer."],["What is a DoS attack?",["Flooding a server so it can't respond","Stealing passwords","Guessing a PIN","Sending spam"],"Denial of service."],["What is SQL injection?",["Entering code into a form to access a database","A type of cable","A backup method","A virus scanner"],"Prevented by validating input."],["What can affect network performance?",["Number of users and bandwidth","Screen size","Keyboard type","Printer ink"],"More users share the bandwidth."]],"bonus":["What is penetration testing?",["Authorised attacks to find weaknesses","Testing cables","Checking printers","A virus"],"Ethical hacking."]},{"t":"Data representation & logic","intro":"Binary, hex and data sizes are guaranteed marks if you practise. This week also covers images, sound, compression and logic gates.","how":["Units: bit → nibble (4 bits) → byte (8 bits) → KB (1000 bytes) → MB → GB → TB → PB.","Binary place values for 8 bits: 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1.","Hex uses 0–9 then A–F (A = 10 … F = 15). Each hex digit = 4 bits.","A left binary shift multiplies by 2. A right shift divides by 2.","Image file size = width × height × colour depth (bits). Sound = sample rate × bit depth × seconds."],"example":{"q":"Convert the denary number 75 to 8-bit binary and to hex.","steps":["75 = 64 + 8 + 2 + 1.","Binary: 0100 1011.","Split into nibbles: 0100 = 4, 1011 = 11 = B.","Hex: 4B."],"a":"01001011 = 4B"},"walk":[{"h":"Binary addition and overflow","b":"Add right to left: 1 + 1 = 10 (write 0 carry 1), 1 + 1 + 1 = 11. If the answer needs a 9th bit in an 8-bit system, that's an overflow error."},{"h":"Characters","b":"A character set maps characters to binary codes. ASCII uses 7 bits (128 characters). Unicode uses more bits, so it can represent every language and emojis. More bits per character means more characters can be represented."},{"h":"Logic gates","b":"AND outputs 1 only if both inputs are 1. OR outputs 1 if either is 1. NOT flips the input. Draw truth tables listing every input combination: 2 inputs give 4 rows."}],"qs":[["How many bits in a byte?",["8","4","16","1000"],"A nibble is 4."],["What is 1010 in denary?",["10","12","8","5"],"8 + 2 = 10."],["What is the hex digit for 12?",["C","B","D","12"],"A = 10, B = 11, C = 12."],["What is 255 in hex?",["FF","EE","100","F5"],"15 × 16 + 15 = 255."],["A left shift of 1 place…",["Multiplies by 2","Divides by 2","Adds 1","Does nothing"],"Each place doubles."],["Which has more characters available?",["Unicode","ASCII","They're equal","Neither"],"More bits per character."],["Output of 1 AND 0?",["0","1","2","10"],"AND needs both to be 1."],["Output of NOT 1?",["0","1","2","11"],"NOT flips the input."],["Increasing colour depth makes an image file…",["Larger","Smaller","Unchanged","Black and white"],"More bits per pixel."],["What is lossy compression?",["Permanently removes some data","Keeps all data","Adds data","Encrypts data"],"e.g. JPEG and MP3."]],"bonus":["Image 100 × 100 pixels, 8-bit colour. Size in bytes?",["10 000 bytes","80 000 bytes","800 bytes","1000 bytes"],"100 × 100 × 8 = 80 000 bits ÷ 8 = 10 000 bytes."]},{"t":"Algorithms, programming & testing (Paper 2)","intro":"Paper 2 tests computational thinking, algorithms and programming. Learn the standard searching and sorting algorithms, the three programming constructs and how to test code.","how":["Computational thinking: abstraction (remove unnecessary detail), decomposition (break into parts), algorithmic thinking.","Linear search checks each item in turn. Binary search needs a sorted list and halves it each time.","Sorting: bubble sort (swap pairs), merge sort (split then merge), insertion sort (insert into place).","Three constructs: sequence, selection (if), iteration (for and while loops).","Testing: iterative (during development) and final (at the end). Use normal, boundary, invalid and erroneous data."],"example":{"q":"Binary search for 23 in [3, 8, 15, 23, 42, 57, 61].","steps":["Middle item is 23 (position 4).","23 = 23, so found!","If the target were 42: 42 > 23, so search the right half [42, 57, 61].","Middle is 57; 42 < 57, so check 42: found."],"a":"Found 23 in one comparison."},"walk":[{"h":"Defensive design","b":"Anticipate misuse: input validation (range, type, presence, length checks), authentication (passwords), and maintainability (comments, sensible variable names, indentation, subprograms)."},{"h":"Errors","b":"A syntax error breaks the rules of the language, so the code won't run (like a missing bracket). A logic error means the code runs but gives the wrong result (like using > instead of >=)."},{"h":"Translators","b":"High-level languages are easy for humans. Low-level languages are close to machine code. A compiler translates the whole program at once into an executable. An interpreter translates and runs line by line. An assembler translates assembly language."}],"qs":[["What does binary search need?",["A sorted list","An unsorted list","Exactly 10 items","Strings only"],"It relies on order."],["What is abstraction?",["Removing unnecessary detail","Breaking into parts","Writing code","Testing"],"e.g. a tube map."],["Which construct is a loop?",["Iteration","Selection","Sequence","Declaration"],"for and while."],["What does bubble sort do?",["Swaps adjacent items that are in the wrong order","Splits the list in half","Searches the list","Deletes duplicates"],"Repeats passes until no swaps."],["Boundary test data for 1–10 includes…",["1 and 10","5","−3","'hello'"],"Values at the edges."],["A missing bracket causes a…",["Syntax error","Logic error","Runtime success","Overflow"],"The code won't run."],["What does a compiler do?",["Translates the whole program at once","Runs line by line","Draws flowcharts","Tests data"],"It creates an executable file."],["What is decomposition?",["Breaking a problem into smaller parts","Removing detail","Sorting data","Encrypting data"],"Easier to solve."],["Which is a validation check?",["Range check","Bubble sort","Compiler","Binary shift"],"Checks input is within limits."],["Which SQL keyword picks the columns to show?",["SELECT","FROM","WHERE","ORDER"],"SELECT name FROM students WHERE age > 15."]],"bonus":["Which sort is generally fastest on large lists?",["Merge sort","Bubble sort","Insertion sort","Linear sort"],"Divide and conquer."]}]},{"id":"business","name":"Business Studies","weeks":[{"t":"Enterprise & spotting an opportunity","intro":"Theme 1 starts with why businesses exist and how entrepreneurs spot opportunities. Learn the key terms and use the case study in every answer.","how":["An entrepreneur takes a risk to start a business, hoping for reward (profit, independence).","Businesses start because of changes in technology, what consumers want, and products becoming obsolete.","Market research: primary (surveys, focus groups) and secondary (internet, reports).","Market segmentation splits customers by age, location, income, lifestyle or gender.","A market map shows where competitors are and finds gaps in the market."],"example":{"q":"Explain one benefit of market research for a new café. (3 marks)","steps":["Point: it identifies customer needs.","Because: the owner learns what drinks and prices local customers want.","Therefore: the café is more likely to attract customers and make sales."],"a":"Point, linked to context, with two connected steps."},"walk":[{"h":"Business objectives","b":"Financial objectives: survival, profit, sales, market share, financial security. Non-financial: social objectives, personal satisfaction, challenge, independence. New businesses usually aim to survive first."},{"h":"Competitive environment","b":"Strengths and weaknesses of competitors include price, quality, location, product range and customer service. Strong competition may force a business to lower prices or improve quality."},{"h":"Answer structure","b":"3-mark Explain: a point plus two linked strands. 6-mark Analyse: two developed points in context. 9-mark Justify: choose an option, justify it and add 'it depends on'. 12-mark Evaluate: both sides and a justified conclusion."}],"qs":[["What is an entrepreneur?",["Someone who takes a risk to start a business","A bank manager","A customer","An employee"],"They organise resources and take risks."],["Which is primary research?",["A questionnaire","Government statistics","Internet articles","Market reports"],"Collected first-hand."],["What is market segmentation?",["Dividing a market into groups of similar customers","Cutting prices","Opening new shops","Merging businesses"],"Helps target customers."],["What does a market map show?",["Where products are positioned and gaps in the market","Shop locations","Profit","Staff numbers"],"Usually two axes, like price and quality."],["Which is a non-financial objective?",["Personal satisfaction","Profit","Sales growth","Market share"],"Not measured in money."],["What do new businesses usually aim for first?",["Survival","Global expansion","Takeovers","Becoming a PLC"],"Many fail in year one."],["What is a reward of entrepreneurship?",["Profit","Loss","Bankruptcy","Stress"],"Also independence."],["Which is secondary research?",["Using ONS statistics","A focus group","Observation","Interviews"],"Collected by someone else."],["How long for a 3-mark Explain question?",["3–4 minutes","15 minutes","1 minute","20 minutes"],"From the school's guide."],["Obsolete means…",["Out of date and no longer wanted","Brand new","Very popular","Expensive"],"Creates opportunities for new products."]],"bonus":["What is 'adding value'?",["Making a product worth more than its inputs","Raising prices randomly","Lowering quality","Cutting staff"],"e.g. branding, convenience, design."]},{"t":"Business finance","intro":"Finance questions include calculations every year. Learn the formulas from the school's formula sheet and practise interpreting the answers.","how":["Revenue = price × quantity. Total costs = fixed + variable costs.","Profit = revenue − total costs.","Break-even output = fixed costs ÷ (price − variable cost per unit).","Margin of safety = actual sales − break-even sales.","Net cash flow = inflows − outflows. Closing balance = opening balance + net cash flow."],"example":{"q":"Fixed costs £2000, price £10, variable cost £6. Find break-even output.","steps":["Contribution per unit = £10 − £6 = £4.","Break-even = fixed costs ÷ contribution.","£2000 ÷ £4 = 500 units."],"a":"500 units"},"walk":[{"h":"Profit margins","b":"Gross profit = revenue − cost of sales. Net profit = gross profit − other operating expenses (overheads) and interest. Margins are shown as percentages: gross profit ÷ revenue × 100."},{"h":"Sources of finance","b":"New businesses: personal savings, family and friends, bank loan, crowdfunding, government grants. Growing businesses: retained profit, selling assets, share capital, loans. Short-term: overdraft and trade credit."},{"h":"Cash flow","b":"Cash flow is not the same as profit. A profitable business can fail if it runs out of cash. Fix problems by using an overdraft, delaying payments, chasing debtors or cutting costs."}],"qs":[["Revenue when 200 items sell at £5?",["£1000","£205","£40","£500"],"200 × 5."],["Total costs if fixed = £500 and variable = £300?",["£800","£200","£150 000","£500"],"Add them."],["Break-even: FC £1200, price £8, VC £5?",["400 units","240 units","150 units","100 units"],"1200 ÷ 3."],["Sales 600, break-even 400. Margin of safety?",["200 units","1000 units","1.5","400 units"],"600 − 400."],["Inflows £5000, outflows £3500, opening balance £1000. Closing balance?",["£2500","£1500","£9500","£500"],"1000 + 1500."],["Which is a fixed cost?",["Rent","Raw materials","Packaging","Delivery per item"],"Doesn't change with output."],["Which is a short-term source of finance?",["Overdraft","Share capital","Mortgage","Retained profit over 10 years"],"Flexible borrowing."],["What is crowdfunding?",["Many people each invest a small amount online","A bank loan","Selling shares on the stock market","A grant"],"e.g. Kickstarter."],["Can a profitable business run out of cash?",["Yes","No","Only PLCs","Only in winter"],"Timing of payments matters."],["Gross profit = revenue minus…",["Cost of sales","All expenses","Tax","Dividends"],"Net profit then subtracts overheads."]],"bonus":["Revenue £50 000, gross profit £20 000. Gross profit margin?",["40%","250%","20%","60%"],"20 000 ÷ 50 000 × 100."]},{"t":"Marketing decisions","intro":"Marketing is about the 4Ps: product, price, promotion and place. You need to say how the mix should fit the business in the case study.","how":["Product: design mix (function, aesthetics, cost), product life cycle, extension strategies.","Price: penetration (low to start), skimming (high to start), competitive, cost-plus.","Promotion: advertising, sponsorship, special offers, social media, branding.","Place: shops, e-commerce, distribution channels.","The 4Ps must work together and suit the target market."],"example":{"q":"Justify whether a new tech gadget should use price skimming. (9 marks)","steps":["Answer: yes, price skimming.","Justify: early adopters will pay high prices for new technology, recovering R&D costs.","It depends on: competitors launching similar products soon.","Most important: whether the product is genuinely unique."],"a":"Answer, justification, 'it depends' and the most important factor."},"walk":[{"h":"Product life cycle","b":"Stages: development, introduction, growth, maturity, decline. Extension strategies keep sales going in maturity: new features, new packaging, new markets, price cuts or advertising campaigns."},{"h":"Promotion choices","b":"Small businesses use cheaper methods: social media, leaflets, local press, special offers. Large businesses use TV and sponsorship. Targeted social media ads reach specific segments cheaply."},{"h":"Place and e-commerce","b":"Selling online means wider reach and lower costs but more competition. Distribution channels: producer → consumer, producer → retailer → consumer, or producer → wholesaler → retailer → consumer."}],"qs":[["What are the 4Ps?",["Product, price, promotion, place","People, profit, plan, place","Price, people, product, power","Plan, price, promote, pack"],"The marketing mix."],["Which strategy starts with a low price to gain market share?",["Penetration pricing","Price skimming","Cost-plus","Premium pricing"],"Then raises the price later."],["What is an extension strategy?",["A way to keep a product selling in maturity","Launching a product","Closing a shop","Hiring staff"],"e.g. a new flavour."],["Which stage has the highest sales?",["Maturity","Introduction","Development","Decline"],"Sales peak."],["Which is a cheap promotion for a small business?",["Social media","TV advertising","Sponsoring the Premier League","Billboards worldwide"],"Low cost, targeted."],["What is e-commerce?",["Selling online","Selling in shops","Bartering","Wholesale"],"Wider reach."],["Design mix includes function, aesthetics and…",["Cost","Location","Staff","Tax"],"Three elements."],["Price skimming suits…",["New, unique products","Basic supermarket goods","Old products","Products in decline"],"Early adopters pay more."],["What is branding?",["Giving a product a distinct identity","Pricing low","Delivering products","Producing goods"],"Helps add value."],["A 12-mark question needs…",["Balanced arguments and a justified conclusion","One point","A calculation only","A definition only"],"Evaluate."]],"bonus":["What is the 'it depends on' part of a 9-mark answer?",["A conditional factor that could change your decision","A definition","A formula","A diagram"],"e.g. 'it depends on how fast competitors react'."]},{"t":"Operations & human resources","intro":"Theme 2 also covers how businesses produce goods, manage quality and stock, and recruit, train and motivate staff.","how":["Production: job (one-off), batch (groups), flow (continuous mass production).","Quality control checks products at the end. Quality assurance checks at every stage.","Stock: buffer stock, just in time (JIT), and bar gate stock graphs.","Recruitment: internal (existing staff) or external (job adverts). Use job descriptions and person specifications.","Motivation: financial (pay, bonus, commission) and non-financial (training, job rotation, praise)."],"example":{"q":"Explain one advantage of just-in-time stock for a restaurant. (3 marks)","steps":["Point: less food waste.","Because: ingredients arrive just before they're needed.","Therefore: less food goes off, so costs are lower."],"a":"Point plus two linked strands in context."},"walk":[{"h":"Organisational structures","b":"Tall structures have many layers and narrow spans of control, so communication is slow. Flat structures have few layers and wide spans of control, so communication is quicker. Centralised means decisions are made at the top. Decentralised means managers are given power."},{"h":"Training","b":"Formal training is planned courses. Informal training is learning on the job. Training improves skills and motivation and lowers staff turnover, but costs money and staff may leave for better jobs."},{"h":"Supply chain","b":"Choose suppliers on price, quality, reliability and delivery time. Good procurement keeps costs down and customers happy."}],"qs":[["Making cars on an assembly line is…",["Flow production","Job production","Batch production","Craft"],"Continuous mass production."],["What is quality assurance?",["Checking quality at every stage","Checking only at the end","Cheap materials","A marketing method"],"Prevents faults."],["What is JIT?",["Stock arrives just when it's needed","Holding large stocks","A training scheme","Selling online"],"Reduces storage costs."],["A person specification describes…",["Qualities and skills the ideal candidate needs","The tasks of the job","The salary only","The company history"],"Used to shortlist."],["Which is a financial motivator?",["Commission","Praise","Job rotation","Training"],"Paid for sales made."],["A flat structure has…",["Few layers","Many layers","No staff","Only one manager"],"Faster communication."],["What is buffer stock?",["Minimum stock kept for emergencies","Unsold stock","Stock in transit","Stock for sale today"],"Prevents running out."],["Which is internal recruitment?",["Promoting an existing employee","Advertising in a newspaper","Using a job centre","LinkedIn advert"],"Cheaper and quicker."],["What is staff turnover?",["Rate at which staff leave","Total sales","Profit","Stock movement"],"High turnover is costly."],["Making 100 loaves then switching to rolls is…",["Batch production","Flow production","Job production","JIT"],"Groups of identical products."]],"bonus":["What is span of control?",["Number of people a manager is directly responsible for","The size of the factory","A pricing method","A legal limit"],"Wide spans suit flat structures."]}]},{"id":"french","name":"French","weeks":[{"t":"Identity & relationships","intro":"Theme 1, Topic 1: describing yourself, family and friends. Learn key vocabulary, present tense verbs and opinions for speaking and writing.","how":["Use être for descriptions: je suis grand, elle est gentille.","Adjectives agree: add -e for feminine, -s for plural (petit, petite, petits, petites).","Relationship phrases: je m'entends bien avec… (I get on well with), je me dispute avec… (I argue with).","Give opinions with reasons: je pense que…, parce que…, car…","Add another tense to boost your grade: quand j'étais jeune… (when I was young)."],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'I get on well with my sister because she is funny.'","steps":["I get on well with = Je m'entends bien avec","my sister = ma sœur","because she is funny = parce qu'elle est drôle"],"a":"Je m'entends bien avec ma sœur parce qu'elle est drôle."},"walk":[{"h":"Family words","b":"Le père (father), la mère (mother), le frère (brother), la sœur (sister), les grands-parents (grandparents), le demi-frère (half-brother), l'oncle (uncle), la tante (aunt), le cousin/la cousine (cousin)."},{"h":"Describing personality","b":"Sympa (nice), drôle (funny), bavard(e) (chatty), paresseux/paresseuse (lazy), timide (shy), égoïste (selfish), travailleur/travailleuse (hard-working), compréhensif/compréhensive (understanding)."},{"h":"Role models","b":"Mon modèle est… parce qu'il/elle est courageux/courageuse et travaille dur. Il/elle m'inspire car… This topic often comes up in the photo card and in writing."}],"qs":[["'Je m'entends bien avec' means…",["I get on well with","I argue with","I live with","I look like"],"s'entendre = to get on."],["Feminine of 'grand'?",["grande","grands","grandes","grand"],"Add -e."],["'Ma sœur' means…",["My sister","My brother","My mother","My aunt"],"La sœur."],["'Il est paresseux' means…",["He is lazy","He is funny","He is shy","He is tall"],"Paresseux = lazy."],["'Je me dispute avec mon frère' means…",["I argue with my brother","I play with my brother","I like my brother","I live with my brother"],"Se disputer = to argue."],["Which means 'because'?",["parce que","mais","aussi","donc"],"Car also works."],["'Quand j'étais jeune' means…",["When I was young","When I am old","When I will be young","If I were young"],"Imperfect tense."],["'Les grands-parents' are…",["Grandparents","Parents","Big parents","Cousins"],"Grand-père and grand-mère."],["'Sympa' means…",["Nice","Sad","Strict","Silly"],"Short for sympathique."],["How do you say 'she is funny'?",["Elle est drôle","Il est drôle","Elle a drôle","Elle drôle"],"Être for descriptions."]],"bonus":["'Mon modèle m'inspire' means…",["My role model inspires me","My model is boring","My mother inspires me","I inspire my model"],"Inspirer = to inspire."]},{"t":"Healthy living, education & work","intro":"Theme 1, Topics 2 and 3: health, school and future jobs. Use a range of tenses: what you do, what you did, and what you will do.","how":["Health: pour être en forme, il faut… (to be healthy, you must…) + infinitive.","Negatives: je ne fume pas, je ne bois jamais d'alcool.","School: ma matière préférée est… parce que… Les profs sont…","Future tense: je vais étudier (near future), j'étudierai (simple future).","Conditional: je voudrais être médecin (I would like to be a doctor)."],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'Next year I am going to study science.'","steps":["Next year = L'année prochaine","I am going to study = je vais étudier","science = les sciences"],"a":"L'année prochaine, je vais étudier les sciences."},"walk":[{"h":"Health vocabulary","b":"Manger sainement (eat healthily), faire de l'exercice (exercise), dormir huit heures (sleep 8 hours), éviter le sucre (avoid sugar), le tabac (tobacco), la drogue (drugs), le stress."},{"h":"School","b":"Les matières (subjects): les maths, l'anglais, l'histoire, la géographie, l'informatique. L'uniforme scolaire, les devoirs (homework), la pression (pressure), les examens."},{"h":"Jobs and the future","b":"Je voudrais devenir… (I would like to become…): avocat (lawyer), ingénieur (engineer), médecin (doctor), professeur (teacher). Je voudrais aller à l'université, faire un apprentissage (apprenticeship), voyager."}],"qs":[["'Pour être en forme, il faut…' means…",["To be healthy, you must…","I am very healthy","I don't exercise","It is fun"],"Il faut + infinitive."],["'Je ne fume jamais' means…",["I never smoke","I always smoke","I smoke sometimes","I don't eat"],"Ne… jamais = never."],["'Je vais étudier' is which tense?",["Near future","Past","Present","Imperfect"],"Aller + infinitive."],["'Je voudrais être médecin' means…",["I would like to be a doctor","I am a doctor","I was a doctor","I will see a doctor"],"Conditional."],["'Les devoirs' are…",["Homework","Teachers","Exams","Subjects"],"Faire ses devoirs."],["'L'informatique' is…",["Computing","Information","Maths","Science"],"ICT and computer science."],["'Un apprentissage' is…",["An apprenticeship","An apartment","An exam","A teacher"],"Training while working."],["'Manger sainement' means…",["To eat healthily","To eat a lot","To eat quickly","To not eat"],"Sain = healthy."],["Simple future of 'travailler' (je)?",["je travaillerai","je travaillais","j'ai travaillé","je travaille"],"Infinitive + -ai."],["'La pression' at school means…",["Pressure","Lessons","Uniform","Break time"],"Stress from exams."]],"bonus":["'L'année dernière, j'ai fait du sport' — which tense?",["Perfect (past)","Future","Present","Conditional"],"Avoir + past participle."]},{"t":"Free time, festivals & celebrities","intro":"Theme 2: popular culture. Talk about hobbies, French customs and festivals, and celebrity culture, with opinions and past tense examples.","how":["Faire + du/de la/des for activities: je fais du vélo, je fais de la natation.","Jouer à for sports, jouer de for instruments: je joue au foot, je joue de la guitare.","Perfect tense: j'ai regardé (I watched), je suis allé(e) (I went).","French festivals: Noël, Pâques, le 14 juillet (Bastille Day), la Chandeleur.","Celebrities: ils sont de bons/mauvais modèles parce que…"],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'Last weekend I went to the cinema with my friends.'","steps":["Last weekend = Le week-end dernier","I went = je suis allé(e)","to the cinema = au cinéma","with my friends = avec mes amis"],"a":"Le week-end dernier, je suis allé(e) au cinéma avec mes amis."},"walk":[{"h":"Être verbs in the perfect","b":"Verbs of movement use être: aller, venir, partir, arriver, sortir, entrer, rester, tomber, naître, mourir. The past participle agrees: elle est allée, ils sont partis."},{"h":"Festivals","b":"Le 14 juillet celebrates the French Revolution with fireworks (les feux d'artifice) and parades (le défilé). Noël: le réveillon (Christmas Eve meal), la bûche de Noël. La Chandeleur on 2 February: people eat crêpes."},{"h":"Celebrity culture","b":"Les célébrités ont beaucoup d'influence sur les jeunes. Certaines célébrités font du bénévolat (charity work). D'autres donnent un mauvais exemple. Use opinions: à mon avis, je trouve que…"}],"qs":[["'Je fais de la natation' means…",["I go swimming","I play football","I go cycling","I do dance"],"La natation = swimming."],["How do you say 'I play the guitar'?",["Je joue de la guitare","Je joue à la guitare","Je fais la guitare","Je suis guitare"],"Jouer de + instrument."],["'Je suis allé' is the perfect tense of…",["aller","avoir","être","faire"],"Aller uses être."],["What is celebrated on 14 July?",["Bastille Day","Christmas","Easter","New Year"],"La fête nationale."],["'Les feux d'artifice' are…",["Fireworks","Parades","Presents","Candles"],"Seen on 14 July."],["What do the French eat at la Chandeleur?",["Crêpes","Turkey","Chocolate eggs","Fish"],"On 2 February."],["'Elle est partie' — why the extra -e?",["Agreement with a feminine subject","Spelling mistake","Future tense","It's plural"],"Être verbs agree."],["'J'ai regardé un film' means…",["I watched a film","I will watch a film","I am watching a film","I like films"],"Perfect tense."],["'Le bénévolat' is…",["Voluntary work","Fame","Money","Music"],"Unpaid charity work."],["'À mon avis' means…",["In my opinion","In my house","On my birthday","At my school"],"Useful opinion phrase."]],"bonus":["'Le réveillon' is…",["A special late-night meal on Christmas Eve or New Year's Eve","An alarm clock","A parade","A cake"],"From réveiller, to wake."]},{"t":"Travel, technology & the environment","intro":"Theme 3: communication and the world around us. Talk about holidays, social media and technology, and environmental problems and solutions.","how":["Holidays: je suis allé(e) en France, j'ai logé dans un hôtel, il faisait beau.","Imperfect for descriptions in the past: il faisait chaud, c'était génial.","Technology: j'utilise mon portable pour… / les réseaux sociaux (social media).","Environment: la pollution, le réchauffement climatique, le recyclage.","Solutions: on devrait… (we should…) + infinitive, e.g. on devrait recycler."],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'We should use public transport to protect the environment.'","steps":["We should use = On devrait utiliser","public transport = les transports en commun","to protect the environment = pour protéger l'environnement"],"a":"On devrait utiliser les transports en commun pour protéger l'environnement."},"walk":[{"h":"Holidays","b":"Je suis allé(e) en Espagne avec ma famille. Nous avons voyagé en avion. Nous avons logé dans un camping. Il faisait très chaud. C'était inoubliable (unforgettable). L'année prochaine, je voudrais aller au Canada."},{"h":"Pros and cons of technology","b":"Avantages: on peut rester en contact, c'est pratique, on peut faire des recherches. Inconvénients: le cyber-harcèlement (cyberbullying), c'est addictif, on perd du temps, les fausses nouvelles (fake news)."},{"h":"Where people live","b":"J'habite dans une grande ville / un petit village. En ville il y a beaucoup de choses à faire, mais il y a trop de bruit (noise) et de circulation (traffic). À la campagne c'est calme mais il n'y a rien à faire."}],"qs":[["'Il faisait beau' means…",["The weather was nice","It is raining","It will be cold","It was snowing"],"Imperfect for description."],["'Les réseaux sociaux' are…",["Social networks / social media","Social workers","Social events","Society"],"e.g. Instagram, TikTok."],["'On devrait recycler' means…",["We should recycle","We recycled","We will recycle","We don't recycle"],"Devoir in the conditional."],["'Le réchauffement climatique' is…",["Global warming","Climate science","Hot weather","Central heating"],"Réchauffer = to warm up."],["'Le cyber-harcèlement' is…",["Cyberbullying","Cybersecurity","Online shopping","Computer games"],"A disadvantage of technology."],["'Les transports en commun' are…",["Public transport","Cars","Planes","Bicycles"],"Bus, train, metro."],["'C'était inoubliable' means…",["It was unforgettable","It was boring","It is unknown","It was expensive"],"Inoubliable = unforgettable."],["'J'ai logé dans un hôtel' means…",["I stayed in a hotel","I worked in a hotel","I booked a hotel","I like hotels"],"Loger = to stay."],["'Il y a trop de bruit' means…",["There is too much noise","There is too much traffic","There is nothing to do","It's too quiet"],"Le bruit = noise."],["'Mon portable' is…",["My mobile phone","My laptop","My bag","My TV"],"Un téléphone portable."]],"bonus":["'Si j'avais de l'argent, je voyagerais' means…",["If I had money, I would travel","I had money and travelled","I will travel with money","I travel for money"],"Si + imperfect, then conditional."]}]},{"id":"spanish","name":"Spanish","weeks":[{"t":"Identity & relationships","intro":"Theme 1, Topic 1: describing yourself, family and friends. Learn ser vs estar, adjective agreement and relationship phrases.","how":["Ser for permanent traits: soy alto, es simpática.","Estar for moods and location: estoy cansado, está en casa.","Adjectives agree: alto, alta, altos, altas.","Relationships: me llevo bien con… (I get on well with), me peleo con… (I argue with).","Add opinions with reasons: creo que…, porque…, ya que…"],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'I get on well with my brother because he is kind.'","steps":["I get on well with = Me llevo bien con","my brother = mi hermano","because he is kind = porque es amable"],"a":"Me llevo bien con mi hermano porque es amable."},"walk":[{"h":"Family words","b":"El padre, la madre, el hermano, la hermana, los abuelos (grandparents), el tío (uncle), la tía (aunt), el primo/la prima (cousin), el hermanastro (stepbrother)."},{"h":"Personality","b":"Simpático (nice), gracioso (funny), hablador (chatty), perezoso (lazy), tímido (shy), egoísta (selfish), trabajador (hard-working), generoso (generous)."},{"h":"Past description","b":"Cuando era pequeño/a (when I was little) uses the imperfect: era tímido, tenía el pelo rubio, jugaba con mis primos. This shows a second tense to the examiner."}],"qs":[["'Me llevo bien con' means…",["I get on well with","I argue with","I live with","I look like"],"Llevarse bien."],["Which verb for 'I am tired'?",["Estoy cansado","Soy cansado","Tengo cansado","Hago cansado"],"Estar for temporary states."],["Feminine of 'alto'?",["alta","altos","altas","alte"],"Change -o to -a."],["'Mi hermana' means…",["My sister","My brother","My mother","My aunt"],"La hermana."],["'Es perezoso' means…",["He is lazy","He is funny","He is shy","He is tall"],"Perezoso = lazy."],["'Me peleo con mi padre' means…",["I argue with my dad","I play with my dad","I like my dad","I live with my dad"],"Pelearse = to argue."],["'Cuando era pequeño' means…",["When I was little","When I'm older","When I will be small","If I were little"],"Imperfect."],["'Los abuelos' are…",["Grandparents","Parents","Uncles","Cousins"],"Abuelo and abuela."],["'Gracioso' means…",["Funny","Grateful","Greedy","Grumpy"],"False friend alert: not 'gracious'."],["Which means 'because'?",["porque","pero","también","entonces"],"Ya que also works."]],"bonus":["Ser or estar for location: 'Madrid ___ en España'?",["está","es","son","hay"],"Estar for location."]},{"t":"Healthy living, education & work","intro":"Theme 1, Topics 2 and 3: health, school and future plans. Show off three tenses: present, past and future.","how":["Health: para llevar una vida sana hay que… (to lead a healthy life you have to…) + infinitive.","Negatives: no fumo, nunca bebo alcohol.","School: mi asignatura favorita es… porque…","Near future: voy a estudiar. Simple future: estudiaré.","Conditional: me gustaría ser médico (I would like to be a doctor)."],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'Next year I am going to study history.'","steps":["Next year = El año que viene / El año próximo","I am going to study = voy a estudiar","history = historia"],"a":"El año que viene voy a estudiar historia."},"walk":[{"h":"Health vocabulary","b":"Comer sano (eat healthily), hacer ejercicio, dormir ocho horas, evitar la comida basura (avoid junk food), el tabaco, las drogas, el estrés."},{"h":"School","b":"Las asignaturas: matemáticas, inglés, ciencias, informática, historia. Los deberes (homework), el uniforme, las normas (rules), los exámenes, la presión."},{"h":"Jobs and the future","b":"Me gustaría ser… abogado (lawyer), ingeniero (engineer), médico (doctor), profesor (teacher). Quiero ir a la universidad, hacer prácticas laborales (work experience), viajar por el mundo."}],"qs":[["'Hay que hacer ejercicio' means…",["You have to exercise","There is exercise","I hate exercise","I did exercise"],"Hay que + infinitive."],["'Nunca bebo alcohol' means…",["I never drink alcohol","I always drink","I drank alcohol","I will drink"],"Nunca = never."],["'Voy a estudiar' is which tense?",["Near future","Past","Imperfect","Conditional"],"Ir a + infinitive."],["'Me gustaría ser médico' means…",["I would like to be a doctor","I like doctors","I was a doctor","I am a doctor"],"Conditional."],["'Los deberes' are…",["Homework","Duties at home only","Teachers","Exams"],"Hacer los deberes."],["'La comida basura' is…",["Junk food","Healthy food","Breakfast","A basket of food"],"Basura = rubbish."],["'Estudiaré' is which tense?",["Simple future","Preterite","Present","Imperfect"],"Infinitive + -é."],["'Las normas' at school are…",["The rules","The marks","The lessons","The teachers"],"School rules."],["'Un abogado' is…",["A lawyer","An avocado","A doctor","A pilot"],"Abogado = lawyer."],["'Hacer prácticas laborales' means…",["To do work experience","To practise sport","To do homework","To go to work daily"],"Prácticas = placements."]],"bonus":["'El año pasado fui a la playa' — which tense is 'fui'?",["Preterite (past)","Future","Present","Conditional"],"Fui = I went (ir)."]},{"t":"Free time, festivals & celebrities","intro":"Theme 2: popular culture. Hobbies, Spanish and Latin American festivals, and celebrity culture with opinions.","how":["Jugar al for sports: juego al fútbol. Tocar for instruments: toco la guitarra.","Me gusta / me encanta + infinitive or noun: me encanta bailar.","Preterite: jugué (I played), vi (I saw), fui (I went).","Festivals: La Tomatina, San Fermín, el Día de los Muertos, la Semana Santa, los Reyes Magos.","Celebrities: son buenos/malos modelos a seguir porque…"],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'Last Saturday I watched a film with my friends.'","steps":["Last Saturday = El sábado pasado","I watched = vi","a film = una película","with my friends = con mis amigos"],"a":"El sábado pasado vi una película con mis amigos."},"walk":[{"h":"Preterite endings","b":"-ar verbs: -é, -aste, -ó (jugué, jugaste, jugó). -er/-ir verbs: -í, -iste, -ió (comí, comiste, comió). Key irregulars: fui (I went/was), hice (I did), tuve (I had), vi (I saw)."},{"h":"Festivals","b":"La Tomatina in Buñol is a giant tomato fight. San Fermín in Pamplona has the running of the bulls. El Día de los Muertos in Mexico honours dead relatives. On 6 January (los Reyes Magos) children get presents."},{"h":"Celebrities","b":"Los famosos tienen mucha influencia en los jóvenes. Algunos hacen trabajo voluntario. Otros dan un mal ejemplo. En mi opinión… Pienso que…"}],"qs":[["'Toco el piano' means…",["I play the piano","I touch the piano","I like the piano","I bought a piano"],"Tocar for instruments."],["'Juego al baloncesto' means…",["I play basketball","I play baseball","I watch basketball","I like volleyball"],"Baloncesto = basketball."],["Preterite 'yo' of jugar?",["jugué","jugó","jugaba","juego"],"Spelling change: g → gu."],["'Vi' means…",["I saw","I went","I lived","I came"],"From ver."],["La Tomatina involves…",["A tomato fight","Bull running","Fireworks","Remembering the dead"],"In Buñol, Valencia."],["Where does San Fermín take place?",["Pamplona","Madrid","Mexico City","Seville"],"Running of the bulls."],["El Día de los Muertos is from…",["Mexico","Spain","Argentina","Cuba"],"Honours relatives who have died."],["When do Spanish children traditionally get presents from los Reyes Magos?",["6 January","25 December","1 November","14 February"],"The Three Kings."],["'Me encanta bailar' means…",["I love dancing","I hate dancing","I can't dance","I danced"],"Encantar = to love."],["'Fui' can mean…",["I went or I was","I will go","I go","I would go"],"Ir and ser share the preterite."]],"bonus":["'Hice mis deberes' means…",["I did my homework","I will do my homework","I do my homework","I didn't do homework"],"Hice = I did (hacer)."]},{"t":"Travel, technology & the environment","intro":"Theme 3: holidays, social media and technology, and the environment where you live. Use past, present and future and give solutions.","how":["Holidays: fui a España, me alojé en un hotel, hacía sol.","Imperfect for description: hacía calor, era precioso.","Technology: uso mi móvil para… / las redes sociales.","Environment: la contaminación, el cambio climático, reciclar, la basura.","Solutions: deberíamos… (we should…) + infinitive: deberíamos reciclar más."],"example":{"q":"Translate: 'We should use public transport to protect the planet.'","steps":["We should use = Deberíamos usar","public transport = el transporte público","to protect the planet = para proteger el planeta"],"a":"Deberíamos usar el transporte público para proteger el planeta."},"walk":[{"h":"Holidays","b":"El verano pasado fui a Francia con mi familia. Viajamos en avión. Nos alojamos en un camping. Hacía mucho calor. Fue inolvidable (unforgettable). El año que viene me gustaría ir a México."},{"h":"Technology: pros and cons","b":"Ventajas: puedes comunicarte con amigos, es útil para buscar información. Desventajas: el ciberacoso (cyberbullying), es adictivo, las noticias falsas, pierdes tiempo."},{"h":"Where you live","b":"Vivo en una ciudad grande / un pueblo pequeño. En la ciudad hay mucho que hacer pero hay mucho tráfico y ruido. En el campo es tranquilo pero no hay nada que hacer."}],"qs":[["'Hacía sol' means…",["It was sunny","It is raining","It will be sunny","I made the sun"],"Imperfect weather."],["'Las redes sociales' are…",["Social media","Social workers","Fishing nets","Social events"],"Red = network."],["'Deberíamos reciclar' means…",["We should recycle","We recycled","We will recycle","We never recycle"],"Conditional of deber."],["'El cambio climático' is…",["Climate change","Exchange rate","Weather forecast","Hot climate"],"Cambio = change."],["'El ciberacoso' is…",["Cyberbullying","Cybercafé","Internet speed","Online shopping"],"Acoso = harassment."],["'Me alojé en un hotel' means…",["I stayed in a hotel","I worked in a hotel","I left the hotel","I like hotels"],"Alojarse = to stay."],["'Fue inolvidable' means…",["It was unforgettable","It was forgettable","It was boring","It was expensive"],"Inolvidable."],["'La contaminación' is…",["Pollution","Contamination of food only","Recycling","Rubbish bins"],"Air or water pollution."],["'Hay mucho tráfico' means…",["There's a lot of traffic","There's a lot of noise","There's nothing to do","There are many shops"],"Tráfico = traffic."],["'Mi móvil' is…",["My mobile phone","My car","My computer","My bag"],"Teléfono móvil."]],"bonus":["'Si tuviera dinero, viajaría' means…",["If I had money, I would travel","I had money and travelled","I will travel with money","I travel for money"],"Imperfect subjunctive + conditional."]}]},{"id":"pe","name":"Physical Education","weeks":[{"t":"Participation & commercialisation (2.1a, 2.1b)","intro":"Paper 2 starts with who takes part in sport and why, and how the media and sponsorship have commercialised sport.","how":["Know participation trends from sources like Sport England and DCMS.","Factors affecting participation (CRISPED): Cultural, Resources, Image, Socio-economic, People, Education, Discrimination.","Strategies to improve participation: promotion, provision and access.","The golden triangle links sport, sponsorship and the media.","Know positive and negative effects of media and sponsorship, with real examples."],"example":{"q":"Explain one way the media can have a positive effect on sport. (2 marks)","steps":["Point: TV coverage increases a sport's profile.","Development: more people watch, which can inspire them to take part, e.g. women's football after the Lionesses' Euro 2022 win."],"a":"Point plus a developed example."},"walk":[{"h":"CRISPED in practice","b":"Cultural: age, gender, ethnicity, religion, disability. Resources: facilities, climate, time. Image: media coverage of role models. Socio-economic: cost of kit and fees. People: family and friends. Education: PE at school. Discrimination: unfair treatment."},{"h":"Sponsorship","b":"Positives: money for clubs and athletes, cheaper tickets, better facilities. Negatives: sponsors may control kick-off times or kit, and unhealthy products (fast food, alcohol) can be linked to sport. Athletes can lose deals if they misbehave."},{"h":"Types of media","b":"Social media (direct contact with fans), internet streaming, TV and visual media, newspapers and magazines. More coverage means more money but can also mean intrusion into players' private lives."}],"qs":[["What does the S in CRISPED stand for?",["Socio-economic","Sport","Sponsorship","Skill"],"Cost and disposable income."],["What is the golden triangle?",["Sport, sponsorship and media","Coach, player and fan","Diet, sleep and training","FIFA, UEFA and the FA"],"They depend on each other."],["Which is a negative of sponsorship?",["Promoting unhealthy products","More money","Better facilities","Cheaper tickets"],"e.g. fast food brands."],["Which organisation publishes participation data?",["Sport England","NHS","BBC","UEFA"],"Active Lives survey."],["What is commercialisation?",["Managing sport to make a profit","Playing for fun","Coaching children","Building stadiums only"],"Sport as a business."],["Which is a positive effect of the media?",["Increases participation through role models","Changes match times","Invades privacy","Puts pressure on players"],"More exposure."],["The cost of kit fits which CRISPED factor?",["Socio-economic","Image","People","Education"],"Affordability."],["Which strategy improves participation?",["Free taster sessions","Higher prices","Fewer facilities","Less coverage"],"Promotion and access."],["How can sponsorship harm a performer?",["Pressure to perform or lose the deal","Free equipment","More money","Better coaching"],"Contracts can be cancelled."],["Which is social media?",["Instagram","A newspaper","Terrestrial TV","Radio"],"Direct contact with fans."]],"bonus":["Why might TV companies want matches at certain times?",["To get bigger audiences and more advertising money","To help players sleep","To avoid rain","Because referees prefer it"],"Commercial pressure."]},{"t":"Ethics & drugs in sport (2.1c)","intro":"This topic covers sportsmanship, gamesmanship, deviance and performance-enhancing drugs. Learn the drug types, their effects and their risks.","how":["Sportsmanship: playing fairly and respecting opponents, like kicking the ball out when someone's injured.","Gamesmanship: bending the rules without breaking them, like time-wasting.","Deviance: breaking rules or ethics, like doping, match-fixing or violence.","Anabolic steroids build muscle and aid recovery. Beta blockers steady nerves. Stimulants increase alertness.","Drug use can harm the performer's health and reputation, and harms sport's image."],"example":{"q":"Which sport would a performer use beta blockers in, and why?","steps":["Sport: archery or shooting.","Effect: beta blockers lower heart rate and reduce shaking.","Benefit: steadier aim and more accuracy.","Side effects: tiredness, low blood pressure."],"a":"Precision sports, to keep calm and steady."},"walk":[{"h":"Anabolic steroids","b":"These mimic testosterone. They increase muscle size and strength and speed up recovery. They're used by power athletes like sprinters and weightlifters. Side effects: liver damage, heart disease, aggression, acne."},{"h":"Stimulants","b":"Stimulants such as amphetamines increase alertness and reduce tiredness. Used in endurance or contact sports. Side effects: addiction, high blood pressure, insomnia, heart problems."},{"h":"Why performers take drugs","b":"Pressure to win, money and sponsorship, fame, recovering faster from injury, and the belief that others are doing it. Drug testing and bans try to stop this."}],"qs":[["Time-wasting to protect a lead is…",["Gamesmanship","Sportsmanship","Deviance","Etiquette"],"Bending the rules."],["Kicking the ball out for an injured player is…",["Sportsmanship","Gamesmanship","Deviance","Cheating"],"Fair play."],["What do anabolic steroids do?",["Increase muscle mass and strength","Calm nerves","Reduce weight","Improve eyesight"],"Like testosterone."],["Beta blockers are most useful in…",["Archery","Rugby","Sprinting","Weightlifting"],"They reduce shaking."],["Side effect of steroids?",["Liver damage","Better health","Lower aggression","Improved sleep"],"Also heart disease."],["What do stimulants do?",["Increase alertness","Reduce heart rate","Build muscle","Help you sleep"],"They speed up the nervous system."],["Match-fixing is an example of…",["Deviance","Sportsmanship","Gamesmanship","Etiquette"],"It breaks rules and laws."],["Why might a performer take drugs?",["Pressure to win","To lose","To get banned","Because it's allowed"],"Plus money and fame."],["Effect of drugs on sport itself?",["Damages its reputation","Makes it fairer","No effect","More fans trust it"],"Fans lose trust."],["Side effect of beta blockers?",["Tiredness / low blood pressure","Huge muscles","Aggression","Acne"],"They slow the heart."]],"bonus":["Which athlete type is most likely to misuse stimulants?",["Endurance or contact athletes needing alertness","Archers","Snooker players","Chess players"],"To delay fatigue."]},{"t":"Sports psychology (2.2)","intro":"Sports psychology covers skilful movement, classifying skills, goal setting, mental preparation, guidance and feedback. Always use practical examples.","how":["Skilful movement is efficient, pre-determined, coordinated, fluent and aesthetic.","Classify skills on continua: simple to complex (difficulty) and open to closed (environment).","SMART goals: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Recorded, Timed.","Mental preparation: imagery, mental rehearsal, selective attention, positive thinking.","Guidance: visual, verbal, manual, mechanical. Feedback: intrinsic, extrinsic, knowledge of performance, knowledge of results."],"example":{"q":"Classify a tennis serve on the open-closed continuum and justify it.","steps":["Placement: towards the closed end.","Justification: the server controls when to start.","The environment is stable and not affected by others.","It's not fully closed, because wind can affect the ball."],"a":"Closed, with justification."},"walk":[{"h":"Open vs closed","b":"Open skills are affected by the environment and other players, like a pass in football. Closed skills are not affected, and the performer controls the timing, like a free throw or a gymnastics vault."},{"h":"Goal setting","b":"SMART goals improve motivation and adherence. Example: 'Reduce my 5 km time from 25 to 24 minutes in 8 weeks, recording each run in my training diary.'"},{"h":"Guidance and feedback","b":"Beginners need visual guidance (demonstrations), manual guidance (physical support) and extrinsic, positive feedback. Experts use verbal guidance and intrinsic feedback (feeling the movement) more."}],"qs":[["Which is a closed skill?",["A penalty in rugby (place kick)","A pass in football","Dribbling in hockey","Tackling in rugby"],"Stable environment, self-paced."],["What does the M in SMART stand for?",["Measurable","Motivating","Mental","Maximum"],"You can track it."],["Which guidance uses a demonstration?",["Visual","Verbal","Manual","Mechanical"],"Seeing the skill."],["Using a float in swimming is…",["Mechanical guidance","Manual guidance","Verbal guidance","Visual guidance"],"Equipment helps."],["Feeling the movement yourself is…",["Intrinsic feedback","Extrinsic feedback","Knowledge of results","Visual guidance"],"From inside the performer."],["'You won the race' is…",["Knowledge of results","Knowledge of performance","Intrinsic feedback","Manual guidance"],"About the outcome."],["Picturing a successful performance is…",["Imagery","Selective attention","Positive thinking","Feedback"],"Mental preparation."],["Selective attention means…",["Focusing on important cues and ignoring distractions","Choosing the team","Picking your position","Choosing a coach"],"e.g. ignoring the crowd."],["A complex skill needs…",["Lots of decisions and attention","No thought","No practice","Only strength"],"e.g. a tumble turn."],["Which feedback suits beginners most?",["Positive, extrinsic feedback","Only negative feedback","No feedback","Intrinsic only"],"Builds confidence."]],"bonus":["Which characteristic of skill means it 'looks good'?",["Aesthetic","Efficient","Fluent","Pre-determined"],"Pleasing to watch."]},{"t":"Health, fitness, well-being & diet (2.3)","intro":"The last Paper 2 topic links physical activity to physical, emotional and social health, plus diet and hydration.","how":["Health is a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, not just the absence of illness.","Fitness is the ability to meet the demands of the environment.","Physical benefits: lower risk of coronary heart disease, healthy weight, stronger bones, better posture.","Emotional and social benefits: confidence, stress relief, friendship, belonging.","Balanced diet: carbohydrates, protein, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre, water."],"example":{"q":"Explain one consequence of a sedentary lifestyle. (2 marks)","steps":["Point: increased risk of obesity.","Development: fewer calories are used than eaten, so weight is gained, increasing the risk of type 2 diabetes and CHD."],"a":"Point plus a developed consequence."},"walk":[{"h":"Different age groups","b":"Teenagers benefit from friendship and confidence. Adults benefit from stress relief and preventing weight gain. Older people benefit from keeping bone density, balance and independence."},{"h":"Nutrients for sport","b":"Carbohydrates are the main energy source. Protein is for growth and repair. Fats provide energy for low-intensity activity. Vitamins and minerals support body functions. Fibre aids digestion."},{"h":"Hydration","b":"Dehydration thickens the blood, raises heart rate and body temperature, slows reactions, and causes cramp. Drink before, during and after exercise, especially in heat."}],"qs":[["What is health?",["Complete physical, mental and social well-being","Not being ill","Being fit","Having muscles"],"The WHO definition."],["What is the main energy source in sport?",["Carbohydrates","Protein","Fibre","Vitamins"],"Stored as glycogen."],["What is protein mainly for?",["Growth and repair","Quick energy","Digestion","Hydration"],"Muscle repair."],["Which is a social benefit of exercise?",["Making friends","Lower blood pressure","Stronger bones","Better posture"],"Belonging to a group."],["What does sedentary mean?",["Little or no physical activity","Very active","Healthy eating","Sleeping well"],"Sitting a lot."],["Which is an emotional benefit?",["Stress relief","Better posture","Stronger heart","More friends"],"Releases endorphins."],["Effect of dehydration?",["Slower reaction time","Faster sprinting","Lower heart rate","Better focus"],"Also higher body temperature."],["What does fibre do?",["Aids digestion","Builds muscle","Gives quick energy","Stores oxygen"],"Prevents constipation."],["CHD stands for…",["Coronary heart disease","Chronic health disorder","Cardio heart damage","Cholesterol high diet"],"Linked to inactivity."],["A benefit for older people?",["Maintaining bone density","Winning trophies","Sponsorship","Gaining weight"],"Reduces osteoporosis risk."]],"bonus":["Fitness is the ability to…",["Meet the demands of the environment","Lift heavy weights","Run a marathon","Never get ill"],"Fitness can differ from health."]}]},{"id":"sociology","name":"Sociology","weeks":[{"t":"The sociological approach","intro":"Topic 3.1 covers key theories and thinkers. Every essay needs you to compare perspectives: functionalism, Marxism, feminism and interactionism.","how":["Functionalism (Durkheim): society is like a body, and each part has a function that keeps it stable. Consensus theory.","Marxism (Marx): society is based on conflict between the ruling class (bourgeoisie) and the working class (proletariat).","Feminism: society is patriarchal (male-dominated) and women are disadvantaged.","Interactionism: focuses on small-scale interactions and labelling.","Weber: class, status and power all affect life chances."],"example":{"q":"Describe one difference between functionalism and Marxism. (3 marks)","steps":["Functionalists see society as based on consensus and shared values.","Marxists see society as based on conflict between classes.","So functionalists think institutions benefit everyone, Marxists think they benefit the ruling class."],"a":"Consensus vs conflict, explained."},"walk":[{"h":"Key concepts","b":"Socialisation: learning norms and values. Primary socialisation happens in the family, secondary at school and through the media. Norms are expected behaviour. Values are beliefs about what's important. Roles are expected behaviour linked to a position."},{"h":"Consensus vs conflict","b":"Consensus theories (functionalism) stress agreement and stability. Conflict theories (Marxism, feminism) stress inequality and struggle. Interactionists look at how meaning is created in everyday interactions."},{"h":"Using thinkers","b":"Durkheim: social solidarity. Marx: class conflict and capitalism. Weber: class, status and party, and social action. Name them in essays and compare their views."}],"qs":[["Which theory sees society as based on consensus?",["Functionalism","Marxism","Feminism","Interactionism"],"Shared norms and values."],["Who are the bourgeoisie?",["The ruling class who own the means of production","The working class","Teachers","The middle class"],"Marx's term."],["What is patriarchy?",["A male-dominated society","A family of fathers","Rule by priests","Equal society"],"A key feminist concept."],["Primary socialisation happens mainly in…",["The family","School","The workplace","The media"],"Early childhood."],["Which thinker is linked to functionalism?",["Durkheim","Marx","Oakley","Becker"],"Social solidarity."],["What are norms?",["Expected patterns of behaviour","Laws only","Beliefs about God","Statistics"],"e.g. queuing."],["Interactionists focus on…",["Small-scale interactions and labelling","The whole economy","Biology","Government only"],"Micro approach."],["Weber said life chances are affected by class, status and…",["Power (party)","Religion only","Age only","Luck"],"Three dimensions."],["Who are the proletariat?",["The working class","The ruling class","Landowners","Politicians"],"They sell their labour."],["Marxism is a…",["Conflict theory","Consensus theory","Biological theory","Religious theory"],"Class conflict."]],"bonus":["What is secondary socialisation?",["Learning norms from school, peers, media and work","Learning from parents as a baby","A type of crime","A research method"],"Later in life."]},{"t":"Research methods (3.7)","intro":"Research methods appear across every topic. Learn the methods, sampling, types of data and ethical and practical issues.","how":["Research process: aim → hypothesis → pilot study → sampling → data collection → analysis.","Quantitative data is numbers (questionnaires, official statistics). Qualitative data is words and meanings (interviews, observation).","Sampling: random, systematic, stratified, quota, snowball.","Evaluate methods using reliability, validity, representativeness and generalisability.","Ethics: informed consent, confidentiality, anonymity, no harm, right to withdraw."],"example":{"q":"Identify one advantage of using unstructured interviews. (2 marks)","steps":["Advantage: high validity.","Development: respondents can explain their views in depth, so the researcher gets a true picture of their experiences."],"a":"Point and development."},"walk":[{"h":"Questionnaires","b":"Closed questions give quantitative, reliable data that's easy to compare. Open questions give qualitative data. Questionnaires are cheap and reach large samples, but response rates can be low and people may lie."},{"h":"Observation","b":"Participant observation means joining the group, which gives rich, valid data but risks the researcher 'going native'. Non-participant observation means watching from outside. Covert means secret (ethical issues). Overt means the group knows, which can change their behaviour (the Hawthorne effect)."},{"h":"Pilot studies and mixed methods","b":"A pilot study is a small trial run to check questions work. Mixed methods combine quantitative and qualitative data (triangulation) to gain both reliability and validity."}],"qs":[["Quantitative data is…",["Numerical","Words and meanings","Always valid","Only from interviews"],"Numbers and statistics."],["What is a pilot study?",["A small trial run of the research","The final report","A sampling frame","A type of observation"],"Finds problems early."],["What does reliability mean?",["Research can be repeated with the same results","A true picture of reality","Representative sample","Ethical approval"],"Consistency."],["What does validity mean?",["A true picture of what's being studied","Repeatable results","Large sample","Cheap research"],"Accuracy."],["Covert observation raises issues of…",["Informed consent","Cost","Speed","Reliability only"],"People don't know they're studied."],["Which sampling divides the population into groups first?",["Stratified","Random","Snowball","Opportunity"],"Reflects the population's makeup."],["Snowball sampling is useful for…",["Hard-to-reach groups","National surveys","Schools","Census data"],"Participants recruit others."],["Official statistics are…",["Secondary quantitative data","Primary qualitative data","Interviews","Observations"],"Collected by government."],["What is the Hawthorne effect?",["People change behaviour because they know they're being studied","A type of sample","Lying in questionnaires","Low response rate"],"Affects validity."],["Using several methods together is called…",["Mixed methods / triangulation","Pilot study","Sampling frame","Stratification"],"Combines strengths."]],"bonus":["What is a sampling frame?",["A list of the population from which a sample is drawn","A picture frame","A hypothesis","An interview schedule"],"e.g. the electoral register."]},{"t":"Crime & deviance (3.5)","intro":"This topic covers why crime happens, social control, patterns of crime and data on crime. Compare theories and name the key sociologists.","how":["Crime breaks the law. Deviance breaks social norms. Both are socially constructed.","Merton (strain theory): crime happens when people can't achieve cultural goals by legitimate means.","Cohen (subcultures): working-class boys form delinquent subcultures because of status frustration.","Becker (labelling): deviance is behaviour that people label as such, which can lead to a self-fulfilling prophecy.","Data: official statistics, victim surveys (Crime Survey for England and Wales), self-report studies. The dark figure is unrecorded crime."],"example":{"q":"Explain the 'dark figure' of crime.","steps":["The dark figure is crime that is unreported or unrecorded.","Victims may not report crimes they see as trivial or embarrassing.","Police may decide not to record some crimes.","So official statistics underestimate the true amount of crime."],"a":"Unreported and unrecorded crime makes official statistics incomplete."},"walk":[{"h":"Social control","b":"Formal control comes from the police, courts and prisons using written laws. Informal control comes from family, peers and the community using unwritten rules and sanctions like disapproval. Heidensohn argued women conform more because they face stronger control at home, school and in public."},{"h":"Patterns","b":"Official statistics suggest crime is more common among young, working-class males. Explanations include more opportunity, subcultures, poverty, and police bias in who gets stopped and arrested. Carlen found women's crime is linked to poverty and broken 'deals' with society."},{"h":"Public debates","b":"Debates include violent crime, sentencing, young offenders, the prison system and media coverage. The media can cause moral panics, exaggerating a problem and creating 'folk devils'."}],"qs":[["Deviance means…",["Breaking social norms","Only breaking laws","Being a victim","Following rules"],"Not always illegal."],["Who developed strain theory?",["Merton","Becker","Cohen","Marx"],"Goals vs means."],["Labelling theory is linked to…",["Becker","Durkheim","Merton","Weber"],"Interactionism."],["What is the dark figure of crime?",["Crime that is unreported or unrecorded","Crime at night","Violent crime","Crime by gangs"],"Missing from statistics."],["Which is formal social control?",["The police","Parents' disapproval","Peer pressure","Gossip"],"Official agencies."],["Cohen said delinquent subcultures arise from…",["Status frustration","Too much money","Strict parents","Genetics"],"Working-class boys failing at school."],["Who studied female conformity?",["Heidensohn","Merton","Becker","Durkheim"],"Women face more control."],["A victim survey in England and Wales is the…",["Crime Survey for England and Wales","Census","Police report","Self-report study"],"Asks people about crimes against them."],["What is a self-fulfilling prophecy?",["A label causes the person to act as predicted","A crime statistic","A prison sentence","A survey"],"Labelling leads to more deviance."],["A moral panic is…",["Exaggerated public concern stirred by the media","A real crime wave","A police strategy","A court case"],"Creates 'folk devils'."]],"bonus":["Carlen linked women's crime to…",["Poverty and the class and gender 'deals' breaking down","Biology","Too much freedom","Football"],"Women, crime and poverty."]},{"t":"Social stratification (3.6)","intro":"Stratification is how society is divided into layers of inequality. Learn the theories of Davis and Moore, Marx and Weber, and factors affecting life chances.","how":["Stratification: a system of layers based on inequality of wealth, income, status and power.","Davis and Moore (functionalist): inequality is necessary to make sure the most able people fill the most important jobs.","Marx: class is based on ownership of the means of production. Capitalism exploits workers.","Weber: class, status and party (power) are separate dimensions.","Life chances are affected by class, gender, ethnicity, age, disability and more."],"example":{"q":"Evaluate Davis and Moore's theory of stratification.","steps":["Claim: high rewards motivate talented people to train for important roles.","Criticism (Marxist): rewards reflect power, not talent; the rich inherit wealth.","Criticism (feminist): women's work, like caring, is undervalued.","Many important jobs, like nurses, are poorly paid."],"a":"Explain the theory, then criticise from other perspectives."},"walk":[{"h":"Socio-economic class","b":"Class divisions are often measured by occupation. Marx saw two main classes. Weber added a middle class based on market position and skills. Devine's research into 'affluent workers' questioned whether the working class was becoming middle class."},{"h":"Life chances","b":"Life chances are the opportunities to have a good life: health, education, income, housing. Working-class people on average have lower life expectancy and educational attainment. Gender pay gaps and discrimination affect women and ethnic minorities."},{"h":"Poverty","b":"Absolute poverty means lacking basics for survival. Relative poverty means being poor compared with the society's average. Explanations include structural causes (unemployment, low pay) and cultural ones (a culture of poverty), which are debated."}],"qs":[["What is social stratification?",["Layers of inequality in society","Equal society","A research method","A type of crime"],"Like layers of rock."],["Davis and Moore are…",["Functionalists","Marxists","Feminists","Interactionists"],"Role allocation."],["Marx based class on…",["Ownership of the means of production","Personality","Religion","Age"],"Bourgeoisie vs proletariat."],["Weber's three dimensions are class, status and…",["Party (power)","Gender","Age","Money"],"Multi-dimensional."],["What are life chances?",["Opportunities to achieve a good quality of life","Lottery odds","Job titles","Crime rates"],"Health, education, income."],["Relative poverty is…",["Being poor compared with the average in society","Having no food at all","Being rich","Living abroad"],"Compared with others."],["Who studied 'the affluent worker' idea?",["Devine","Durkheim","Merton","Oakley"],"Questioned embourgeoisement."],["A Marxist criticism of Davis and Moore?",["Rewards reflect power and inheritance, not talent","Inequality is natural","Everyone is equal","Jobs don't matter"],"The rich pass on advantage."],["Which factor can affect life chances?",["Ethnicity","Shoe size","Favourite colour","Star sign"],"Plus class, gender, age, disability."],["Absolute poverty means…",["Lacking the basics needed to survive","Earning below average","Having no car","Not owning a home"],"Food, water, shelter."]],"bonus":["What is social mobility?",["Moving between social classes","Moving house","Travelling abroad","Changing jobs at the same level"],"Up or down the class structure."]}]}];
